# Chapter 5 — The Evaluator

The courtesy copy reached the corner office on the third floor of the Compact's western administration in the ordinary morning file, between a stations-office staffing note and a certification dispute from the coastal district, and Vastin knew what it was before he opened it, because he had been expecting it since a chamber with no windows had entered a question for formal proceedings and set his testimony aside with courtesy.

He had not expected it as a courtesy copy.

That was the first thing he noted, and he noted it the way he noted everything, without decoration: for three years, every document touching the enrollee's matter had crossed his desk as a *query* — an advisory requested, an evaluation invited, an assessment sought. This one crossed it as information. *For the evaluator's file, per standing distribution.* Not asked. Told. The instrument that had grown from his own advisory — he found the paragraph on the second page and read his own sentence back in the petitioner's type, *adopted*, approvingly, the way a man finds his signature on a document he did not sign — had been filed at Ostrand a fortnight ago, and the Compact's senior evaluator, the only officer of the Compact who had personally assessed the evaluand, was being informed of it in the same envelope as a staffing note.

The corner office on the third floor had been his for fourteen years and had, in that time, acquired exactly four things: a desk, a map cabinet with the western district's station network on its top sheet, a locked press behind the cabinet, and a chair for a visitor that was neither comfortable nor uncomfortable, having been chosen for a room where visits were expected to be brief and useful. There were no plaques on the walls. He had been offered plaques. The morning file came up at the half-bell in a leather wallet and went back down at the fourth, and in fourteen years it had never once been late, because the outer office understood that lateness in this room was a finding about the outer office.

He worked the file the way he had worked every file since he was a district evaluator with a bad coat: left-hand stack, things requiring judgment; right-hand stack, things requiring only a signature; and, in the middle, whatever had arrived that did not yet know which stack it belonged in. He put the courtesy copy in the middle and did the staffing note and the coastal certification first, because a man who read the interesting item first was a man whose judgment on the dull ones could be predicted, and predictable judgment was the beginning of bad judgment.

Then he read the whole instrument once, at his own pace, with the office quiet enough to hear paper.

It took him forty minutes to reach the finding a researcher at Halcenvane had taken a night to reach, and he reached it by the same road: the conceded execution, the conceded text, the integrity provision pointed at a person, the re-evaluation remedy, the protocol with no bin, chapter fourteen. The corridor. He had spent thirty years inside instruments and he recognized the architecture of this one the way a mason recognizes a wall built by a colleague — every course true, every joint tight, and the whole thing standing on a foundation the mason had been asked not to inspect. He did not need chapter fourteen read to him. He had written evaluations that triggered it. Twice. Both findings correct on the evidence, both protocols run to the letter, both files closed inside the schedule. What the sequence did afterward was entered under a different heading, in an office he had never worked in.

*Compelled re-evaluation under standard Arbiter protocol.* He read the phrase a third time. He had sat in a records hall a year and a half ago and asked a boy eleven questions and declined to ask the twelfth, and had written afterward, in his own hand, the only finding the evidence supported: *Classification error is unlikely. Continue observation.* The instruments could not read the evaluand. He had said so. Now the petition proposed to stand the evaluand in front of the instruments again, formally, on the record, and to treat the instruments' silence as the evaluand's failure — and the proposal cited, as its authority for writing new rules, the evaluator who had said the old ones were being misread.

He got the enrollee's file out of the left-hand stack before he did anything else, because a finding was not a memory and he did not permit himself to work from memory about a subject.

It was thicker than it had been at the inspection and most of what had thickened it was other people's paper. His own portion was nine leaves. The observation schedule. The floor notes, in his hand, from a records hall on a bluff: the evasion frames run at three tempos, the surface read, the declared and the undeclared, the three-hour session and the two-hour, the boy's answers set down verbatim because an evaluator who paraphrased a subject was writing about himself. Eleven questions asked. He had numbered them. He read them now in order, which took four minutes, and found — as he had found each of the six times he had read them since — that they were good questions, correctly ordered, and that the twelfth, which was written on no leaf anywhere and had been declined in the room, was the only one that would have been of any use to a proceeding.

He had declined it because asking it would have been an interrogation and not an evaluation, and because an evaluator who asks a subject to explain what the instrument cannot read has stopped trusting his instrument and started trusting his suspicion. He had been right to decline it. He was still right. It occurred to him, holding the nine leaves, that being right had now cost the file everything it was worth, since a finding built on honest questions could be set aside in two sentences and a finding built on the twelfth could not have been written at all.

He put the leaves back in order and squared them and returned the file to the stack.

He set the instrument down and looked at the window for the length of time he permitted himself for such things, which was not long — long enough to see the river, and the stations-office roof across it, where four Arbiter frames he had personally certified sat in their housings reading practitioners every working day, and to remember, without wanting to, the two evaluations of his career that had triggered chapter fourteen. A guild-house officer who had falsified his own sponsorship records for a decade: finding entered, standing nullified, the sequence run out in nine days. A district assessor who had taken money to alter a Kindling entry: the same finding, the same nine days — and, four months after the file closed, one letter from the man's daughter, asking whether her father's name would ever come off the restricted list. He had answered the letter. He had told her the truth, which was no. He had not thought about the letter in six years and he thought about it now for the length of the window, and then he stopped, because the window's time was up.

Then he did what he did, because doing was his language. He drew a sheet of the office's own stock and drafted a petition for standing: the Compact's senior-most evaluator, the only official of the Compact who had personally assessed the enrollee under any protocol, requesting leave to submit an evaluator's finding to the proceedings, under his own name and seal, for the evaluation seat's consideration. He drafted it in the register the registry used for such things — plain, exact, citing the provisions that permitted supplementary findings in institutional proceedings, of which there were two, both old — the older of them pre-standardization text, drafted in the era when evaluators' findings were the proceedings' whole substance rather than their ornament, surviving in the modern code the way old clauses survived, unrepealed, unfashionable, and waiting; he read both twice and cited both, because a petition that stood on one leg deserved to be refused — and he read it back, and it was textually unimpeachable, and he filed it by the noon dispatch.

His clerk — nine years in the outer office; a man who had learned to enter the Archmarshal's directions without comment and to spend the remainder of each morning not asking about them — entered the petition into the dispatch ledger, and paused with the pen above the routing column, and Vastin, who missed nothing that happened within forty feet of his desk, said, "Registry seat, Ostrand. Proceedings. Institutional," and the clerk wrote it, and did not say that in nine years he had never routed a petition from that desk *to* a proceeding rather than from one. He did not need to say it. Vastin had noted the pause, and filed it, and the filing read: *even the outer office knows which direction this desk sends paper. Today it sent it the other way.*

He had submitted supplementary findings four times in thirty years and he set the four out on the desk from memory, in order, because a man predicting an outcome should first assemble his sample. A chartered institution's sponsorship dispute, sixteen years ago: standing granted in six days, finding received, finding decisive. A stations-office proceeding on a frame's calibration: granted in nine, received, and the frame retired on his page alone. A guild-house evaluation matter: granted in four. And a district's enrollment challenge, twelve years back, the nearest of the four to this one, in which he had submitted a finding against the district's own petition and the district had received it, entered it, and lost on it, and had written to him afterward to say so without complaint, because that was how the thing had worked for three hundred years.

Four for four. It was not a large sample. It was every instance there was.

He set an expectation, because he always set an expectation. Standing granted within the week, as a formality; a request from the evaluator of record for the file, to be scheduled; his finding entered at the second session. He had submitted supplementary findings to four institutional proceedings in thirty years and every one had been received with the courtesy the grade commanded.

The four days ran on the office's ordinary clock. The certification dispute from the coastal district was resolved by a two-line advisory. The stations-office staffing note was annotated and returned. On the third day a junior evaluator from the northern district came up to the corner office with a difficult file — a Kindling record that did not match its practitioner's demonstrated architecture by a margin the junior found troubling — and Vastin reviewed it with him for an hour, patiently, the way he had reviewed such files for thirty years: the architecture was within tolerance; the record was sound; the margin was the ordinary variance of a young practitioner developing faster than his evaluators expected; continue observation. The junior thanked him and went down, reassured. Vastin sat afterward with the file's copy for longer than the file warranted, and could not have said why, and entered the not-saying in no ledger, because it was not yet a thing with a shape.

On the fourth morning the outer office's step on the stair was wrong.

He knew the step. Nine years of it: two treads at a time to the landing, then single, then the pause at the door that was the clerk deciding whether the Archmarshal was reading. This one came up single from the bottom and did not pause at all, which meant the man had decided the thing in his hand outranked the reading, and Vastin had time, in the four seconds between the stair and the door, to know what it was and to compose his face, and did the second and resented, briefly and privately, having needed to.

"From the registry seat, sir. By seat courier, not the post."

"Thank you. Leave the door."

The denial arrived in four days.

---

He read the two old provisions again on the fourth morning, before the file came, the way a man rechecks a sum he has already proved: the pre-standardization text first, in the compilation's own type, three sentences that had once been the whole substance of how a proceeding learned what an evaluator knew. *The evaluator of record, or any evaluator of senior grade having personally assessed the subject, may submit findings to the proceedings, and the proceedings shall receive them.* Shall receive. The old text's favorite word, and his. He had cited it correctly. It had been invoked a bare handful of times in three hundred years and honored on every one of them, because a proceeding that declined to receive an evaluator's findings was a proceeding that had decided evaluation was not its business, and no proceeding in three hundred years had been willing to say so in writing.

It came in the morning file, in the registry's flawless prose, on registry-seat stock with the seal impressed rather than inked — which was itself a grade of reply, and he catalogued the grade before he read the words. Two sentences.

*The proceedings' evaluation seat is fully constituted. Supplementary findings are not contemplated by the schedule.*

He read it standing, which he did not do, and then he sat down, which he did not need to, and the office was very quiet, and he held the sheet at the reading distance his eyes now required and read it a second time, and a third, and the third reading was the one that mattered, because on the third reading he stopped reading it as a reply and started reading it as a *sentence*.

*Not contemplated by the schedule.*

He had written that sentence. Not that one — its sisters. A hundred of them, across thirty years, in the correct register, to the correct offices, declining unhelpful input: a field assessor's supplementary opinion not contemplated by the review's terms; a station's petition to reopen a closed finding not contemplated by the schedule; a junior officer's escalation not contemplated by the reporting structure. He had written them as a craftsman writes — cleanly, without cruelty, in the exact language that closed a door while leaving the person on the other side of it nothing to grip. It was good technique. It was among the best he had. And he recognized it now, at the reading distance his eyes required, from the receiving end, with the specific vertigo of a man who has spent his life fitting instruments and has just been handed one of his own, calibrated, pointed at him.

The proceedings did not want the best evaluation available. He turned the sentence over, and it did not change. The proceedings had constituted an evaluation seat, and the seat was *full*, and the Compact's senior evaluator — the one officer who had put the instrument to the evaluand and written down what it returned — was not contemplated by the schedule, because the schedule had been written by people who already knew what the evaluation would find, and an evaluator who might find something else was not an asset to a schedule. He was an irregularity. The proceedings wanted the evaluation they had been built to produce. His grade, his thirty years, his personal assessment of the subject: not contemplated.

He noted, with the evaluator's habit of keeping the instrument honest even while it recorded its own reading, that the sentence had cost the sender nothing. Two lines. Flawless. Deniable at every altitude. Exactly as his own had been.

He put the denial in the enrollee's file, in the left-hand stack, the one he read first.

---

And then, because an evaluator evaluates, he began writing the finding anyway.

He began it that afternoon, after the day's other business, when the office was quiet enough to hear paper, on the office's own stock, in his own hand — not the office's; the hand he used for the things that were his — and he headed it the way he headed every finding of his career, with the subject, the protocol, and the date, and beneath them, in the line the form provided for the addressee, he wrote nothing. Unsolicited. Uncommissioned. Addressed to no one.

*Evaluator's finding. Subject: the enrollee of record, Halcenvane, unclassified. Basis: personal assessment under the demonstration provision's evaluation-linked observation, the inspection year; the continental tournament record entire, personally attended at three sessions and reviewed at all; the assay-provision panel minutes, four terms; the enrollee's public season record, two years.*

He wrote for three hours. He wrote it as he would have written it for the second session — completely, at his own speed, in the register of a man correcting a category error by demonstration — and it ran to nine pages, and its finding was the one the evidence had supported since a records-hall room: that the standard Arbiter protocol was not an instrument capable of evaluating the subject; that its silence was a limit of the instrument and not a finding about the evaluand; that compelled re-evaluation under that protocol would therefore return, with certainty, no valid classification, and that an evaluation whose result was certain before it was conducted was not an evaluation but a procedure wearing one; and that the evaluation seat, if it wished to evaluate rather than to proceed, had one honest instrument available to it, which was the demonstration record, and should use it.

The finding's ninth page carried its last paragraph, and he wrote it slower than the rest, because it was the paragraph an evaluator was not supposed to write and he wrote it anyway:

*The evaluator notes, for the file, that this finding was offered to the proceedings and declined as not contemplated by the schedule. The evaluator records no objection to the schedule. The evaluator records that a schedule which does not contemplate the only personal assessment of the subject in the Compact's possession has been drafted with a purpose the evaluator was not consulted on and cannot, from this desk, identify — and that an evaluation which begins from its conclusion is a report about the evaluator, and that the evaluator, in this instance, was not the one evaluating.*

The clerk came up at the fourth bell to take the day's file down and found the Archmarshal writing, which he had seen perhaps a dozen times in nine years, because the Archmarshal's writing was ordinarily done in the mornings and ordinarily on the office's forms.

"The file, sir."

"Take it. Nothing of mine in it tonight."

The clerk took it, and went to the door, and stopped there for the length of time a careful man permits himself, and Vastin, who missed nothing within forty feet of his desk, allowed the stop.

"Sir. The lamps go out at the sixth bell on this floor. The building's rule, not mine."

"Then bring me a candle from the outer office and enter it on the office's consumables, under my name."

"Yes, sir." A pause, and then the only sentence the man had spoken in nine years that was not about the work: "Is it a finding, sir?"

Vastin considered the question, which was the correct question, and found that he could answer it accurately.

"It is a finding. It has no addressee."

"Very good, sir," said the clerk, who had no idea what to do with that, and brought the candle, and entered it on the consumables under the Archmarshal's name, where it would sit for the next forty years as the only irregularity in fourteen years of that office's accounts.

He wrote past the sixth bell by the candle. The third page cost him the most, because it was the page on which he had to set down, in the register a finding required, that the instruments were not at fault: that the Arbiter protocol was performing exactly as designed, that its silence in this instance was not a malfunction of any frame he had ever certified, and that a limit correctly reported by an instrument is the most honest output an instrument can produce. He had spent thirty years defending that sentence to people who wanted the frames blamed. He wrote it now in defense of a boy, and noted, in the margin of his own mind and nowhere on the leaf, that it was the same sentence.

He read it back once. It was the best finding he had written in a decade. It was addressed to no one, and there was no one to address it to, and he knew both things with the exactness that had made him what he was.

He filed it in his own records — not the office's; the locked press behind the map cabinet where he kept the things that were his — under a designation he had never used in thirty years, because he had never needed one, because every finding he had ever written had somewhere to go. He wrote the designation on the folder's tab in his own hand, and looked at it, and left it.

*Pending a proceeding worth sending it to.*

---

Word reached Halcenvane through Withrow's counsel eight days later, in the way word reached anyone from that altitude: sideways, secondhand, as a thing the counsel's district contacts had heard the registry seat's clerks discussing at the long inn by the wharfs. She brought it up the bluff road in person because it was not a thing to put in a note, and delivered it at the residence table in the voice she used for findings that changed the weather. The Archmarshal had petitioned for standing to submit a finding to the proceedings. The petition had been refused. Two sentences. Not contemplated by the schedule.

Withrow received it first, in her office, from the counsel, and her response was entered in the log that night because Cael was present for it: she listened to the two sentences, and asked to hear them a second time, and then said, in the registry-official's register, "Not contemplated by the schedule. I have written that sentence. Every registry officer of my grade has written that sentence." She looked at the charter framed above the file cases. "I used to think it was the most honest thing the registry did — declining input it didn't need, in plain words, without pretending to have considered it. I'm now going to have to think about what it means that I taught it to junior officers as a kindness." To Cael: "Go and tell the table. And tell them I said the word again. *Choosing.*"

Karis had the two provisions on the annex table by nightfall — the ones the Archmarshal must have cited, because there were only two, both old, one of them pre-standardization stratum — and she read them aloud to the circle before the counsel's news was discussed, because she wanted the table to know exactly how sound the refused petition had been. "He cited the right law. Both provisions. I counted them tonight. Eleven. All granted." She set the volume down. "They refused it in two sentences without citing a provision at all. That's not a ruling. That's a schedule declining to have a ruling in it."

The counsel gave the table one more thing before she went back down the road, and she gave it standing, with her gloves already on, which was how she delivered the items she had decided were not advice.

"I want to be precise about what I have and have not told you. I have not told you that the Archmarshal is on your side. He is not. He is an officer of the Compact who petitioned an office of the Compact under the Compact's own provisions and was refused by it, and every one of those words is the same institution." She drew the glove tight. "What I have told you is that the refusal was two sentences with no provision cited. In twenty-six years I have been refused perhaps two hundred times and I have been refused *without a citation* four times, and all four were offices that had already decided and did not wish to write down the ground they had decided on." She took up the case. "Write the citation down when you get one. Its absence is a finding."

Cael had done one piece of arithmetic while the counsel was still speaking, because the route was the only part of the news he could check himself: the long inn by the wharfs was where the delegation's coach settled its small accounts on an Ostrand run, and the delegation's small accounts were settled by one man, at a counter, for the quarter-hour it took, in a room of registry-seat clerks who would have taken him for furniture. He did not enter it as a finding. He entered it in the notebook under the heading the man's whole file lived under: *the office was in the room first.*

The inner circle sat with it at the residence table, and nobody spoke for a while, because there was nothing to say to it that the sentence had not already said.

Seln said it anyway, from the table's end, in the dry administrative register — the professional reading the tea leaves for people who had not been trained to, and had learned, by now, to weigh him at full freight.

"They benched their best instrument because he measures true." He turned his cup a quarter-turn. "Whatever this is, it stopped being an evaluation before it started."

Nobody answered. Lira looked at the table. Brom looked at Seln, for a moment longer than usual, the way he looked at things he was pricing, and Cael charted the look and got the reading on the stair afterward, from Brom himself, unprompted, which happened perhaps four times a year: "He said *their best instrument*. He was talking about the Archmarshal." Brom stopped at the stair's turn. "He was also talking about himself. Fifteen years the best instrument they had. Benched by his own reports." He went up. Karis wrote it down.

The log, that night, at the window, with the counsel's note of the refusal on the sill beside the docket dates:

*Vastin warned me the channels would change. He didn't say they'd change on him too.*

*He's the Compact's best evaluator. He put the instrument to me himself, in a room, for a day, and wrote down what it returned. He asked to tell the proceedings what he found and the proceedings told him, in his own grammar, that he wasn't on the schedule. Note the shape: they didn't dispute his finding. They declined to contemplate it. That's not an argument lost. That's an instrument put back in the case unread.*

*Whatever is deciding what I am, it has now declined the only two credentialed opinions the system ever produced about me — his at the convening, his at the proceedings. Somebody decided, before the first session sat, that the best evaluation available was not an asset. I've spent four years learning to read the people the machine sends. I am beginning to understand that the machine has learned to send people I can't read, and to bench the ones I can.*
# Chapter 6 — Public Record

The proceedings were public record, and the enrollee was the most-reviewed match result in tournament history, and the two facts met in the term's third month the way weather fronts meet, with the collision audible from every direction at once.

Cael charted it because it was the largest apparatus of attention ever pointed at him and cataloguing was how he kept his footing in weather. The broadsides had the challenge within a week of its service — Withrow's one-line answer had guaranteed that; a chancellor who wrote her own document expected it read — and they built the story out of the parts they had, which were the Norhold interpretations with a registry seal added: the concealed-classification theory, now *confirmed* (the challenge proved the registry suspected it); the fraud theory, now *vindicated* (why else compel re-evaluation); the suppressed-Path theory, now the darkest and most popular (the Compact was covering its own error). A dozen versions, printed in four cities, every one of them still wrong, and every one of them now carrying, in its final paragraph, the phrase *evaluation-integrity question* as though the phrase meant something its authors understood.

The economy of it was the part he could actually chart, so he charted the economy.

Karis's assistant, sent down to Ostrand on an archive errand in the month's first week, came back with the whole market on four sheets and a receipt, because Karis had told her to buy one of everything and account for it, and because the girl had understood that *account for it* meant something more than the sum. Cael read her sheet at the annex table with real admiration. Six titles in Ostrand alone. The assay transcript, still on sale at six marks, two years old, in its fourth printing. A new pamphlet at three marks — *The Halcenvane Question, With The Instrument Entire* — which reproduced the challenge's first page accurately and the rest of it from a clerk's recollection, wrongly. The woodcut at a copper and a half, unchanged, still showing a boy who looked nothing like him in a stance no practitioner had ever adopted. And two competing broadside series at a half-copper the sheet, issued weekly, whose entire business was the proceedings and whose sales, the print-seller had told her without being asked, had tripled in a fortnight.

"She asked him what sold before the challenge," Karis said, reading her own assistant's sheet. "He said the tournament. She asked what sold before the tournament. He said nothing; there was no series. He said the series was started *for* you, in the spring, by a man in the wharf street who bet his press on it and has now bought a second press." She set the sheet down. "There is a man in Ostrand whose children eat because you cannot be classified. I want that in your notebook and not in mine."

Cael put it in the notebook. He put beside it the number the sheet made available for the first time, which was the one he had wanted for two years and had never been able to price: at a half-copper a sheet, two series, weekly, four cities — the continent was spending something on the order of a working man's yearly wage every week on the question of what he was.

*Nobody is buying the answer,* he wrote. *There isn't one for sale. They're buying the asking. That's a market that doesn't close when the ruling comes down, and the men who own the presses know it, which is why the series that started in the spring is still running in the winter. Whatever the seat decides, it will be printed within four days, at a half-copper, beside a woodcut of a boy who isn't me.*

The correspondents came up the bluff road twice that month and were declined at the lodge twice, courteously, on a standing instruction Withrow had issued in twelve words and Bracken had posted at the gate in the registry's own type: *the academy's answer to the challenge is its filing; the filing is public.* The second time, one of them waited four hours at the switchback in the cold on the theory that a boy of seventeen would eventually come down a road, and Rooke — who had gone down at dusk on his own business and had not been told to do anything at all — stopped, and looked at him, and said, "He trains at dawn and he doesn't come down. You'll get pneumonia and no copy," and the man thanked him and left, and that was the whole of the academy's press policy for the term.

The post drowned Halcenvane. Bracken's counter took two sacks a day by the month's second week — academies writing to ask whether the demonstration provision was safe to invoke; guild houses writing to withdraw or affirm sponsorships they had never actually extended; three registry-law faculties writing for the response's text, which Withrow released whole, because a filing that perfect deserved readers; and the ordinary mail of a continent that had adopted a story, some of it kind, some of it not, all of it addressed to a boy who read every piece and answered none, per an information policy two years old.

Bracken ran the counter the way he ran everything, which was to build a procedure on the second day rather than complain on the tenth. He put a second table in the records hall's outer room, split the intake four ways with a chalked card above each pile, and entered the day's count in the fire-watch's ledger every evening in his own hand, because the sacks were a fact about the institution and facts about the institution belonged in a ledger. Two hundred and eleven items on the heaviest day. Fourteen of them from chartered academies, which he answered himself, in four lines each, identically, and showed Cael one of the answers unprompted.

"*The demonstration provision remains lawful and remains in force. The academy is not in a position to advise other institutions on their own enrollments.*" He blotted it. "Fourteen academies wrote to ask whether the clause is safe. Not one of them wrote to ask whether you are. I don't offer that as a comment on human nature. I offer it as a description of what a registrar's post looks like when a provision goes under challenge: institutions write about the provision." He tied the day's bundle. "The rest of the continent writes about the boy. Your friend is sorting that pile."

Lira had adopted the sacks. It was the found family's oldest arithmetic — somebody carried what nobody could put down — and she sorted the post at the residence table each evening with the flat efficiency she gave bout sheets: the academies' inquiries to Bracken's tray, the sponsorship letters to Withrow's, the faculties' requests to Karis, and the rest into three piles she named aloud the first night and never renamed. "Kind. Unkind. Unhinged." The kind pile was the largest and the strangest — a barge-master's widow from the confluence who had watched him fight her husband's rival and wanted him to know the river was on his side; a Copper-tier washout from a hill academy who had read the transcript and wanted to explain, at four pages' length, that the corridor was real because he had walked one; a child's letter, in a schoolroom hand, asking whether it hurt to be unclassified. Lira read that one aloud, once, and put it in the kind pile, and nobody at the table said anything for a moment, and Brom refilled the cups.

"Answer none of them," Karis said. "Keep all of them. When this is over, the kind pile is the only record of the year that wasn't written by an office."

"You're a *case* now," Ephram observed, reading the day's sack at the residence table with the cohort captain's file forgotten under his elbow. "At Norhold you were a story. Stories end. Cases get *cited*."

"I've been a case since Denvash."

"You've been a *file* since Denvash. Files are private." Ephram held up a broadside from a city Cael had never seen. "This is a case. Three faculties are teaching you. Somebody at the coastal academy has written a thesis." He set it down. "Try to be a disappointing precedent."

The cost landed on the others first, which Cael did not see for nine days and then could not stop seeing.

It reached Lira at the rating table. The Silver program's clerk had begun logging visitors to the second floor's gallery — Rooke's instruction, unexplained — and the log ran to twenty-three names in a fortnight, of whom four were from academies with no business on the bluff and one had asked the clerk, in the ordinary way of a man making conversation, how often the unclassified enrollee came to watch. Rooke read the log, said nothing to the clerk, and moved the Silver drills to the closed floor for the rest of the month, which cost the program its high windows and its light. "He didn't tell me why," Lira said, at the rail, rolling the burst-shoulder while she said it — iced its two days, cleared for the frames on the Thirdsday, reporting clean under the closed floor's poorer light ever since. "He told the clerk to keep logging."

It reached Brom in the Velmere post, which arrived that fortnight at three letters instead of two, and which he read at the table, folded, without comment, as he had read it since the recess.

It reached Karis at the archive counter in Ostrand, where a request she had filed under the academy's budget code — the certified copy of the founding-era signatures, ordered before any of this — came back with a query attached asking her to confirm the research standing under which it was made. "It's a proper query," she said, in the annex, at the lamp. "Entirely proper. They are entitled to confirm a standing before they certify a copy for it. I have made two hundred archive requests in five years and I have never been asked to confirm a standing." She wrote the confirmation, and filed it, and then sat for a moment with her hands flat on the table in the way she did when she was deciding whether something was a finding. "Once is a clerk being careful. I'll tell you if it's twice."

And it reached the assistant, who was asked in the covered walk by a second-year she barely knew what the researcher's enrollee was *actually* like, and who answered, at length and with citations, that the enrollee was enrolled on a lawful basis adjudicated at public record and renewed at evaluation four times, and who reported the exchange to Karis afterward in some distress at having been, she said, boring.

"You weren't boring," Karis told her. "You were accurate at speed under a question you weren't expecting. That's the whole of the job." She turned back to her page. "Boring is the point. Be boring for another year and I'll have taught you something."

*Every one of them is being asked about me now,* the log got that night, *by people who have no standing to ask, in corridors and at counters and across rating tables, and every one of them is answering the way we agreed years ago nobody would ever have to. I wrote last month that whatever this costs me it shouldn't cost them. It has begun costing them and it hasn't cost me a thing yet. Note the order. The order is the thing I got wrong.*

---

Umber's transmission arrived in the month's second week, and it was the coldest document of the term that was not addressed to Cael, because it was addressed to the proceedings and it was true.

The adjudication office at Norhold, having referred the exhibition bout's result to the registry for evaluation of a demonstrated capability outside classification — the eleven words, the referral, the tables that would hold or teach something about the tables — now formally transmitted the referred file to the proceedings that had taken it up: the match record, the nine panel charts, the unscorable entry. Copied, sealed, sent, under the Chief Adjudicator's own signature, with a covering note in the tower's dry grammar: *the adjudication office's referral having been taken up by the registry, the referred file is transmitted entire for the proceedings' use, and the office notes that the file is the complete record of the office's finding and contains no characterization beyond it.*

It reached Halcenvane as a courtesy copy in the ordinary post, and Karis read it three times and then took it to the annex and did not come out for two hours, and when she came out she had it mapped.

"Understand what he's done, because it's important and it's the opposite of what it looks like." She laid the covering note on the table. "The office referred a result to the registry for evaluation. That referral has been taken up. Once it's taken up, the referring office has no discretion at all — the file goes, entire, on demand. He couldn't have withheld a page of it if he'd wanted to." She put her finger on the last clause. "So he wrote *the file is the complete record of the office's finding and contains no characterization beyond it.* Nine words of it are procedure. The rest is a man on the record refusing, in advance, to be quoted as having meant anything by it."

"Will the seat read the clause?" the counsel asked.

"The clerk will read it into the minute, because it's part of the covering note and the code says the covering note is read." Karis closed the file. "And in a hundred years somebody reading the minute will find a Chief Adjudicator declining to characterize his own referral in the same paragraph that transmits it, and will understand exactly what he thought of the proceedings he was transmitting it to. That's the entire remedy available to him, and he used all of it."

Cael took the covering note to the window and read the nine words twice more and filed the thing that had begun to trouble him about the season's honest men, which was that every one of them, given no lever, reached for the record — and that the record was the one instrument the seat had shown, at the first session, that it received without weighing.

The second session at Ostrand ran on the same trading floor under the same seal, and it was an exhibits session — the code's grammar for a sitting at which the record was built and nothing was decided — and Cael attended it, as Withrow had ruled he would attend everything, and charted it as the machine's second appearance in his life with no face on it.

The gallery was fuller. The correspondents had multiplied and the registry-literate had brought friends, and the room had the specific density of a public watching a case it had already decided in print. The seal sat as it had sat: a competent official of no age, conducting. The counsel entered the response's exhibits in sequence, each read into the minute by the clerk, each received without expression — Prynn's index, Gault's panel minutes, the inspection finding, forty pages on the integrity provisions — and Cael charted the seal through every one and got nothing, and entered the nothing that night as the session's finding: *the seat received eleven pounds of the truth exactly as it received the staffing note the clerk read before it. The instrument doesn't weigh. It receives.*

He timed the entering, because timing it was the only measurement available. Two hundred and six items, read into the minute by the clerk at the rate the clerk read: title, date, provenance, receipt — nine seconds an item on the short ones, twenty on the ones with a certifying line to recite. Prynn's index took seventeen. Gault's four sets of panel minutes took a minute and eight seconds together. The inspection year's finding — eleven days of the Compact's best evaluator in a records hall, a chancellor's tenure staked, a whole autumn of an academy's life — took twenty-two seconds, because the certifying line was long.

The forty pages on the integrity provisions took nine.

Cael added it as it ran and had the total before the clerk did: fifty-one minutes and some seconds to read the whole documentary architecture of four years into a room, at a clerk's pace, without a single word of it being disputed by anyone present. Jent did not object once. There was nothing to object to; the exhibits were what they said they were, and the petitioner had conceded that in writing before the term began. The counsel entered them anyway, all two hundred and six, because the record was the point and the counsel was a professional.

At the fortieth minute Cael stopped charting the seal and started charting the gallery, and found that a third of it had stopped watching. Not from hostility. From the ordinary attrition of people who had come to see a case and were being read a list. The correspondents wrote nothing for nine minutes together. He entered it that night, and it was the coldest line the session produced: *the truth is admissible, unopposed, and boring. They have built a room in which our best work is a procedural delay.*

Withrow's release of the response to the faculties had its own small scene, at the counter, because Bracken had objected — mildly, on procedure; a respondent's filing was the respondent's, and three registry-law faculties asking for it was three institutions about to have opinions. "Let them have opinions," Withrow said, signing the release. "The filing is perfect. Karis says so and Jent said so on the record. A perfect filing that nobody reads is a private loss. A perfect filing three faculties teach is a public fact, and public facts outlast rulings." She handed the copies to the courier. "I spent twenty years in the registry learning what survives a seat's discretion. Nothing survives it in the room. Everything survives it in the libraries."

Jent entered it as an exhibit at the second session. Cael watched him do it — the referred file laid on the petitioner's table, the eleven words read into the proceedings' record by the clerk in a registry voice, the seal receiving it without expression — and turned the thing over that night with the bleak appreciation the season was teaching him to keep for the honest men.

*Umber wrote the truest sentence any instrument ever wrote about me — no number goes here — and refused every prettier alternative, at cost, all night, in front of his own panel. Vastin evaluated me for a day and wrote "continue observation" and stood in a chamber and said the finding was the instruments', not the boy's. Ilsev filed her referrals through channels, properly, with citations, because unanswered questions are pursued through process. Every one of them told the truth. And every one of their true reports is now an exhibit for the petition.*

*The honest men keep filing true reports, and the machine keeps using them as evidence. That's not a flaw in the honest men. It's what the machine is for. It runs on true reports the way a mill runs on water — it doesn't care where the river wanted to go.*

---

Daeva's two documents arrived in the same week, and the delegation understood what the week was the moment the first was read aloud.

The first was public. It came to Halcenvane as a courtesy copy, already filed — a statement to the proceedings, submitted at the registry seat under the filer's own name, rank, and academy, which the code permitted any practitioner of Silver or above to do in an institutional matter and which, Karis established within the hour, no practitioner of Gold had ever done in the registry's recorded history. It came up the bluff road on an ordinary evening in the ordinary post, in a registry-seat envelope that Bracken opened at the counter, read, read again, and then did the only unprofessional thing Cael ever saw him do in three years, which was to leave the counter unattended and walk the sheet to the residence himself at something faster than his own pace.

The table was at dinner. He put it down in front of the counsel without a word, because it was hers to read first, and stood at the end of the table with his hands behind his back and did not sit when Withrow offered him the chair.

The counsel read it at the residence table because the counsel had the copy, and she read it in the voice she used for documents that were going to be cited for a century:

*To the proceedings convened upon the enrollment of the unclassified practitioner of Halcenvane, from Daeva, Auremont Academy, Storm Path, Gold-tier Rank Three, in her own name and under her own rank:*

*I fought him. Your instruments failed him, not the other way around. Classify the instruments.*

That was the whole statement. Three sentences, on registry-seat stock, given under her own hand — the youngest Gold-tier in continental history, the system's favorite anomaly, the practitioner every instrument on the continent talked about instead of measured, entering the record on the side of the boy the instruments could not hold, in fourteen words.

Nobody said anything for a moment. Lira put her cup down without drinking from it. Brom read the sheet after the counsel set it down, once, completely, the way he read the things that cost, and then turned it face up on the table so the words were toward the room, which was not a gesture he had a precedent for.

"Fourteen words," Ephram said, at last. "She could have written forty pages. Auremont has a whole faculty for it."

"Forty pages would have been an argument," Withrow said. She had not touched the sheet. "An argument invites an answer and can be lost. That is a *statement of a witness*, in fourteen words, from the highest-credentialed practitioner alive, and there is no answer to it in the code, because the code did not anticipate anyone at that rank ever bothering." She looked at Bracken, who was still standing. "Registrar. In your thirty years."

"Never," said Bracken. "Not at Gold. Not once." He picked up the envelope, which he had brought with him, and turned it over, and put it down. "She paid the filing fee. Four marks. There's a receipt."

Karis had the statement's legal status mapped by the evening, in the annex, one lamp, the code open at the provision that permitted it: "Any practitioner of Silver or above may enter a statement in an institutional matter touching evaluation standards. It's a fossil — pre-standardization; the era when senior practitioners' opinions were part of how evaluation *worked*. It has been used, in the registry's recorded history, sixty-one times. By Silvers. By Golds, never — Golds don't need to enter statements; Golds are what the statements are about." She turned the page. "And *classify the instruments* is not a request the code contains a form for. She's filed a demand no office can process, under a provision no office remembers, at a rank no one has ever used it from. It's the most Daeva document I've ever read and I've read her challenge filing."

"Will it matter?" Lira asked.

"To the seat? No. To the record?" Karis closed the code. "Sixty-two. Somebody a century from now will read the sixty-second and wonder what the proceedings were thinking. That's the only jurisdiction she's ever had. She used it."

The proceedings noted it at the second session and set it aside. The clerk read it into the minute; the seal received it; Jent did not object, because there was nothing in it to object to, and the seat did not engage it, because engaging it was not contemplated by the schedule. The rhyme was deliberate and everyone at the respondent's table heard it: a chamber with no windows had received the Compact's best evaluator's testimony with courtesy and set it aside, and now the proceedings had received the continent's best practitioner's and done the same — the two most credentialed voices the system had ever produced, both discounted, in the same grammar, because credentials had never been the currency. Cael filed the rhyme in the log with the flat care he kept for findings about the machine's character: *it doesn't matter who says the instruments are wrong. It doesn't matter how high up the ladder they stand when they say it. The ladder isn't the thing that hears.*

Lira's reading of the statement came at the rail the next evening, and it came sideways, the way her important readings did. "Zerin told me *be there*. Next cycle, Silver bracket, in four words, at a board, and then walked off, and I've been building toward it since." She watched the yard. "Daeva's just done the same thing for you. In fourteen words, on registry stock, from the top of the ladder. *Classify the instruments.* That's not a defense. That's *be there* — she's telling the whole machine where she'll be standing when it's done, and daring it to file her somewhere else." She pushed off the rail. "Auremont builds them strange. I'm starting to think it's the only thing they build right."

The second document was private, and it came in the ordinary post, in a hand he had seen once — on a challenge filing, one word, *Overdue* — and he took it to the window, because letters lived at windows, and read it there.

*They did this to me the other way — built the ladder to my height and called it fairness. Nobody files challenges against a favorite. Don't mistake what's happening to you for procedure. It stopped being procedure the day they couldn't score you.*

*— D.*

*The rematch stands. Keep yourself unclassified.*

He read it four times, which was the number he read the letters that mattered, and sat with it while the residence court went dark, because it was direct and without comfort and it was, he understood after the fourth reading, the truest letter anyone had ever written him about his own situation — and it had been written by the one person on the continent who had lived the situation's mirror. *Built the ladder to my height and called it fairness.* She had said the sentence once before, at a ring's north arc, in the plural. She had said it now in the singular, on paper, to him, as the only credential she had left to offer: *I know what the machine's love is worth. I've had all of it.*

*Keep yourself unclassified.*

The log got the letter's whole architecture that night, and the reading:

*She's the only person on the continent the system loves who knows exactly what its love is worth. "Keep yourself unclassified." From her, that's not a joke. That's a strategy. Filed.*

Then he capped the pen and did the filing itself, which was the part the word had been standing in for: behind the volume's front cover, beside a page in Hesk's hand and a page in Vell's. Three pages, three people who had never met one another, each of whom had told him the same thing about the machine in a different grammar. He squared them against the spine, and closed the cover on the three of them, and set the volume down on the sill, squared to the edge.
# Chapter 7 — Assets

The finding came to the residence table on a Fifth-day evening in the term's fourth month, and it came the way Seln's findings always came: flat, complete, in the administrative register, with a single sheet of the office's own paper in front of him on which he had drawn nothing, because a drawing could be found.

What he did not give the table was how he had got it. He never did. So Cael took the finding apart afterward, the way he took a floor apart after a bout — from the thing shown, backward to the thing done — because a finding is a document and the method under it is a second document, and the second was the one he wanted.

The method was the service side. Cael had watched the man walk it for a year and a half without once seeing it as a circuit: the delivery lane behind the refectory, where the bluff road's carts turned and the drivers talked while they waited; the stores counter, where every requisition in the academy passed under a clerk's initial and where the assessment office's own entitlement to read the stores ledger had never once been questioned, because nobody had ever thought to ask what an assessment office wanted with sacking and lamp oil; the post-room's morning sort; the gate lodge's arrivals book, which the office was permitted to inspect on the seventh day of each week and which Seln inspected on the seventh day of each week, standing at the lodge counter for a quarter of an hour with a cup of the lodge-man's tea, talking about the lodge-man's knee.

None of it was surveillance. That, Cael understood a year and a half late, was its whole merit. A man who reads the arrivals book because the code says he may is not watching anything; he is doing his work, in front of the person whose work it is, with a cup in his hand.

And on the seventh day of the third session's week the arrivals book had told him nothing at all, which was the finding — a negative, produced on schedule, by the only man who would have gone to a lodge counter looking for one.

The next joint Cael could build because he had been its subject. Two men at the waystation, senior grade, the rotation running on the waystation's own schedule, and for four years the entire content of their work had been his own movement — where he trained, who he ate with, when he went down the bluff road and in what company. A following watch adjusts to its subject. It is the cheapest tell in the trade and nobody can suppress it, because a man told to follow a subject must go where the subject goes or file a lie. Seln had been keeping the adjustments since his second month at Halcenvane, unwritten, in the only archive that could not be requisitioned, and Cael could produce no evidence that the index existed except the accuracy of the sentence it had made.

In the third session's week his own pattern had moved four times: down the bluff at an unscheduled hour with Brom, twice; the river path alone before dawn on a Fourth-day, which he had not run since the autumn; a Sixth-day at the ferry landing with Karis and two document cases, which was an itinerary a watcher would have paid for.

The rotation had not moved once.

*A watcher who does not adjust to his subject is not watching his subject,* Cael wrote in the notebook, and noted underneath that the sentence was Seln's and he had only found the shape it was cut to. *He is holding a road.*

That was half of it. The other half Cael got out of the post-room over two mornings, without asking for it. The district courier's delivery went into three piles and Seln sorted them, because the clerk had a bad shoulder and Seln had never once been in a hurry. Two of the piles were the academy's. The third was the registrar's, and it was heavy, and it had been heavy on the courier's previous run, and on both runs the assessment office's pile had contained nothing from the district seat at all.

The district seat had stopped writing to the office that watched him and had started writing to the office that held his papers.

The last step the clerk supplied himself, unasked: Seln had put the piles down, said something civil about the shoulder, and carried the office's dispatch back across the fountain court at exactly the pace he carried it every morning.

---

The counter-scouting had never been formally asked for. It was the tournament autumn's pattern, continued into the winter by a man who audited surveillance the way other clerks audited floor schedules, because auditing was what his trade did with its hands when nobody was watching them; and the delegation had learned, across two years, to treat his findings the way instruments read the weather: without asking how the instrument worked.

"The office notes a change in the observation's composition," he said, to the teapot. "The bluff-road rotation is unchanged — two men, senior grade, the waystation schedule holding. But the rotation is no longer the observation." He turned his cup a quarter-turn. "For four years the watchers watched the enrollee. Where he trained. Who he ate with. When he went down the bluff road and in what company. That work is done, and the men at the road are keeping it done, and that is all they are doing." He set the cup down. "Since the second session, the watching has moved. It is no longer on the enrollee. It is on everything *attached* to him."

He gave the table the inventory, because inventories were his native document.

A records request, three weeks old, at Halcenvane's own counter — routine in form, a district office confirming sponsorship files "for the proceedings' record" — which Bracken had answered per procedure and which had asked, in its fourth item, for the academy's full schedule of institutional relationships with the enrollee: sponsorship, floor time, evaluation-linked standing, travel endorsements. Bracken had answered it in twelve days, completely, with a covering letter that noted the completeness twice, because Bracken's whole doctrine was that a true record answered fully and let the office that read it own the consequences. Seln had read the office copy at the registrar's counter with the registrar standing beside him and had said nothing about the fourth item, and had thought about the fourth item for three weeks.

A registry query, twelve days old, at the district seat, touching Lira's certification history — the Fenmark record, the Greyvane re-certification track, the Ostrand station's supersession — "for completeness of the respondent institution's file."

A second query, nine days old, at the Velmere estate's provincial registry, touching Brom's family standing: the household's tier, the sister's guild placement, the estate's registered relationships.

A third, six days old, at Ternhall, touching Karis's transfer file — the vacated standing, the research-access records, the paired-doctrine training log.

"None of them touches the enrollee," Seln said. "Every one of them touches a person the enrollee is attached to, and asks the same question in four different offices' grammar: what does his name reach, and through whom." He squared the blank sheet. "Each is lawful. Each would survive a challenge, and two of them are the kind of query a diligent junior files without being told to. That is not an argument against the pattern. It is how the pattern is built. The office has no formal view. The office observes that this is a *pattern*, and that patterns are the only thing the office was ever trained to see."

The table sat with it, and the sitting took a while, because each of them had been handed a different sentence.

Lira looked at her own hands. She had looked at them in a wool town, at a rail, on the evening she had first been priced right, and the looking then had been a woman checking whether a good thing was hers to keep. This was the same posture doing the opposite arithmetic.

"They're reading my file at Ostrand," she said. "My certification. For *completeness*." She turned one hand over. "That file has a supersession in it. It has the Fenmark entry underneath the supersession, because the registry doesn't delete, it corrects, and the correction sits on top of the thing it corrected, forever, where anyone with a reason can read both." She looked up. "I was fourteen when the bottom of that page got written. I have spent five years getting a line drawn through it by an office with a seal. And a clerk at the district seat has now read both halves, in one afternoon, for completeness."

"They will not use it," Karis said. "There's nothing in it to use."

"I know that." Lira's voice did not move. "I'd still like it noted that they've read it."

Karis noted it. That was the answer the room had, and Lira took it, and it was worth something, and everyone at the table knew exactly how much.

Brom said nothing at all.

That was the whole Velmere ledger opening again, on a table in a provincial registry he had never entered, in a district he had left at fifteen and had returned to exactly twice, both times in a delegation's colors. The household's tier. The sister's guild placement. His father's estate, its registered relationships, its standing — read by a clerk at a counter for the purpose of understanding what a boy in a border town's shop had to do with any of it. He sat with his forearms on his knees and looked at the middle of the table for the length of time he looked at things he intended to remember, and then he asked one question, which was the only question he asked all evening.

"Does the query show at the estate's end? Does somebody there know it was made?"

"The provincial registry logs queries in an open ledger," said Seln. "Anyone entitled to the estate's file may read who has asked after it. Your father is entitled."

Brom nodded once and did not say anything else, and Cael charted the nod, and filed it under the heading he had been building for two years, which was: *Brom's costs are never the ones on the schedule.*

Karis had the notebook out and was writing the four offices in a column, dated, in the hand she used for findings rather than notes. And when she had written the fourth she left a line beneath it — a whole line, ruled, empty — and went on to the next page for her own working, and Cael watched her leave the line and understood that the researcher had already run the shape ahead of the rest of them, and had concluded there would be a fifth, and had allocated it space.

"Why the blank line?" Lira asked her, afterward, at the stair.

"Because an inventory of four is a coincidence," Karis said. "An inventory that includes his family is a document. They haven't reached the family yet." She closed the notebook. "They will. That's how you'd build it if you were building it, and whoever is building this is competent."

---

The fifth arrived four days later, in the ordinary post, in a hand the delegation knew.

It was not in the day's stack when the post-room sorted it; it was on the stack's top, because the post clerk had recognized the hand — the Denvash letters had been arriving at Halcenvane for two years and the clerk knew them by their upright address block and put them out first, as a courtesy to a boy he had never spoken to about anything but weather. Cael took it on the covered walk and opened it walking, which he did with Hesk's letters and nothing else, because Hesk's letters had never once in two years contained bad news and the habit had been built on the record.

*The circulars say the proceedings are at Ostrand and the broadsides say you are a case, and I say a case is what a fiddle travels in, and the fiddle is what matters. Mind the fiddle.*

The man's whole register in the first line: instrument-maker's upright letters, the same letters that had taught Cael his own at a workbench with a lamp over it, the same refusal to grant a large thing more room than it had earned. Two paragraphs on the shop and the winter — the roof over the back store, patched again; the price of gut up a third and nobody's fault; a repair that had taken him nine days because the customer's grandfather had built the instrument wrong in a way the customer loved, and Hesk had spent five of the nine deciding to preserve the fault rather than correct it, which he reported as a business decision and which Cael read as the entire philosophy of the man who had raised him.

And then the third paragraph, which Cael read standing on the covered walk, and read again, and then carried at a pace the walk had not seen from him in a year to the residence, where the table was assembling for the evening and where he read it aloud at the window, because the letter had been opened there and its third paragraph could not be carried across the room unshared.

*Two polite men from the registry came through Denvash asking after my trade licenses and the shop's title. Wanted to know if you had any interest recorded in either. I told them my grandson's interest in this shop is that he grew up in it, and they wrote that down. Whatever they're building, it has a shape. Mind the shape. — H.*

Nobody at the table spoke.

Cael read it a second time, silently, because the letter was the kind he read four times, and the log would have the fourth reading. The first two readings were the table's. *Two polite men from the registry.* In Denvash — the border town, the back exit, the place a fourteen-year-old had been walked out of through a door nobody used, four years and a continent ago; the shop with the sign Hesk had hung himself, the workbench, the leather book, the man who had written *sixteen suits you* and *the fault's in the workshop, never the instrument* and *mind the shape* — visited, politely, by the registry, asking whether the boy's name reached the title of the only building on the continent he had ever called home.

He could put the two men in the shop. That was the thing he could not stop doing, standing at the window with the paper in his hand: the instrument reconstructed the room without being asked. They would have come in the front, because polite men used front doors, and the bell over it would have gone, and Hesk would have come through from the back with his glasses up on his head and his hands not yet wiped, and would have stood behind the counter where he stood for everyone, and would have answered every question they asked, accurately, at the length the question deserved and no longer. He would have offered them nothing. He would not have lied. And when they had gone he would have wiped his hands, and gone back to the nine-day repair, and that evening he would have got the paper out and written *whatever they're building, it has a shape* in the upright letters, and gone to bed.

The two men had written down *grew up in it*. Cael could see the hand doing it. A pencil, a folded form, a category with a number on it.

"They went to Denvash," Lira said. Not a question. The voice she had used at a coat-rack, discovering an institution was small — inverted; discovering one was everywhere.

"They went to Denvash," Cael said.

Seln had the blank sheet in front of him, and he did not write on it, but he laid one finger on it where the fifth line would have gone, and the table watched him do it, and understood that the pattern had its fifth point and that the pattern was now a shape, and that the man at the table's end had known its shape since the second session and had been waiting, per his trade, for the shape to draw itself.

---

He read the letter the third time at the window, alone, after the table had thinned, and the fourth time at the residence table an hour later with the inner circle assembled and Seln at the bench's middle, because the fourth reading was the council's, and the council needed the shape named by the one man at the table who had built shapes like it.

Seln named it in the flat register of a man describing his own former trade — which was, Cael understood as he listened, exactly what the man was doing, and the flatness was the price.

"Pre-restriction inventory." He said it to the blank sheet. "Before the registry freezes an unclassified person's standing under chapter fourteen, it maps everything the standing touches. Sponsors. Institutions. Family assets. Endorsements, credentials, titles, the shop a grandfather holds in a border town. It runs the map in advance so that when the restriction executes, it executes *complete* — every door on the continent closing on the same morning, because every door has already been found." He turned his cup. "It is run only when the outcome is already assumed. Nobody inventories the assets of a man who might be found lawful; the inventory is itself a finding, made before the seat makes one." He set the cup down. "They're not preparing for a ruling. They're preparing for the day after it."

"How long does it take to run?" Karis asked. She had her pen down, which she did not do.

"Six to ten weeks for a person with four relationships. This one has a delegation, an academy, three provincial registries and a border town." Seln's eyes stayed on the sheet. "Nine weeks. They are in the sixth."

"And when it's finished?"

"Nothing," he said. "That is the part the code does not explain and the offices do not need explained. A completed inventory is not an instrument. It is not served, it is not published, and it does not exist as a document with a name — it exists as five files in five offices, each lawful, each unremarkable, indexed to one name. It waits. If the seat rules for the respondent, it is never assembled, and in four years a clerk clears it, and nobody has done anything wrong at any point." He squared the blank sheet against the table's edge. "If the seat rules the other way, the assembly takes an afternoon, and the restriction executes on every file at once, and the enrollee learns what the sixth week was for on the morning it is already finished."

The sentence sat on the table, and the table let it sit, because there was nothing to say to it that its own grammar had not already said.

Karis wrote the fifth office in her column — *Denvash, provincial registry, trade licenses and title* — and dated it, and drew a line under the five, and the line was a shape.

Then she set the pen down beside the notebook and said the thing she had been holding since the stair, in the flat voice she used for findings she had checked twice.

"I want it on the record that the four of them were about *us*. Certification, family standing, transfer file — those are the three of us at the table. The fifth is a man who has never been near an academy and has no standing to freeze." She looked at Cael. "Hesk isn't in the inventory because Hesk is an asset. He's in it because the ninth category is *other relationships*, and the shape they're building has to be complete, and the only way to complete a shape around you is to find everything you would not want touched." She closed the notebook. "That's not restriction machinery. That's the same machinery with a second use, and I'd like somebody at this table to say out loud that we noticed."

"Noticed," said Brom.

Brom looked at the letter in Cael's hands for the length of time he looked at things he intended to remember — at the upright letters, at the address block, at the fold — and said nothing more, and the nothing had Velmere in it, and had also, Cael thought, a shop in it, and a man behind a counter with his glasses up on his head, whom Brom had never met and had heard about at a hundred tables.

It was Lira who turned the room, and she did it the way she had turned every corner of her life, by declining the premise.

"So the question was never whether we win the argument." She said it evenly, a woman reading a bout sheet's honest arithmetic aloud. "Karis wins every filing. The counsel wins every session. Cael wins every point that can be argued. And their men are in Denvash measuring the shop." She looked around at them. "The question is what we do the day after."

Nobody answered that either. But Cael charted the table as she said it, the way he charted everything, and the charting recorded four people not answering in four registers — Karis with her hand flat on a closed notebook, Brom with his forearms on his knees, Lira with the question still open in front of her and her chin up over it — and one of the four, the man at the bench's middle, who had built inventories like this one and knew what the day after them looked like from the inside, not answering in a fifth, which was the register of a man whose reasons for keeping a thing sealed had just been handed their first invoice.

---

Withrow was informed that evening, in her office, by Cael, with the letter in his hand, and she read it standing, and the paragraph about Denvash produced in her the thing he had never once seen it produce in two years: a pause, mid-page, with nothing on her face, that lasted long enough to count.

He counted it. Nine seconds. She had read the first two paragraphs in twelve.

"Trade licenses and title," she said, at the end of it. "In Denvash. On an instrument-maker." She handed the letter back. "I administered two restrictions. I told you that."

"You did. At the enrollment."

"I did not tell you that I ran the inventories myself, both times, because the district was short-staffed and I was thorough." She sat down, which she did not do while he stood, and gestured him to the chair opposite, which she did not do either. "The second one I remember better, and I'll tell you it because you should hear a completed one from somebody who completed it, rather than assemble it out of Seln's grammar and your own imagination, which will be worse and will also be wrong in the wrong direction."

"How wrong?"

"Too dramatic," Withrow said. "There is no scene in it. That's the thing you need to have in your hands." She put her palms flat on the desk. "A Bronze Rank 4. Force Path, thirty-one years old, a haulage guild's contracted practitioner in a river district, married, two children, a house with fourteen years left owing on it. He came up on an evaluation-integrity matter of his own — not yours; nothing like yours; his Kindling record had a defect in it that was somebody else's error twenty years earlier and could not be cured because the evaluating officer was dead. The re-evaluation returned no valid classification. That is the whole of what he did wrong: an instrument was pointed at him and produced nothing, twice."

"And the inventory."

"Nine categories." She counted them off on the desk with two fingers, not looking at her hand. "Registered employment. Guild membership and standing. Credentials and endorsements. Property held or part-held. Financial instruments and debts. Institutional enrollments, his own and his dependents'. Travel and transit endorsements. Public office or contracted service. And *other relationships.*" She stopped. "It took me seven weeks. I was proud of it, in the way you are proud of a thorough piece of work, and I have thought about that pride for twenty-five years."

"What happened on the morning?"

"Nothing happened. That's the answer. Nothing happened *at* him." Her voice did not rise; it flattened, which he had learned was the same thing. "I did not go to his house. Nobody went to his house. Nine instruments went out from four offices on one morning, and by the second bell his guild had suspended his contract because a guild may not contract an unclassified practitioner, and the haulage yard had put another man on his run, and his bank had frozen the house's instrument because the instrument named his standing as security, and his daughter's academy had written to say her enrollment was under review because a dependent's enrollment carries the parent's standing, and his transit endorsement had lapsed, which meant he could not lawfully leave the district to argue about any of it." She looked at the charter above the file cases. "He found out in the order the post arrived. He came to my counter on the fourth day with all nine letters in a satchel and asked me which one to answer first. I told him the truth, which was that they were not nine problems. They were one problem with nine faces, and the one problem had no counter to be answered at, because the restriction is not an act. It is a *condition.* There is nobody to argue with. That is the design."

Cael was quiet. Then: "What did he do?"

"He went across the registry line — east, past the last station — eventually. Most of them do; there is nowhere else to be an unclassified man with a family. It took him a year and a half to get there, because a transit endorsement takes a year and a half to work around, and I do not know what happened after, because a person who crosses that line stops producing records and records are the only eyes I have." Withrow put her hands together. "Now. Your grandfather. They wrote 'grew up in it' under the ninth category, because that is what he told them, and it will be entered, and it will be true, and it will be *there*, on the day the ninth category is executed. Nothing will be alleged against him. Nothing needs to be. A trade license held by a man with a recorded relationship to a restricted person is a license a district office may review. Reviewing is not refusing. It is only slow."

"How slow?"

"As slow as anyone wants," she said. "That's the whole instrument. Not a hand on the door. A hand on the clock."

She stood, then, and went to the window, and the fountain court was below it and the dark was down over the bluff, and she said the last of it to the glass.

"I have been choosing. I want you to know I'm still choosing. I also want you to know that from tonight the word costs more than it did, and that I've priced it, and I'm paying."

---

The log, at the window, with the letter on the sill and the fountain running in the dark, was the first pole cracking at its edge, and it went down in one pass with nothing struck out, which was how the honest entries arrived:

*I keep winning sessions. Karis keeps winning filings. And their men are in Denvash measuring my grandfather's shop. Somebody explain to me which part of that is the system working — because I built three years on the difference between the system and the people, and the difference is getting hard to find in the dark.*
# Chapter 8 — Preliminary

The third session at Ostrand was the full one, and the delegation went down the bluff road for it in the coach at first light with the whole term's argument packed in two cases, and the coach was quiet the way it had been quiet on the road to Norhold: nobody circling the thing, because the thing had stopped needing to be circled.

Cael charted the ride anyway, because riding was cataloguing time. Lira with the Silver drill-book shut on her knee, the program's first road meet three weeks out and her whole attention on a trading floor instead. Brom with his forearms on his knees and the Velmere letter's second envelope in his kit — he had said nothing about it in four weeks and the table had said nothing back, per the arrangement, and the arrangement was beginning to cost the table something. Karis with the closing summary in her lap, not reading it, because she had written it and could not have improved it and knew both things. Seln at the coach's end with the office's cross-index and his eyes shut. The counsel with the privileged minute open, adding to it in her small exact hand as the river went by. And Withrow, in the registry grey, at the coach's head, looking at the road the way she had looked at the bluff road from a waystation the spring before, with the flinty enjoyment of a person whose worrying was done in advance — except that it was not enjoyment now; it was the same face with the enjoyment removed, and what was left was load-bearing.

"We win it," Karis said, at the ferry, to the river. "On the text. Every point. I want everyone braced for what winning feels like, again, because it will feel like winning, again, and it isn't."

"You said that before the first session," said Lira.

"I'll say it before every session. Repetition is how you keep a finding from turning into a hope."

---

The registry seat at Ostrand had been a wool exchange for a hundred and fifty years before it had been a registry seat, and it had never troubled to stop looking like one, which was the first fact Cael had catalogued about it at the first session and the fact he came back to at every session since.

It stood at the top of the market street on a stone plinth with four steps, and its double doors were merchant doors, wide enough to walk bales through, and the hall behind them was a trading floor: a long room under a clerestory, with a raised platform at the north end where the wool factors' arbitration bench had sat and where the registry's seal now sat instead, and a rail running the room's whole length at waist height that had once separated buyers from stock and now separated the gallery from the proceedings. The registry had put a chartered seal over the doors, had replaced the arbitration bench with a registrar's bench, and had changed nothing else, including the floor's brass inlay, which still marked out the exchange's old lot positions in a grid nobody had swept away.

Cael sat at the respondent's table on lot seventeen. He had established this at the first session, on his hands and knees during the recess, and had told nobody except Karis, who had written it down.

He catalogued the room again while the clerk opened the minute, because the room was the machine and the machine's parts were countable. Forty gallery benches, filled to the rails and standing at the back — the registry-literate of three districts, the correspondents at the front two benches with the folding writing-boards that marked continental papers, four of them with the flat cases of houses that could afford a courier standing outside. The petitioner's table, north-west of the seal, one man and one clerk. The respondent's table, north-east, four: the counsel, Bracken, Withrow, and the enrollee of record. The records table behind and below the seal's platform, two officers on rotation. The evaluation seat, west of the seal, at an angle to both tables — a chair, a lap-desk, and a low rail of its own — which at the first session had been empty and at the second had been empty and which today was not.

The seal itself sat above all of it in the registrar's bench and had no face. That was not a figure of speech and Cael had stopped treating it as one. The presiding registrar entered from the platform's own door, wearing the district's full grey with the hood forward as the code prescribed for a seated presiding officer, and was identified in the minute by the seal's designation and by nothing else, and spoke in a registrar's voice that could have belonged to any of a hundred people, and had presided over two sessions in which not one word had been addressed to it as a person. Karis had checked. There was no procedural defect in it whatsoever. The code permitted an unnamed presiding seal in institutional matters and had permitted it for four hundred years, for reasons the code gave in a single clause about the impartiality of the office over the officer, and the clause was, Karis said, the most beautifully argued sentence in registry law and the most convenient one ever written.

*The face the proceedings show is nobody's,* the log had recorded after the first session. *That isn't a hiding place. It's the design, published, four hundred years old, and defensible in every line. I keep looking for the trick and finding architecture.*

---

The trading floor was full to the rails, and the seal sat as it had sat, and the evaluation seat — empty at two sessions — had a person in it, and Cael read the person from the respondent's table before the clerk had finished the opening minute, because he had read her once before, across a hearing room, two years and a continent ago.

Ilsev.

Senior evaluator of record, in the registry's grey, files squared on the seat's lap-desk at angles that had not varied by a degree since the first counter she had ever read his case across — the woman who had read that case at the counter of his life and had been, in every recorded act since, exactly correct. She had come in with the working morning's traffic rather than with the officers, carrying her own files, and had set them down and squared them, and had spent the four minutes before the opening reading the topmost one with the specific attention of a person who has read it three times already and intends to read it a fourth.

Empaneled. The clerk read the empanelment into the minute: *the evaluation seat constituted, the district's senior evaluator presiding, scope per the code's institutional provisions.* Ilsev inclined her head to the seal at the exact depth the registry taught, and opened the first of her files, and the room proceeded, and Cael sat with the reading the sight of her produced, which was not comfort and was not its opposite.

*The most honest instrument the Compact owns,* the log would say that night, *installed in a machine that is counting on her honesty to legitimize it. They didn't put her there despite her scruples. They put her there because of them. A seat that nobody can accuse of leaning is the only seat that can carry a corridor without the corridor showing.*

Behind her, at the records table, on rotation: Havel. He logged the session's opening in the records officer's ledger and did not look at the respondent's table, and did not look at it through the whole of the morning, and from Havel — four years, three postings, five entries in a private note that Cael had never seen and knew existed the way he knew the anomaly existed — the not-looking was a sentence with a subject and a verb, and the sentence was *I am on rotation and I am doing my rotation and I am not permitted to be anything else in this room, and I have decided to be exactly that, completely, so that nobody can say otherwise.*

---

Jent argued the petition, and he argued it in forty minutes, and it was — the counsel would say this in the coach, with professional distaste and professional respect in equal measure — the cleanest submission she had heard in twenty-six years, because it contained no argument at all.

He rose without papers. That was the first thing Cael charted, and he charted it because it was information: a procurator with four hundred enrollment matters behind him, arguing the largest one on the continent, standing up at the petitioner's table with his hands empty and his clerk's tabbed folder untouched at his elbow. He was a spare, colorless man in his fifties with a voice pitched exactly loud enough to reach the fortieth bench and not one degree past it, and he addressed the seal and never once the gallery, and in forty minutes he did not raise the voice, sharpen it, or slow it for emphasis. He read weather.

He conceded the enrollment. He did it first, in his opening minute, before anyone could make him: the enrollment was lawfully executed under a valid provision, the petitioner did not contend otherwise, and any reading of the petition as an allegation of irregularity was a misreading the petitioner wished corrected on the record now, at the outset, so that the seat's time would not be spent on it.

He conceded the provision. The assay clause was law; the standardization directive had not repealed it; the petitioner accepted the respondent institution's reconstruction of its provenance in full and adopted it.

He conceded the documentation, by name, with the compliment repeated into the record — *the best-documented enrollment this procurator has reviewed* — and he added, when the clerk asked him to confirm the phrasing for the minute, that the clerk should enter it as spoken and that he would not soften it on reflection.

There was a small sound from the gallery at that. Cael, charting, could not decide what it meant, and wrote down that he could not.

And then, having given away everything an opponent could want, Jent laid out the petition's actual substance, in the registry's own grammar, in four moves, each of which he numbered aloud for the minute.

First: that the evaluation-integrity provisions existed to protect the registry's confidence in its own evaluations — not the practitioner's rights, not the institution's, but the registry's confidence, which the code named as the provisions' object in the provisions' own opening line, and which he read out.

Second: that a practitioner no standard evaluation could read was, by definition and not by accusation, a practitioner in whom the registry could place no evaluated confidence. He was careful with the word *by definition*. He used it three times and defined it once.

Third: that the demonstration provision, however lawfully executed, was a *description* of such a practitioner and not an *evaluation* of him, since it ran on no Arbiter — and here he did the thing that made him, Cael understood with a species of cold admiration, unhateable: he corrected himself. He had begun to say that the provision produced no record capable of evaluation, stopped mid-sentence, and said that the formulation was too broad and that the seat should disregard it, because the provision plainly produced a record, and the petitioner's contention was narrower — that the record was of a different kind, and that the difference was the whole matter. He asked the clerk to strike the broader formulation. The clerk struck it.

Fourth: that the registry's discretion to compel re-evaluation in an integrity matter was plenary and undisputed, and that the petitioner did not need the seat to find anything defective in order to exercise it.

And so the petitioner asked the seat, not to find the enrollment defective — it was not — but to find the integrity question *cognizable*, and to order the remedy the code provided for cognizable integrity questions, which was re-evaluation under the standard protocol, the enrollment to stand or fall on its result.

He tendered the exhibits. Umber's referred file, entered at the second session, cited once, with courteous efficiency: *the adjudication office's own finding that the practitioner's demonstrated capability lies outside the Standard's tables — which is to say, outside evaluation.* He did not read the tournament result. He did not mention the match, or the crowd, or the name of the Gold-tier who had fought it. He cited a page number.

He sat down at the correct height.

Cael had spent three sessions building a file on him and closed it that morning with an entry he disliked writing: *He isn't cruel. He isn't even cold — he's courteous, and the courtesy is real, and he corrected his own overstatement against his own interest in front of forty benches because the correction was true. If he were hateable this would be easier and I would be learning less. He is a good instrument, being used well, and the file to open is not his.*

---

The counsel argued the response, and she argued it for two hours, and the term's discipline held all the way down, because the defense was *excellent*.

She rose with papers and did not consult them. She was a big, unhurried woman of sixty with a voice built for stone rooms, and she had been Halcenvane's instructed counsel since the enrollment and had told Withrow at the retainer that she took institutional work because institutional work was the only place left where a lawyer could still lose slowly enough to learn something. She argued in four movements and she signposted every one of them, because a two-hour submission that a gallery cannot follow is a two-hour submission nobody remembers.

*First, the text.* The assay clause's provenance: orphaned, not repealed. Three independent reconstructions — the academy's, the researcher's, and the district archivist's, commissioned by the counsel herself precisely because two agreeing reconstructions are a coincidence and three are a finding — arriving at one conclusion by three roads. She put the directive's own transitional schedule into the record and walked the seal through the schedule's structure item by item, and at the ninth item she stopped and said that the seat would notice, as she had, that the schedule enumerated the provisions it superseded and that the assay clause was not among them, and that the drafters of that schedule had been careful men, and that carefulness cut in only one direction.

*Second, the precedent.* Greyvane's ruling, entered. Four prior challenges to demonstration-provision enrollments, all sustained on the text, entered with their citations. And the point she drew from the four: the demonstration provision's whole litigated history was a shelf of institutions losing to a clause they had failed to read, which meant the clause had been tested — repeatedly, adversarially, by well-resourced parties — and had held every time it had been tried.

*Third, the integrity provisions themselves,* from Karis's forty pages. Their origin in the auditing of *instruments*: the counsel read the provisions' enacting preamble aloud, which took ninety seconds and which nobody in the room, by the look of the benches, had ever heard read. Their ninety-one recorded invocations, which the researcher had traced individually and tabulated by object — Arbiter frames, sixty-two; station calibration records, nineteen; evaluation protocols and their custody, three — and seven against an individual's conduct, and against what a practitioner *is*, exactly never. And the plain textual fact that a provision written to test whether an instrument could be trusted could not, without violence to its own language, be turned to ask whether a person could be.

The seal put its only question of the session there. Whether the counsel contended that the provisions were incapable in law of application to a practitioner, or merely that they had not been so applied.

"The second, and the second is enough," said the counsel, "because the seat is being asked to be the first in four hundred years, and a seat asked to be the first should be told so plainly, which the petition does not do."

Jent, on his feet immediately, said that the petitioner had never contended the application was routine, and would accept a minute entry that it was novel.

"The petitioner will accept a minute entry that it is novel," the counsel repeated, to the gallery for the first and only time in two hours, and sat back down into her fourth movement.

*Fourth, the demonstration record as evaluation.* Four semester panels, sighted, sealed, renewed. A public season under a continental rating instrument. A tournament fielded under a charter clause, and an inspection by the Compact's own senior evaluator that had declined to find. More evaluation, she said, than any Iron-tier practitioner on the continent had ever received in the whole of a career, conducted by human beings with eyes, under seal, on the record, over three years, by twenty-two separate officers whose names she then read into the minute, one at a time, at reading pace, which took four minutes and which the gallery sat through in complete silence.

Karis passed one card forward during the third movement and one during the fourth. Cael read both upside down without meaning to, because reading upside down was a thing he could not switch off: the first was a citation correction, a page number struck and re-entered. The second said only *don't take the fifth point, it's not needed*, and the counsel glanced at it, and did not take the fifth point.

And she closed with the sentence Karis had written for her and she had made her own: "The petitioner asks the seat to prefer the silence of an instrument to the testimony of every human evaluator who has ever assessed this practitioner. The seat should decline. The record is the evaluation. It has been for three years. The only thing that has changed is who is asking."

She sat. The gallery — forty benches of correspondents and the registry-literate — did the thing galleries did when they had heard craft, which was a low sound, sustained. Ilsev wrote for a long time. The seal received it.

---

The midday adjournment ran an hour and the respondent's party spent it in the exchange's old wool-hall corridor, which had windows and a bench and a smell of lanolin two centuries had not got out of the stone.

Nobody said the submission had been good. Bracken said the citation correction had landed cleanly. Withrow ate half a roll and gave the rest to a correspondent's runner who had been standing in the corridor for two hours with nothing. Lira and Brom had come down for the session and had sat in the gallery's fourth bench and had not been able to hear the first movement properly, and Karis explained the transitional schedule to them against the corridor wall in six sentences, with her hands, and Brom said "so the men who wrote the list of what they were cancelling didn't put it on the list," and Karis said "that is the entire first movement, yes," and Brom said "then why does it take two hours," and Karis said, "Because they might be right about the second thing."

Havel came through the corridor at the adjournment's end with an armful of exhibit folders, going the other way. He was three feet from Cael in a corridor eight feet wide. He looked at the folders. Cael stood aside to give him the room, and Havel said "thank you" to the folders, and went on, and it was the only word that passed between them at Ostrand that term.

---

The ruling came after the midday adjournment, and it came in one paragraph, read by the seal in a registrar's voice from a page the seal had plainly drafted during the adjournment, and the paragraph did both things at once, exactly as Karis had said it would, and the doing was the corridor's first door closing on schedule.

*The seat, having heard the petition and the response, finds as follows. The enrollment's execution is sound. The provision under which it was executed is valid law, surviving the standardization directive unrepealed, and the seat so holds. The documentary record of the enrollment is complete and is not disturbed. The respondent institution has conducted itself throughout in full compliance with its charter.*

Every point. Every documentary point that could be won, won, in the seal's own type, on the record, forever.

The gallery had begun to move at *is not disturbed* — the correspondents' boards coming up, one man on the second bench already half-risen to get a courier out ahead of the others — and the seal read straight through the movement without a pause of any kind, in the same voice, at the same pace.

*Notwithstanding the enrollment's validity, the seat finds the evaluation-integrity question raised by the petition to be cognizable under the code's integrity provisions, the applicability of those provisions to a practitioner being a matter within the registry's discretion which the seat exercises. The challenge is sustained to full proceedings. The petition for compelled re-evaluation under standard Arbiter protocol will be heard on the merits at the schedule's remaining sessions, and the seat will rule upon it before term's end.*

Both things at once. You are lawful. The machine will proceed.

The half-risen man sat back down. Cael charted the room in the four seconds after the paragraph ended, because four seconds after a ruling is when a room tells the truth: the counsel writing without expression; Bracken's hands flat on the table; Withrow's chin at the exact angle it had held for two hours; forty benches of registry-literate people working out, at forty different speeds, that they had just watched a respondent win and lose in one paragraph; and at the petitioner's table, Jent, who did not smile, did not gather his papers early, and did not look across the floor even once — a man receiving a result he had been sent to obtain, in the posture of a man receiving weather.

Ilsev's beat came at the ruling's end, and the session rationed it carefully, and she spent it as she had spent every beat of her career, on the text's edges.

She asked leave to address the seat. It was granted. She asked the seal, for the record, to confirm the evaluation seat's scope.

The seal confirmed it: the seat's mandate was to assess the *procedure* of any re-evaluation ordered, its conformity to protocol, its conduct; not the outcome.

"And not," said Ilsev, "the question of whether the protocol is capable of evaluating this practitioner."

"That question is not within the evaluation seat's scope," said the seal.

"I ask that the answer be entered in the minute in those words," said Ilsev, "so that the seat's scope is not later understood to have included it."

It was entered. Ilsev entered the confirmation in her own file as well, in her own hand, and squared the file, and Cael, watching from the respondent's table, read the entering the way he had read her at Greyvane — a textualist marking the text's boundary so that the text could not later be said to have meant more than it said — and understood, with the instrument's whole cold clarity, that she had just told the room, in the only language she had, that her seat would certify the corridor's *conduct* and had no authority over where it led.

Adjourned. The seal rose; the room rose; Withrow inclined her head at the exact depth.

The market street outside had four hours of afternoon left in it and the correspondents got their couriers away inside three minutes, which Cael timed from the top step, because the speed of a ruling's departure was a measurement and he had nothing else to do with the interval while the cases were brought down.

Four couriers, four directions, all four riding before the gallery had finished emptying. By the following week the paragraph would be in print in three districts, and it would be printed accurately, and it would be understood wrongly by almost everyone who read it, because a paragraph that says *lawful* and *proceeding* in the same breath is not a paragraph most people have a shape for. Half the continent would read it as a win. The other half would read it as the beginning of a fraud finding. Nobody outside the room would read it as what it was, which was a door being closed politely with the respondent still inside and thanked for his documentation.

Jent came down the steps behind them with his clerk and the untouched tabbed folder, and paused at the bottom, and said to the counsel, without ceremony, "Your third movement was better than the authorities you had for it."

"I know," said the counsel.

"So did the seat," Jent said, and put his hat on, and went up the market street on foot.

The counsel watched him go the length of two shopfronts. "Twenty-six years," she said, to nobody in particular, "and that is the first opponent I have ever had who tells me what I got right on the way out. He does it because it costs him nothing. It's still true." She took her case from Bracken. "Brace yourselves for the next one. He'll concede more."

"There's nothing left to concede," said Bracken.

"There is always something left to concede," said the counsel, "when conceding is how you advance."


---

The coach took the river road up in the last of the light with the same seven it had carried down — the five of them, the counsel, and the chancellor — the ruling's paper in the counsel's minute-book and the day's closing exchange waiting for whoever would spend it.

Karis spent it, evenly, to the window.

"We won every point that can be won on paper."

Brom, after the length of time Brom took: "And?"

"And it's proceeding."

Nobody added anything for a while. The river went by. Lira looked at the drill-book on her knee and did not open it, and Cael charted her not opening it, and charted Brom's forearms on his knees, and charted the counsel writing in the privileged minute the one line he could read upside down — *ruling: sustained on validity; sustained to proceedings; both* — and charted the Archmarshal's absence from the day, which had been total, an evaluator not contemplated by the schedule, and the seal's presence, which had also been total, and had no face.

He had the night's log by the ferry crossing, and it was one line, because one line was the honest count: *Part of me is still keeping score. The rest of me has started counting doors.*

Then Seln, from the corner, eyes closed, in the voice of a man who had audited the machine from inside for fifteen years and had never once, until this term, heard the audit read aloud by people who had not been trained to it:

"Now you understand the file I've been reading my whole career."

