    76	
    77	It was not in the day's stack when the post-room sorted it; it was on the stack's top, because the post clerk had recognized the hand — the Denvash letters had been arriving at Halcenvane for two years and the clerk knew them by their upright address block and put them out first, as a courtesy to a boy he had never spoken to about anything but weather. Cael took it on the covered walk and opened it walking, which he did with Hesk's letters and nothing else, because Hesk's letters had never once in two years contained bad news and the habit had been built on the record.
    78	
    79	*The circulars say the proceedings are at Ostrand and the broadsides say you are a case, and I say a case is what a fiddle travels in, and the fiddle is what matters. Mind the fiddle.*
    80	
    81	The man's whole register in the first line: instrument-maker's upright letters, the same letters that had taught Cael his own at a workbench with a lamp over it, the same refusal to grant a large thing more room than it had earned. Two paragraphs on the shop and the winter — the roof over the back store, patched again; the price of gut up a third and nobody's fault; a repair that had taken him nine days because the customer's grandfather had built the instrument wrong in a way the customer loved, and Hesk had spent five of the nine deciding to preserve the fault rather than correct it, which he reported as a business decision and which Cael read as the entire philosophy of the man who had raised him.
    82	
    83	And then the third paragraph, which Cael read standing on the covered walk, and read again, and then carried at a pace the walk had not seen from him in a year to the residence, where the table was assembling for the evening and where he read it aloud at the window, because the letter had been opened there and its third paragraph could not be carried across the room unshared.
    84	
    85	*Two polite men from the registry came through Denvash asking after my trade licenses and the shop's title. Wanted to know if you had any interest recorded in either. I told them my grandson's interest in this shop is that he grew up in it, and they wrote that down. Whatever they're building, it has a shape. Mind the shape. — H.*
    86	
    87	Nobody at the table spoke.
