    89	Cael read it a second time, silently, because the letter was the kind he read four times, and the log would have the fourth reading. The first two readings were the table's. *Two polite men from the registry.* In Denvash — the border town, the back exit, the place a fourteen-year-old had been walked out of through a door nobody used, four years and a continent ago; the shop with the sign Hesk had hung himself, the workbench, the leather book, the man who had written *sixteen suits you* and *the fault's in the workshop, never the instrument* and *mind the shape* — visited, politely, by the registry, asking whether the boy's name reached the title of the only building on the continent he had ever called home.
    90	
    91	He could put the two men in the shop. That was the thing he could not stop doing, standing at the window with the paper in his hand: the instrument reconstructed the room without being asked. They would have come in the front, because polite men used front doors, and the bell over it would have gone, and Hesk would have come through from the back with his glasses up on his head and his hands not yet wiped, and would have stood behind the counter where he stood for everyone, and would have answered every question they asked, accurately, at the length the question deserved and no longer. He would have offered them nothing. He would not have lied. And when they had gone he would have wiped his hands, and gone back to the nine-day repair, and that evening he would have got the paper out and written *whatever they're building, it has a shape* in the upright letters, and gone to bed.
    92	
