 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Chapter 17 — Under the Gallery
     2	
     3	Wing three had a gallery after all.
     4	
     5	Cael had fought in the room every Fifth-day for a year without once looking up at the west wall above the door. There had never been anything there worth looking at: a narrow timber balcony, a rail of dark old oak, and behind the rail a stack of spare benches and broken frames that the porters had been putting there since before he came up the hill. The house had built the gallery in the days when wing three was its examination room, so that visiting assessors could look down on a candidate without standing on the same boards. Nobody had visited for a long time. The benches had gone up into it one at a time, the way things go into a loft.
     6	
     7	On the Fourth-day morning the benches were gone. The porters had swept the boards up there and rubbed the rail, and in the corners where the broom had not reached, the dust lay in soft grey drifts that showed exactly how long it had been. Four people stood at the rail.
     8	
     9	He knew two of them. The pair from the east hall, in their travelling coats, each with his board. The one who wrote stood at the left. The one who did not write stood beside him with his hands behind his back. The third was a woman in the blue-grey of the lake house, with a folding board open on the rail in front of her and three pencils laid out on it in a row. The fourth was an old man in a fur collar who had brought a stool with him and sat on it, so that only his head and shoulders showed above the oak, like a judge at a country fair.
    10	
    11	The rest of the room was what it always was. Gault at the trestle in the middle of the north side, with the instrument case set down very straight beside him. The Ash instructor on his right with her book against her chest, the Mire instructor on his left with a pencil behind each ear. The desk clerk at the trestle's end with the wing's book open. And at the east end of the room, at the rail under the high windows, the cohort, more of them than usual, with Ephram in the middle holding his soft-folded chart.
    12	
    13	Rooke stood beside Ephram.
    14	
    15	He was not on any list for wing three. He had never come to a provision bout in the year Cael had been fighting them. He stood at the rail with his arms folded and his eyes on the gallery over the west door, and he did not look at Cael at all.
    16	
    17	Cael went to his chalk at the south end. He did not take Hesk's book out of his coat. The line had been written at the sill the night before, and he had it by heart.
    18	
    19	The Current came in from the south door.
    20	
    21	She was a third-year from the movement floor, Iron Rank Four by the porter's wall, a tall girl with long legs and a quiet face, who glided the way water goes over a weir: smooth, and a little faster at the end than you expected. He had watched her in the Crown yard. He had never stood across a floor from her.
    22	
    23	She came over and shook his hand, and looked up once, quickly, at the gallery.
    24	
    25	"Is that for you?" she said.
    26	
    27	"I think so."
    28	
    29	"Then I'm sorry about the first exchange," she said, "because I'm going to take it." And she went to her chalk.
    30	
    31	Gault turned the glass.
    32	
    33	"Exchange," said the desk clerk.
    34	
    35	She opened the way the textbook said a Current should open, which was the way she always opened: two glides in, the second one bending out wide to his left, so that the strike came off the end of the curve from a place he had not been looking.
    36	
    37	He gave her the read.
    38	
    39	It went out under thought and came back with her weight. On the first glide she carried it in her back hip, which was right. On the second, half a beat before the bend began, her weight moved into the outside of her front foot, and he knew where the curve would end before she did.
    40	
    41	And he could see the other thing, as clearly as he had seen it at the sill: Lira's answer, one burst, low, *along* the bend instead of across it, and himself standing at the end of the curve with his palm out when the Current arrived.
    42	
    43	The book said the first exchange was hers.
    44	
    45	He did not burst. He stepped the way an honest Iron steps against a Current, a fraction late, and met the end of her curve with his forearm up. Her strike came off the bend and took him across the forearm, flat and fast, and the give was there, Reydan's give, waiting to drink the whole of the blow into the arm and keep it. He shut it. He let the blow go on up the arm the way it would have gone in anybody, into the elbow and then the shoulder, and felt it arrive there like a door slammed somewhere in a house.
    46	
    47	"Touch," said Gault. "Current."
    48	
    49	Nought to one.
    50	
    51	Up in the gallery a pencil moved. He did not look. He did not need to; the read did not reach that far, but he had stood in enough rooms to know the sound a board makes when a man leans his wrist on it to write.
    52	
    53	He walked back to his chalk with the forearm ringing and set his feet.
    54	
