   173	It was the preamble that made the pencils go down, because nobody on those benches had heard it before. She laid her papers face down to read it. It was ninety seconds long, plain and old, and it said what the integrity sections were for: the testing of instruments, frames and stations and records and protocols, so that the registry might know which of its instruments it could trust. Then Karis's ninety-one, and Cael felt Karis go still beside him. Eighty-four about things. Seven about persons, every one of the seven about something a person had done. None, in ninety-one, about what a person was.
   174	
   175	"Only that nobody ever has," the counsel said then, without a pause, and Cael understood from her answer what the hood had asked: whether the sections could never lawfully reach a practitioner, or only had never been made to. "That's enough. In all the years these sections have stood, this seat is asked to go first, and turn them on a person for what he is. A seat that's asked to go first deserves to hear it in plain words. The petition never used them."
   176	
   177	Jent rose to say the petitioner had never called the use an ordinary one, and was content for the minute to say it was new. The counsel said it after him, word for word, and this once, the only time all morning, she said it over her shoulder to the benches. Third turn. Tick.
   178	
   179	The shelf went quickly: Greyvane, and four older rulings behind it, four houses that had gone to law against this clause with money and counsel and lost on the text. Tick.
   180	
   181	And last, because the whole case ended in something that was not there, the drafters' list. She took up the directive that had standardized the code and turned to the schedule at its back where its drafters had listed every older provision they meant to cancel, and walked the hood down it slowly, item by item. At the bottom she stopped. They had been careful men, she said. They had listed everything they meant to end. The assay clause was not on their list. Care only runs one way.
   182	
   183	Twice Karis passed a card forward to her, and Cael, who could never help reading upside down, read both: a page number corrected, which the counsel used in her next sentence without a hitch; and *Leave the fifth point. Don't need it.* The counsel turned that one face down.
   184	
   185	She finished with no flourish at all.
   186	
   187	"The petition asks this seat to measure a man with the one instrument that has never once been able to see him, and to call that measuring. Twenty-two officers who could see him have told you what they saw. You have their names. Ask them."
   188	
   189	She gave the rule its last quarter turn, and sat.
   190	
   191	---
   192	
   193	Ilsev was standing.
   194	
   195	The hood had read the ruling and the room had not yet worked out what it had heard, and in that gap Ilsev rose from the middle chair and asked leave, and had it, and did not open her files.
   196	
   197	Cael heard the hood's answer before he understood her question. *Its keeping to protocol. Not its result. Nor that.* Ilsev had asked what her seat was there to do, and whether that included deciding if the standard protocol could read this practitioner at all, and the seat had said no to the second. Now she was asking that its words be minuted exactly as spoken, so that nobody could stretch her chair afterward to cover the question. The clerk wrote. Ilsev sat, and wrote the same words into her own file in her own hand, and set the file back at its old angle.
   198	
   199	"She's fencing her chair," Karis whispered. "So they can't say later she sat on it."
   200	
   201	A chair whose only work was to watch how a re-evaluation was carried out. Then there was going to be one to watch.
   202	
   203	Cael went back to the ruling and made himself hear it again in his head. The hood had read it from its single leaf, in the same unhurried voice it had used for its one question; only now he heard it the other way about, from what the house had kept to what it had lost.
   204	
   205	*The house has observed its charter in every particular. Its record is entire, and stands untouched. The clause beneath the enrollment is in force; the standardizing directive did not reach it. The enrollment was made lawfully, and the seat so finds.*
   206	
   207	The correspondent's hat had gone onto the floor of the second bench just there, when he half rose to be first to the doors and knocked it off his own knee; Lira had caught Cael's sleeve; and the hood had read straight on, its voice and speed unchanged.
   208	
   209	*Argument on the petition to compel the enrollee's re-evaluation under the standard Arbiter protocol is set down for the sittings that remain, and the seat will rule on it before the academic year closes. The matter proceeds in full: how far the integrity sections reach a practitioner is for the registry to determine, and the seat determines that they reach this one far enough to be heard. On the question raised, the seat may take it up.*
   210	
   211	Karis's hands had gone flat on the table, and stayed there.
   212	
   213	Cael made himself say it the coach way, plainly, so that he would have it straight. What they had won, they had for good: no seat would ever again argue whether the clause was law or whether the house's paper was honest; that was settled under seal, in the minute, forever. What they had not won was the only thing that could still reach him. The sittings that were left would argue a single question, whether to stand him in a frame. And nobody in the room, not Jent, not Ilsev, not the four evaluators, not the hood, could pretend not to know what the frame would say.
   214	
   215	Withrow bowed to the platform at the registry's depth when the hood rose. Jent did not smile or gather his papers or once look across the floor.
   216	
   217	In the passage behind the selling floor, while the porters brought the cases down, Lira caught his sleeve again. She had lost the thread twice from the attendance bench.
   218	
   219	"So we won?"
   220	
   221	"Every point."
   222	
   223	"And he's going in a frame."
   224	
   225	"Probably."
   226	
   227	She stood with that in the fleece smell of the old sample passage. "Then it's like a bout," she said slowly, "where the judges give you every exchange and write down you won every one, and then tell you the bout goes on anyway, under rules they'll pick later." She let go of his sleeve. "That's both. That's what she wrote on the slip, isn't it."
   228	
   229	"I think so."
   230	
   231	"I hate it," said Lira, with deep satisfaction at having understood it, and went to help Brom with the second case.
   232	
   233	Withrow came through the passage a moment later, pulling on her gloves, on her way to her carriage. She did not stop. As she passed Cael she said, without turning her head, "Sit up straight in the coach. The town watches it," which was what she had said to him on the evening of the charter folder, and went on out to the steps.
   234	
   235	Through the far doorway Cael could see the records table. The officer with the careful face had stayed at it. He was squaring the minute-book to the table's edge with both hands, and then, when it was square, squaring it again.
   236	
   237	---
   238	
   239	Brom unfolded the slip at the first milestone past the town and held it up so that they could all read it.
   240	
   241	One word, in Karis's hand. *Both.*
   242	
   243	Karis was looking out of the window at the river, and did not look round.
   244	
   245	Then for a while everybody talked about the steps at once, and nobody agreed on what had happened first.
   246	
   247	Brom said a runner had gone into him on the second step, a boy with a satchel, who said sorry over his shoulder without stopping and was round the corner by the corn chandler's before Brom had his balance back; there had been three more behind him. Lira said four towns by tonight. Cael thought of the printer in the wharf street on the old press that did not smear, needing two headlines and having room for one.
   248	
   249	Karis said Jent had stopped by her, not the counsel.
   250	
   251	"He said, 'Advocate. The ninety-one. Did you count them yourself?'" She was still looking at the river. "I said every one. He said, 'I thought so,' and put on his hat, and walked off up the street as if he were going home to his dinner."
   252	
   253	"He'll give more away next time," said the counsel from her corner, writing. "Giving things away is how that man walks forward." She wrote one line more, and Cael read it upside down: *Valid: held. To proceed: held. Both.* She closed the minute-book on it.
   254	
   255	The river went by. Lira kept one hand on the closed drill-book and never lifted its cover. Brom put the slip back in his right inside pocket and left his hand over it.
   256	
   257	At the ferry the coach waited its turn on the landing, and Cael looked out at the two grey coats who stood there as they always stood, changing on the bell. One of them was not looking at the coach's door or at the faces in its windows. He was looking at the luggage rail on the roof, where the house's two document cases were strapped, the way a clerk looks at a line he means to copy.
   258	
   259	Nobody in the coach said anything about it. All of them had seen it.
   260	
   261	The ferry took them across. On the far landing, while the coach was hauled up the ramp, Karis turned from the window at last.
   262	
   263	"We won every point that can be won on paper."
   264	
   265	Brom took the time he always took. "And?"
   266	
   267	"And it's proceeding."
   268	
   269	Cael set the night's entry in his head as the wheels found the road. It came out at one line, and he did not try to make it longer.
   270	
   271	*Part of me is still keeping score. The rest of me has started counting doors.*
   272	
   273	Seln had not opened his eyes since the town. He did not open them now. When he spoke, it was to the whole coach, and not loudly.
   274	
   275	"Now you understand the file I've been reading my whole career."

