books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-18.md:1:# Chapter 18 — Daeva
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-18.md:41:"Then order the masts," Daeva said. "Order the setback at three rows and the barrier raised and the surface rated, and price the whole floor as though you were pricing me against myself, which is the only honest instrument your office owns. I'll sign anything the office needs signed and I'll sign it first." She said it without impatience, because the man was right about his own profession and she had never in her life mistaken a colleague's competence for an obstacle. "You have a real objection. It's the only one in this room. It is an engineering objection, it has an engineering answer, and by the end of the week your engineers will have built it."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-18.md:47:"Say it," Daeva said, without heat. "It's true. The program has invested eleven years in me. I'm aware of the size of the investment. I watched it being made from inside."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-18.md:55:"Then the objection is noted," Daeva said, in the program's own register, and thanked them for their care — sincerely, because they had built the ladder honestly; it was not their fault she had reached the top of it and found it standing in a garden — and went to her afternoon session and ran it flawlessly, because the curriculum held even when its subject had stopped being its product.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-18.md:81:"It's for the grounding runs," Daeva said. "Water finds the cable trench. If the trench takes water under discharge, four masts become one fault." She crouched, laid the back of her hand flat to the stone at the ring's centre, then again a full stride north, reading the fall the way he had read it, and stood. "Half an inch over twenty-six feet is nothing at all to stand on and everything to go down on. Whoever loses their feet in the north arc slides."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-18.md:87:They walked the gate breaks after that, both counting, because gate breaks were where bouts went wrong and both of them had files that said so. Four breaks in the raised barrier: champion's side, challenger's side, and two service gaps at the east and west quarters for the crews — each a seven-foot section of lower wall with a coping seam a fighter could feel through a boot at speed. Cael paced the breaks against the masts. Daeva paced hers against the ring's centre, which told him something about the shape of her thinking that no compiled dossier had, and he said so, and she asked what it told him, and he told her that too, exactly, without softening it. She considered the answer for the length of four strides and said it was correct and that she would be adjusting it before finals day, and neither of them thought that a strange thing for one of them to have said to the other two days out.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-18.md:97:"The one door they built me was a ladder," Daeva said, "and it only goes up." She said it the way she said everything — flat, exact, no bid for sympathy anywhere in it — and resumed the circuit, and Cael filed the exchange as the mirror completing its silvering: his deprivations and her endowments, arriving at the same room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-003.md:25:- **Karis's information audit** for the whole company: nothing tactical within thirty feet of a rail, letters through Bracken's pouch, "Decent and instrumental at once." Ephram's rehearsed entry was sold within a day.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-18.md:7:The records hall's long table had the delegation down one side and Halcenvane down the other. Delegation counsel at the head of her side with three boxes of her own and a clerk to fetch. Ilsev at her left, with her case shut on the table in front of her and her hands flat on it. Havel at the foot with the recording block and the routing tray, which was the same posture Cael had seen him hold in a Greyvane hearing room and, four years before that, at a corridor desk in Ardenmere: a man whose work was to be the place the paper went.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-18.md:47:Counsel did not argue that the assay clause had been repealed by name, because it had not been, and two independent searches sat in box two saying so — Karis's, performed over eleven weeks of a Greyvane winter, and one of Bracken's clerks', performed from scratch without access to Karis's notes, arriving at the same nothing by a different route.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-18.md:87:*Referral, senior evaluation seat, filed in the matter of the Greyvane adjudication of the preceding year: query as to originating authority for designation Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4, appearing in the priority field of subject file. Determination: no originating authority of record. Designation predates file creation. No action available at this clearance.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-18.md:137:The first entry was Ardenmere: a market square, an anomaly in a routing record, a question he had asked his supervising officer and been moved off. The second was a records anomaly in the same city eight months later, the same designation, the same absence of a source. The third was the Greyvane hearing: the first time anybody senior had seen it, and the first time he had understood that the thing was not his own inexperience.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-18.md:141:*Referral of the Greyvane matter, senior evaluation seat, returned this day. No originating authority of record. Designation predates file creation. No action available at this clearance. Referring officer filed again, escalated one level, same afternoon, grounds recorded.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-18.md:195:He had not predicted that and should have. A floor occupied his attention completely, which was one known hazard he had spent forty-nine days building a countermeasure for. A records session occupied it in long slow bands with gaps in them, and the gaps were where a boy's attention went off its own edges, and it went off them twice in the afternoon and once badly in the sixth hour, during a stretch of procedural argument about the collation clerk's attestation, when he came back to himself at the end of an unmeasured interval to find that the clerk two chairs down had put a folder on the empty chair beside him.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-18.md:265:*The provision held. Ilsev said it out loud, standing up, into a record, in front of the Compact's own counsel: the basis is sound law. Fifteen months, two academies, four boxes, three hundred and eleven documents, and a young woman who spent eleven weeks of a Greyvane winter proving that nothing had happened.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-09.md:31:"Every institution we have ever been inside failed us until text held it accountable. Denvash put him out a back door with no record. Ardenmere's circuit rated him because Vell kept a ledger. Greyvane's hearing found for him because the clause was in a compilation nobody had read. Halcenvane enrolled him because the record was true and Bracken wouldn't falsify it. Norhold fielded him on a sentence." She counted them on her fingers, the way she counted precedents. "Text is the only thing that has ever held. Not once — *every time.*" She set her hand flat on the face-down papers. "And the record of walking out mid-proceedings reads *fled.* It's the registry's word. I've read it in two hundred files. *Respondent fled the jurisdiction pending ruling* — and after it, in every file, the ruling goes in anyway, by default, and the default is the petition's whole prayer, granted, with a line beneath it that says the respondent's flight is *itself evidence of the integrity question.*" She sat down. "Leave, and we hand them the confession they couldn't get from Umber, from Vastin, from Daeva, from eleven pounds of the truth. That's the record. I'm the archive. I'm telling you what the archive says."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-09.md:79:"The hearing at Greyvane. The demonstration provision. Four semester stamps, renewed, on a Compact-observed record. An inspection that declined to find. A tournament their own clause put me in, and a match their own instrument couldn't score, and the instrument's keeper writing *no number goes here* rather than a lie." He said them in order, the way he wrote inventories, because the order was the argument. "Every time the people failed, the text held. Not because text is kind — because text is *there.* You can read it. You can find the orphaned clause. You can put a true record on a counter and make a registrar decline to falsify it. The people are weather. The text is ground."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-09.md:142:He had done the arithmetic of the postings once, in his twelfth year, and had not enjoyed it. Nineteen towns. Four academies, two guild halls, a district seat, three provincial registries, and the rest of it the kind of place that had no name in the reports and got a designation instead. Four hundred and ten reports filed. Sixty-three subjects. Of the sixty-three, he could name fifty-eight; of the fifty-eight, he knew what had happened to nine, and had stopped, deliberately, at nine, because the tenth had been a woman in a river district and he had gone looking and had found out, and the finding out had cost him a year of sleeping properly and had taught him the only professional lesson he had ever learned twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:22:- **Lira (cutaway, Ch51):** dawn, a hip that sets in two stages, Fenmark's hall in memory, and the north door. She cannot plan for his number and he cannot plan for hers; that was the point of nine days' silence. **Havel (cutaway):** a records officer off duty with nothing in his hands, choosing to be here.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:27:- **The evaluation, the twentieth (two chapters).** Holding from the fourth hour; Brom on the stair ("I'll know. That's the best I've got."); Lira at the wing gate ("Say it if it goes."). Four chairs on the east wall; **Vastin has nothing in his hands**; Havel's four-tenths-of-a-second greeting. Gault restates the three points and asks for any addition: "The ignition point… Second." **1:** displacement eight times; the ceiling restated at *six*, "That is what the provision is for." **2:** the thermal vessel, four instants, "a better instrument reading than the Greyvane exhibit supports"; counsel and Ilsev find their one thing; Vastin does not write. **The drift at ninety minutes**, inside an honest answer: "Magister. I need an interval." Logged, "fatigue affecting response quality." **3:** the combined trial, eleven of twelve. **4:** the plate, Pressure-adjacent withheld (C9); a flat line that Gault defends ("That is what a real semester looks like"). **5: the second detent fails** off the upper rack, early and heavier. He takes a quarter of the burst and *shuts it mid-payment*, once, unrepeatable; the load goes into the hip; "Because the clerk was standing where I'd have landed." Void; the reserve frame; eleven minutes on the rail. The thin second. **Vastin does not look twice.** Eight of nine on the re-run, the last two degraded and said aloud. **6:** cold reprise. **The note, verbatim. Renewed.** Vastin rises last; the notebook has come out twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:9:- Read before drafting: source `chapter-20.md` through `chapter-24.md`, and **Book 6 `chapter-01.md`** (the handoff). The previous ending is `manuscript/chapter-53.md`. From `BOOK_MAP.md`, read §1 (Ending), §2 items 37–41, §5 (Umber, Ilsev, Havel, Seln, Daeva, Vastin; the convening), §7.1 (M9 rows), §8 (Daeva), §9, §10 (the notice; items 16–18, 29–32, 53–59), §11 and §12 (C2, C3, C4, C6, C8, C12, C15). Also `STATE_LEDGER.md`.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:13:> **[Coordinator note 2026-10-05, after M8 review — what M9 inherits.]** (1) THE RING (canon): fifty-two by forty-eight; the oak round the highest point; a half-inch fall from it to the barrier's foot every way (a dome; the long way, north to south, is the longest road downhill); a dry trench under an iron grating, "a hand and a half", falling slightly toward the east break; four masts at the quarters, each grounded twice into the trench, crown-of-nails heads; six-foot barrier with a bull-nose cap; four breaks, each with a cap seam felt through a boot; three rows of seats gone. Specification from Auremont's risk office at the house's expense. (2) FINALS DAY'S CARD: the three bracket finals (Iron, Silver, Gold) and the team-trial final (Auremont v the river academy) — place the trial final on the morning card; the match is the true event after them. Halcenvane is third on the continent "as the count stands at the close of the trials; to be confirmed at the closing". (3) DAEVA: Storm Path from her own side — asking the air, leaning, the road, the crooked lane, "let the air go slack" (the coastal veteran at fifteen) — her vocabulary stays hers, apart from Karis's fold / *permitted* / "arrives third". She told Cael what the masts are rated to, at the west mast (M8 ch52). She never theorizes what he is. (4) BODIES at T24: Cael fit, the hip and right-hand burn healed; a dull hold behind the eyes from deep recall on T23; the medium read narrows attention (Lira, Brom unheard). Brom's LEFT shoulder sore from the third-place trial, forearms taped. Karis's palms pink. Lira fit (the left arm held close is a habit). Ephram hoarse. (5) THE BANK, as minuted in Hesk's book on T23: Wind past six ("the bill comes in the morning"); the push from the Bronze at Ardenmere at fighting load past four blows a sitting; Reydan's give on a scored floor before a crowd, not since the Ardenmere bout; Karis's spark past the two contacts allowed in public; the read as far down as it goes. Shadow sealed for the debt; Seln never told why. (6) Vastin resolved to stay for the figure this time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:25:- **The match (~14,000)** per BOOK_MAP §8, exchange by exchange. E1: the courtesy pass; the full public repertoire inside ninety seconds; Compression on a tournament floor for the first time, before a crowd for the first time since the Reydan bout at Ardenmere [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05: audit A3]; losing ground. E2: the fold read; early; the collapse point; **the score** ("nobody had scored on Daeva in four years"); her face; the courtesy ends. E3: braid; triage; the armed resolution point; the left side, shoulder to hip; the referee steps in and lets it go on; the healers stand and sit. E3 also carries the in-ring discovery of the medium read's Gold-scale price: the woman or the air, not both. The break's inventory, with the other instrument working underneath. E4: the suite fails in sequence with coordinates; the read does not; out of ground; the conditions complete; **the reach**, held as the line drawn the year before at Halcenvane in three numbered rules (B4 ch23's signed minute: conditions not manufactured; a directed acquisition only within earnest engagement the subject begins himself; the full cost written beforehand) — the circle's shorthand this book gives it is *directed, open-eyed, or not at all* [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05: replaces "drawn two years earlier"; audit B8]; his own lane laid crosswise, fifteen feet at a third of the depth; through permission; the spark; the silence in strata (tower stairs, betting ledgers turned face down, the relay man out of sentence); her recognition; **the destabilization** (Wind drops mid-step; "a question of ownership"); her mercy, on a clock; **Stoppage. Daeva.** The ritual: healers at a walk, name/day/exchange, the nine pens not moving, five people at the rail not running. **The notice, verbatim.** Two firsts counted. The hand up; "That was mine." / "A piece of it."; the promise. Vastin standing, leaving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:26:- **Daeva cutaway (~2,500):** split between the minutes after the match (what she saw; a lane that was hers, seeded by him; the delight; she must not name a mechanism) and the four seconds she breaks procession at the closing ceremony, with the citation she will never read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:28:- **The morning after:** the copying counter breaks in small ways; Auremont waits in line at its own request; the wrong theories (including "Daeva did it"); *our fighter does not mis-seed.* The log: "nobody guesses digestion."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:33:- **Placement:** the reverse-order liturgy and the eight-count clapping; Rhagen fourth; the honor mast; Lira's "what the scrapyard placed"; the banner transfer (the four ties in lawful order; a crowd on its feet for a countersignature); **Daeva breaks procession: "Rematch."** The year's ledger in Hesk's volume (C6): seven fragments with Storm's stability flag still standing, the anomaly, the Reydan line. The letters (Hesk's reply verbatim; Vell). Brom: "The recess's problem." [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05: the source's "Summer's problem" is a season word (#35); not a §10 line; owner may prefer other wording.] Lira at the rail: "Four flags and an exchange … Silver bracket. Next cycle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:38:Physical challenge: the match. Cutaways: Umber ≈3,500, Daeva ≈2,500, Ilsev ≈1,400, Havel ≈600, Seln ≈1,000, Vastin ≈1,000, plus the convening window, which counts toward neither share.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:40:Development: about 15 Cael beats. Supporting: Daeva (2), Umber (2), Vastin (2), Lira (2), Brom (2), Karis (2), Seln (1–2), Rooke (1), Withrow (1), Ephram (1), Ilsev and Havel (1 each), Hesk (1).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:45:- **The mechanism stays sealed:** no one outside Lira, Brom and Karis says or thinks *integration*, *fragment*, *acquisition* or *witnessed*. Daeva gets "A piece of it." Seln is not told. Ephram is not told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:9:- Read before drafting: source `chapter-16.md`, `chapter-17.md`, `chapter-18.md` and `chapter-19.md`. The previous ending is `manuscript/chapter-46.md`. From `BOOK_MAP.md`, read §2 items 33–36, §3 A (third place) and B 9 (reading the medium), §5 (Daeva, Vastin, Seln), §7.1 (M8 rows), §8 (third place), §10 items 13–14, 26–28, 47–52, 60, and §11–§12 (C1, C2, C13). Also `STATE_LEDGER.md`.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:17:The day after the team trial. The dossiers have a new final page: *survived Daeva's lane at trial speed.* No ranked fighter with a standing to protect will file against him now. Lira's shoulder is near its week's end, Cael's hip has healed, and the third-place trial is scheduled against Rhagen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:32:- **Daeva's window (≈7,000, cutaway):** thirteen, in a garden court; "am I early, or are you late?"; the ladder that came to her; "specimen"; four years without a doubtful outcome; the Gold Rank 6 coastal exhibition at fifteen and the gap the program closed; the Tuesday calibration [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05: OWNER item — B4 bans English weekday names at Halcenvane (First-day … Seventh-day) and keeps them at Greyvane, "the weekday scheme by house"; default: "Tuesday" survives only inside Daeva's own window as Auremont's word, never in Cael's narration or at Halcenvane]; the dossier read twice the night after the trial; the storm-rated glass that does not open; the filing before dawn, past the delegation head's door; the objection room, the risk officer's engineering objection answered with its own remedy, and her authority question; "the first decision of her entire career that nobody had scheduled for her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:37:Physical challenge: the third-place trial; the medium training; the ring walk. Cutaways: Daeva ≈7,000, Vastin ≈2,000, Seln ≈1,000.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:39:Development: about 15 Cael beats. Supporting: Lira (2), Brom (2), Karis (2), Seln (1), Ephram (1), Rooke (1–2), Withrow (1–2), Daeva (2–3), Umber (1), Vastin (1).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:43:- **Daeva:** Kindled at thirteen (B6 Ch15 quotes it; owner ruling C1). Six years since the garden court and seven in the program, not "eleven" (C13). She has the dossier's public contents and her trial memory only. She must not theorize what Cael is.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:53:Chapter boundaries, including where Daeva's window sits relative to the ring walk; the empty-board days' texture; the convening's room; third-place choreography inside the protected beats; the party's texture; the medium training's specifics; what the ring conversation covers beyond the protected exchange; the eve's room and its sounds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-18.md:25:*Daeva. Academy of Auremont. Storm Path; Gold, Rank Three. Entered on her own account at the seat at Ostrand, in the matter of Halcenvane's enrollee of record:*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-18.md:117:He found he liked her for that more than for anything. Every frame on the continent had read Daeva, early and exactly, and put her figures on every board, and arranged itself round her before she was old enough to see it happening; the machine had looked at her and loved what it saw. It had looked at him and seen a blank. He had always thought of those as opposites. She was telling him, from the far end of the same machine, that they were one thing seen from two sides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-18.md:125:Then he opened Hesk's book and wrote, because a thing that had been read four times needed to be put somewhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-18.md:131:Then he put it where it belonged, which was what *filed* had always meant in this book. He took the old volume down from the shelf over the sill, the full one, whose last line he had written on the ferry coming home from Norhold, and opened its front board. Behind it lay the letters he had kept: Hesk's reply, and Hesk's others, and Vell's from the ends of the years, in her ledger-keeper's hand. He slid Daeva's in among them. Hesk, who had made instruments all his life and trusted none he had not tested. Vell, who had kept honest figures in a cold yard when nobody paid her to. Daeva, whom every instrument on the continent had loved. Not one of the three had ever met another. He thought, squaring the letters against the spine, that they would get on, and that it would be frightening to be in the room when they did. He closed the board on them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-18.md:139:"Everybody at supper last night thought she'd taken your side," she said, before she had properly arrived at the rail. "The first-years. Half the cohort. *Daeva stands up for him.*" She leaned her elbows on the rail. "She didn't. That isn't what fifteen words on the seat's own paper is for. That's a card nailed to a wall, saying where she'll be standing when all this is over, so no office can ever say afterward it didn't know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-18.md:145:"And Daeva?"
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-04.md:79:The budget line was, in the academy's arithmetic, small — a research allocation for copying and courier and archive requests, the kind of sum a faculty member spent without noticing — and she spent the first of it on a request to Greyvane's archive for a certified copy of the founding-era compilation's oldest signatures, the ones Prynn had once said would be there when she was forty. The request went down the bluff road under the academy's seal, with a budget code on it, and Karis stood at the counter watching Bracken enter the code with the expression of a woman who had spent three institutions asking for things on her own account and had just, for the first time, asked on an institution's.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-04.md:139:The coach's council on the river road ran the session the way the delegation ran everything, in order of arrival. The counsel first: "Procedurally immaculate. He entered nothing he didn't need. I entered everything we had. The seat received both as if they weighed the same." Karis: "They do weigh the same. To the seat." Brom, after the length of time Brom took: "Havel didn't look at you." Cael: "No." Brom: "He looked at you at Greyvane. Twice, from the delegation gallery. I counted." Lira, to the window: "So the junior's stopped learning too." And Seln, from the corner, eyes closed, delivering the session's only finding in the register the table weighed at full freight: "The records officer at the senior chair is district grade. The junior behind him is monitoring grade, on rotation. The seat is registry-seat grade — the seal's grade, not a person's. Three grades of officer in one room and not one of them is the grade that decides anything. The office notes it." He did not open his eyes. "The office has sat in rooms like that. From the other chair."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-04.md:143:*The hearing at Greyvane had Coss's face on it — you could read the whole case off him from across the room. The inspection had Vastin's; the evaluation was the man. This has a seal. A registrar of no age and no face reading a designation off a clerk's card, and a procurator with nothing on him, and a room conducting itself. I keep looking for the person and finding the hand.*
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-09.md:13:Daeva.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-09.md:21:*Daeva, first read: too little data. Which is itself data. I have read every fighter I've ever seen at this range — stance, weight, tells, the aim of their attention. She has either none of the ordinary surfaces or manages them past my range. Both possibilities are the same finding: whatever the instruments say about her, the instruments are not why she's dangerous. File open. Primary data at the procession's end: as the delegations dispersed, she looked at exactly one thing that was not architecture. It was our file. It was brief, and it was not curiosity. It was inventory.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:13:It is the last night of a twelve-week recess at Halcenvane, on the bluff above Ostrand [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05: no season word (#35); "the recess", weather only; Halcenvane's days are First-day … Seventh-day]. Book 4 closed on the bluff wall at dusk on the last day of term (edition ch62): "I have six things that aren't a Path… A semester stamp that says I proved it. And four people who know what it costs."; the belief about the file note said as a belief ("the most careful man I've ever met wrote that I'm not an error" / "You're not." / "Never were." / Karis: "Noted."); and the last line, *The bluff held. The stamp was real.* The teaching assistant took the evening air sixty feet along the coping and walked off before Cael spoke — "Nobody waved him over… Nobody watched him go." [B4-reconciled: replaces the source's "which — from him — was agreement", which the edition does not use.] Since then Cael has trained sixty-one sessions and the Wind ceiling held at six bursts free on the sprung oak. Hesk has written nine letters (his last of B4: "Sixteen suits you."). The watchers are two and two, the ferry landing and the road's foot, changing on the bell every four hours, not one man added (Karis's marbled log). Seln's name stood against every working day of the wing's recess counter sheet. Karis spent ten weeks building the argument around a clause she banked with one word (sixth part, subsection four; *Interesting.*), her Greyvane notebooks in the named top-floor reading room. Brom's recess sheet: "Nineteen. Round three. Four a week." Ephram's sheet for supervised floor time "next term" is on Rooke's desk. Gault's calendar has next term's half-yearly evaluation ruled in, "measured against: baseline (entry), and the note of the twentieth of Reaping". Bracken has certified three hundred season sheets across the recess. Cael is sixteen and says so. Open in the log, at the window, then go to convocation.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-09.md:27:"Greyvane, last year, week nine. We ran twenty-two controlled sessions. I declared at demonstration intensity, sustained, under direct observation, with Cael reaching deliberately every time. Twenty-two nulls. I wrote at the time that we were missing a condition, and I was right, and I did not know what it was." She turned a page without looking down at it. "We found out in the match. Sustained observation *plus genuine stakes*. That's the model. Two terms, and the second one is the one everybody gets wrong, because everybody assumes stakes means danger. It doesn't. The Ember-adjacent didn't complete because I was hurting him. It completed because I was *trying*, at full economy, with something real on the outcome, against him, and he had spent six weeks watching me do exactly that."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-09.md:151:"I know I did." He had gone back and read it, in fact, three nights ago: the Greyvane entry, the one written the morning after the match, and the way its sentences arranged themselves around a subject who had been overtaken by his own architecture in a moment of genuine peril. Every word of it had been true. The arrangement had been a lie, and he had not noticed at the time, and had noticed now, at fifteen, sitting at a Halcenvane library carrel in the rain, which was the least comfortable and most useful thing that had happened to him that month.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-09.md:221:He wrote the entry that night in the Power Log, and it ran longer than any entry he had written since Greyvane, and he did not cut it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-19.md:17:"A provincial registry," said Seln. "The one that keeps the Velmere estate's file. Nine days ago. A query on the standing of the household, the tiers of the people in it, and the estate's registered relationships."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-19.md:35:"Then they've read the bottom of it." Lira said it lightly, and the lightness did not hold. "At Fenmark the examiner wiped his slate with his sleeve so the form would be clean. He kept the form. Everything anybody's written about me since, the Greyvane line, the Iron line at the Ostrand station, all of it went down on top of that form, and none of it rubbed the form out, because the registry never rubs anything out. It just writes on top." She pressed her thumb on the board. "Four years, near enough, of good people writing over it. And somebody with a reason has gone to the bottom of the pile on an ordinary afternoon to see what was there first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-19.md:45:"That's mine," she said. "I know which one it is, because the keeper of the archive at Ternhall wrote to tell me. Privately, this morning, four lines. A district clerk came to her counter six days ago with a sealed request for my transfer file. My old standing. My privileges. The training log from the paired floor." She kept two fingers on the letter, as if it might blow away. "On the Third-day a fee office wanted to know who I was before it would send a request of mine to Greyvane. Now somebody wants to know who I used to be." She was quiet a moment. "That's twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-19.md:157:"Write it." The fourth bean went into the pocket. "Give it to the registrar for his pouch, not to the town post. The pouch is locked in Ostrand and opened here, and I open it." He put the fifth bean away. "Letters from a house under inventory are read. Not by anyone wicked. By a clerk, for completeness, who will copy a line of it into a file. Don't give them a line." He stood, and turned his empty cup a quarter-turn on the board, and left it there. "Tell him the stove sounds well. Tell him nothing else he wouldn't want read aloud in a provincial counting-room. He'll understand. He's been minding shapes longer than either of us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-19.md:223:He signed it with the one letter Hesk signed his with, and sealed it, and in the morning he gave it to Bracken for the pouch, and Bracken took it without a word and put it in the pouch himself.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-19.md:5:Cael counted the room in on arrival, because he counted every room in and had never once found the habit wasted. Four file cases along the north wall with the term's whole defense inside them, eleven pounds of true record, closed. The shuttered window on the quadrangle, and past the shutter a campus with nine days of term left going about its evening in complete ignorance. Bracken's spare inkstand on the sideboard, where Bracken kept it and where nobody else was permitted to move it. And in front of his own chair, the ruling's copy: four paragraphs, a seal, and the attestation line at the foot that every binding instrument on the continent closed with, which he had read ten thousand times in Karis's citations without ever once hearing it as a sentence about a hand.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-19.md:9:"Three roads. I'll say all three plainly and then we price them." He had no papers; the papers were spent; he had the ruling's copy and nothing else. "One: submit. Stand in front of the Arbiter in five weeks, be found unclassifiable — because I am, that's the founding fact — and hand the machinery the architecture it has waited two hundred years to file. The re-evaluation isn't a test I might pass. It's a delivery. I'd be walking myself back under the layer that couldn't finish the job the day I Kindled, and this time it has five weeks to prepare and no assay clause in the way." He let it sit. "Two: refuse inside the walls. Decline the re-evaluation, stay enrolled until they revoke it, be expelled on the machine's verb, the machine's record — *respondent refused compelled evaluation* — asset-restriction executing on schedule, Halcenvane bleeding for having harbored me, everyone at this table's file marked for association. That's the machine getting its expulsion and its confession in one. Three." He looked around the table. "Leave. Resign — before the re-evaluation date, before the revocation, before any of it executes. Convert their expulsion into our refusal. Cross the one line the registry cannot follow, because past it there's no registry to execute in."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-19.md:21:"Two is the same bill with an extra line." Karis did not need the chapter for that one. "Refusal inside the walls is a status the code has a word for, and the word attaches to the institution as well as the person. Halcenvane becomes an academy that harbored a respondent through a refused compelled evaluation. Withrow's charter goes to review. Bracken's counter gets audited by people who will find nothing and take eight months to find it. Every enrollee at this table picks up a file entry reading *association, active non-compliance proceedings*, which is a sentence that follows a certification around for thirty years and does its work quietly at every counter they ever stand at." She set the volume down. "The machine would prefer road two. It gets the expulsion, the record, the verb, and four files marked, and it gets all of it without having to write a single dishonest word."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-19.md:31:So Karis argued for it, against herself, for four minutes, at full strength, the way she had argued the Greyvane hearing's weakest branch to its end before discarding it. The cache went to the proceedings; the proceedings were compelled to receive it; the record entered two hundred years of reclassification orders against their own Kindling files. And then the room asked the only question that mattered, which was who verifies it — and the answer was an evaluation seat, and an evaluation seat runs on an Arbiter, and the Arbiter runs the protocol the documents accuse. "You would be asking the machine to certify a finding against a modification inside its own certifying function," she said. "There is no seat on this continent that can receive that finding and no instrument that can test it. It is not that we would lose the argument. It is that the argument has no venue. The only thing entering the cache accomplishes is telling the Compact exactly what an assessment clerk of fifteen years kept in a locked case, at which point there is one man alive who can be found and corrected, and they will find him, because finding him is the one part of the whole apparatus that works perfectly."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-19.md:77:The advancement was never answered. No signature, no refusal filed, no covering letter to the district seat; the form stayed on the table face-up as its own reply and was still there in the morning when the hall was opened, and somebody on the staff, not knowing what it was, squared it with the other papers and left it. The second Velmere envelope, unopened four weeks on the small table in his room, was answered the same way and in the same act, and it stayed unopened, because he did not open it, and the three boxes were the letter back.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-19.md:93:Cael made her price it anyway, and she permitted it, and did it in the order a bibliography goes in rather than the order a heart does. The Iron-tier Rank 3, which was a certification and would survive; the *research standing*, which was not a certification and would not; the archive privileges; the annex allocation with the lock on the door, the first door in her life that had locked on her work and not against it; the budget code Bracken had entered under her name at a counter while she watched. And the request to Greyvane's archive for the founding-era compilation's oldest signatures, sent under that code, filling in three weeks, due before midwinter, addressed to an academy she would not be at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-16.md:39:Lira came up the wall stair with two apples and gave him one without asking. She had her wraps still round her wrists, and her hair was dark with sweat from the Stone's afternoon hour, which she did not usually take; when she leaned on the wall her left calf stood out hard as a rope, and her burst hand kept flexing on its own, as if it were still counting. That morning Rooke had pinned the card for the program's first road meet on the east hall's door, with her name on it in his square capitals among the program's seniors. Ephram said she had read it four times on her way past without once breaking her stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-16.md:135:It was a printed form from the district fee office at Ostrand, which put its seal on every request a chartered house sent to an archive outside its own district. This one concerned her request of the thirty-fifth day, to Greyvane, for certified copies of the founders' signature leaves. The fee was paid and the request in order, but before the fee office would seal it on, it asked the requester to confirm on the back of the form the research standing under which the request was made, the date that standing had last been renewed, and the name of the officer who renewed it. It cited the section that let it ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-16.md:147:"A Mire girl from the north stair stopped me on the covered walk after supper. I hardly know her. She'd heard I work up here, and she wanted to know what the enrollee was really like. *Really*. She said it like that." The girl swallowed. "So I told her. That he's enrolled under a lawful provision, adjudicated at Greyvane on the public record, under seal, with the grant and two renewals on paper. That the Compact's own inspection looked at the provision and it stood. That the challenge doesn't allege any fraud, and the petitioner's conceded the provision is valid. That the next sitting is on the Fifth-day. I gave her the paragraph numbers. I gave her the ninety-one." She stopped for breath. "It went on about a quarter of an hour. She kept looking at the stair. At the end she said *thank you* in a very small voice and went away." Her face went redder. "I think I was boring. I think I was extremely boring. I'm sorry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-16.md:173:He did it at the window that night, at the back of Hesk's book, where he had ruled the column at the month's close with four names at its head, his own last, and had hoped to leave it empty.
