editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-001/compiled-prompt.md:521:It was dated four days before. Hesk had not had his six pages. He could not have; they were still somewhere on the hill road, in Bracken's pouch. He had written off the broadsides, which reached the coast faster than any pouch. Then he had worked out which road the wagons would take, and which crossroads they would come to on which day, and sent the letter to meet them there. He had been right about all of it. Hesk's answers had always set out before the questions reached him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-001/compiled-prompt.md:553:On the ferry he wrote in the old volume for the last time, at the foot of its last leaf, until the line ran into the binding and there was no room for a word more. He shut it and put it inside his coat with Hesk's letter. Everything he wrote from now on would go into Hesk's book, and he thought it would be a long while before Hesk's book was full. There had been no letter from Fiske all year. Lira had said she was not counting the days, and had not, and would be in the Silver bracket next cycle whether Fiske watched it or not.
