editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:8:- Owner names: no new names. Use Daeva, Umber, Zerin, Marek, Ivenne, Ilsev, Havel and Vastin. The Concourse steward and the boy with the likeness stay unnamed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:9:- Read before drafting: source `chapter-08.md` and `chapter-09.md`. The previous ending is `manuscript/chapter-26.md`. From `BOOK_MAP.md`, read §2 items 25–26, §3 A (road sparring; opening rounds) and E 5–6, §5 (Umber, Vastin, Havel), §6 (calendar), §10 items 11–12, 14, 17 (the Daeva first-read log), 40, and §11–§12 (C4, C14). Also `STATE_LEDGER.md`.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:15:The delegation is in its first week on the seventeen-day road to Norhold, two wagons running downhill with the melt [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05: "spring" dropped, #35]. Rooke, Gault, Seln and the reserves travel; Withrow and Bracken will catch up. Cael charts the road as an education. The draw has not posted. Nobody at Halcenvane has seen Daeva closer than a woodcut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:19:Make the reader arrive. First the road, then the city, the mark sealed in public, the brackets beginning, and the one empty chair. The tournament's scale should be felt as physics: eight thousand seats, fourteen delegations, the champion's gate. So should its fairness, which Umber verifies in front of everyone. Daeva is held at a hundred metres.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:32:- **The opening procession:** eleventh of fourteen, last in the file, unbracketed. The crowd's specific sound. **Daeva at a hundred metres;** the first-read log verbatim. **Umber locks the Standard;** the attestation verbatim; "They cheer the banner. They *trust* the seal."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-005.md:45:- Daeva stays at distance: no conversation and no window yet. The first read is all he gets.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-17.md:17:Cael went to his chalk at the south end. He did not take Hesk's book out of his coat. The line had been written at the sill the night before, and he had it by heart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-17.md:165:She had a courtesy copy from Norhold in front of her, and one sheet of her own beside it, and the look of someone who has spent two hours behind a shut door. The west tower's adjudication office had sent its whole referred file on to the proceedings that had claimed the referral: the record of the match; the nine panel charts; and on a sheet by itself the entry Cael had last seen hung on a nail by the north tunnel with half a city reading it over his shoulder. *Result: stoppage, Daeva, fourth exchange. Performance rating: unscorable under standard. Referred.* On top lay a covering note of three sentences, signed by the Chief Adjudicator in his own hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-17.md:193:It was the house's exhibits, all of them, read into the minute by the seat's clerk in the order the counsel had asked, each with its number, title, date, where it came from and its line of receipt. The clerk had one pace and kept it for everything. A short item, eight or nine seconds; one with a certifying officer's line to recite, twenty or a little more. Bracken's index to the enrollment file, with Bracken's name in its certifying line: fourteen strokes' worth. The Greyvane ruling, the hearing that had kept Cael on his feet in a city he had never wanted to see: twenty-one. Karis's renewals table, rebuilt in a single silent hour with two dates on every row: eleven. The integrity section, forty pages, a night without sleep, ninety-one invocations each traced by hand to its own page: eight seconds. Its title had taken longer than its reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-20.md:77:He wrote it in Hesk's book at the sill before he slept. *The only honest measurement anybody has taken of me since Norhold was taken tonight, by Karis, on a closed floor, under two lamps. It will never be entered anywhere. Lira found the lane by its shadow. That will be entered nowhere either, and it's the best thing anybody learned on this hill this week.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-20.md:83:She had them in a twist of paper in her coat pocket, every curl of them, so that not one should lie on the wool exchange's floor, and she was reading the topmost of her files with the patience of somebody who has read it three times and means to read it once more before anybody asks her a question. Cael watched her do it from the house's table. Her files lay on her lap-desk at the angle he would have known anywhere, never square to the edge and never a hair different from the last time: a counter at Greyvane, the east wall of a room on the bluff, the first seat of the Compact's row high over the Concourse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-20.md:99:The hood received Daeva's statement. Jent did not stir. The counsel did not stir. There was nothing anyone on that floor could say, because there was nowhere in the schedule to say it. The clerk laid the sheet on the pile of things received.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-20.md:163:The names came first, and they were why the evaluators had looked up. The counsel told the hood how much evaluating this practitioner had already had: the grant at Greyvane and its two renewals on paper, a season of bouts under a regional registrar's seal and the rating instrument the whole continent used, a tournament entered under a clause of its own charter, and three days of the Compact's own senior evaluator on the bluff with his instruments, after which he had gone home without writing one finding against the boy. More looking, she said, than most practitioners get in a working life; and all of it done by people who could see, sealed and minuted, across three years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-20.md:185:The shelf came third and went quickly: the Greyvane ruling, and the four older rulings behind it, four houses that had gone to law against this clause with money and lawyers and lost, every one of them on the text. Tried by everybody who could afford to try it, she said, and it had held each time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-20.md:215:Cael understood her before he had finished understanding the ruling, because he had watched her do the same thing at Greyvane. A reader of texts was marking the edge of a text in ink, so that nobody could ever say afterward it had said more than it said. In her own dialect, which was the only one she spoke, she had let the whole floor know that her chair would vouch for the way a re-evaluation was run, and had been given no voice at all in whether there should be one. A re-evaluation, then. There was going to be one to conduct.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-19.md:3:The night before had a form now. It had been invented at Greyvane, the eve of a sanctioned bout that had frightened all of them more than they said — gifts, that time; small objects laid on a table by people who did not have the words yet. It had run again before the ladder's witnessed final — strategy, that time; a calibration council with the stakes on the table and the words arrived. Tonight it convened for the third time in its history, in the guesting-house's small room with the shutters closed on the harbor, and the delegation came to it knowing that neither of the old forms fit, because the calibration problem had inverted: for the first time in the found family's life, the question was not how to keep Cael inside the band.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-19.md:7:The study session ran first — the guesting-house's small room, the shutters closed on the harbor, the wall cleared for charts, the whole inner circle plus nobody, the door answered once (Ephram, with food, who handed it in, said "Eyes tomorrow, all of you," and left, the cohort's blessing in four words) — and it ran honest about its limits, which took discipline, because the limits were nearly total. There was no film on Gold. Auremont litigated its students' bout records into proprietary silence — the program's standard practice, enforced by counsel, upheld twice at continental arbitration; even the team trial's official record held only the scoring ledgers, not the panel charting. Storm Path documentation was registry-sealed at the Path's own request, a forty-practitioner rarity guarding its mechanics the way guilds guarded processes. The commercial dossiers on Daeva were, Karis had verified with professional disgust, compiled almost entirely from ceremony: records, thresholds, woodcut-grade description, the storm rendered in adjectives. The primary data on the continent's most-watched practitioner amounted to: one exchange of the team trial, charted live by the compound gaze at survival intensity; Cael's gallery notes from her four bracket-adjacent appearances; and one perimeter conversation at an empty ring, in which she had answered a question about her own ceiling because nobody had ever taught her she shouldn't.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-19.md:49:And Lira's was the third issue of the line she had first spoken outside a Greyvane gate and reissued on the eve of the ladder's final, now liturgy, the found family's own old text, and she delivered it the way liturgy is delivered, plainly, at the door, last:
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-19.md:59:Cael laid the arithmetic on the table because the arithmetic had already been laid on all of them by a season: surviving Gold required everything. The Shield captain had priced Silver at a handspan twice — with the suite constrained to documented rates. Daeva's lane, at trial traffic, had cost the full public repertoire spent openly just to vacate ground. A filed bout at her full intent, against the banking doctrine's public-suite-only discipline, was not a fight; it was a stoppage with preliminaries. The doctrine that had governed every public act of his life since Vell's circuit — show only what the record holds; bank the rest — had reached the fight it could not survive.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md:98:*Adversarial, earnest — non-hostile.* Every entry in the log was adversarial; the stakes rule demanded it and had demanded it since a boy in a market town first understood what the architecture would and would not accept. But the engagement field had never carried *non-hostile* in eight notices — not from Reydan, who had fought him to end him and then wanted the answer afterward; not from Daeva, who had fought him with a Gold-tier's whole seriousness and no malice at all and had still, on the day, been trying to take him apart. This was a machine's grammar bending to describe a fight that was entirely in earnest and entirely without enmity, which was as close as the architecture had ever come to writing down the word friendship, and could not, and had reached for the nearest thing in its vocabulary and found a negative.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-14.md:65:Four times before this, in forty years, he had sent a finding to a proceeding that had not called him. A district that had challenged a house's enrollment, a dozen years back: his finding had gone in against the district's own petition, and the seat had weighed it and the district had lost, and the district had written to him afterward in perfectly good temper to say so. A hill station's frame that read high: the frame had come out of its housing on his evidence. A guild hall. A sponsorship quarrel so old he could barely recall the guild. Four times offered. Four times taken in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-14.md:151:He went to the first sitting's minute, which was still in the grey-taped bundle, and laid it beside the denial. Five days ago, at the wool exchange, the five chairs of the evaluation seat had sat under grey covers, and the minute said *to be called*. This morning the same seat was *fully constituted*. Somewhere in the four days between that sitting and this refusal, then, somebody had filled five chairs. He knew every senior evaluator in that district by name and grade. Not one of them had written to him for the boy's file, which any of them would have needed before they sat; and none of the five chairs had been offered to the one officer who had already sat across a table from the boy with an instrument.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-14.md:163:Then he struck it through, corner to corner, so that it could still be read underneath, and on a clean sheet he wrote the refusal again, longer, giving the station the section it had failed to meet and the one officer it might apply to instead. It took him a quarter of an hour where the old line would have taken a quarter of a minute. He signed it with his name entire and put it in *done*.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-19.md:29:She had lost the crown nine days ago in a semifinal that had taken four exchanges and one very long minute, and had lost it to the woman about to fight for it, and the brass mark had stayed beside her name on the standings sheet all that time because the mark did not move until the final moved it. In about forty minutes a circlet of hammered copper that had been on her shelf for two years was going to be handed to somebody else in front of eleven hundred people.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-19.md:49:Lira's leading hip was nine days old. She had not favoured it in the covered walk that morning and she was not favouring it now, which meant nothing, because Lira could conceal a limp the way other people concealed a yawn; what could not be concealed was the way she set her feet at the mark. She set the leading one first and let the weight arrive onto it in two stages instead of one. Two stages. He had watched her set her feet at a mark ninety times this season and it had been one stage every time.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-19.md:55:Because that was the whole bout, and it had been the whole bout since the pairings resolved, and both of them had refused to train for nine days precisely so that neither could pretend not to know it.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-19.md:57:Brom's hardening ran out at about four minutes under repeat contact. Cael had that in the notebook in Brom's own words from a Greyvane winter and had confirmed it eleven times since — not a collapse, a fade: the skin stayed hard and the *cost* of keeping it hard went up until, somewhere past four minutes, every other thing he could do got slower to pay for it.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-19.md:61:One of those clocks was going to run out first, and both fighters knew both numbers, and neither of them had been permitted to build a plan around the other's number, because for nine days they had refused to speak about it.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-19.md:63:*That's what she meant,* Cael thought, watching them take their marks. *Nine days of not talking wasn't sportsmanship. It was so that neither of them could plan the other's injury. They've each got to fight the fighter, not the ledger.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-19.md:123:"She'll get a third. The third's expensive and it's available." Cael did not take his eyes off the floor. "The fourth is the one she told him about in hall one nine days ago. That's the only number in this bout that isn't a number."
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-19.md:263:*Hers: four bursts. Four held half-breaths, four landing beats. Nine days of a hip that was already hurt and four minutes of taking contact on a guard that isn't built for it — she walked backward around that floor twice on purpose, to spend his clock, which is the single most disciplined thing I have ever watched her do and the least like her. And the fourth burst she took on the injury deliberately, so the leg would fold, so the fold would move her sideways during the beat she can't move in. She used a wound as a mechanism. I don't have a column for that. I'm going to have to build one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/B4-RECONCILIATION-AUDIT.md:14:Also note: the B5 map's own drafting gate ("do not draft until the owner has ruled on C1, C2, C3", BOOK_MAP.md:447) is already satisfied by **defaults in force**: OWNER-DECISIONS #16 (Daeva at thirteen as a rare exception), #17 ("forty"; age never stated), #18 ("since his Kindling", no number). §12 should cite them rather than read as open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/B4-RECONCILIATION-AUDIT.md:52:- **B4 canon:** no season is named at Halcenvane (B4 `STATE_LEDGER.md:148` "No season named at Halcenvane", `:589`, `:734` "No month order and no Sowing→Reaping count is stated (#35)"); B4 `STATE_LEDGER.md:14` "do not tie Sowing or Reaping to a named season"; OWNER-DECISIONS #35 default (never state month order) and #36.4 (owner may later rule the series calendar). B4's only anchors cut the other way: `chapter-01.md:105` the Greyvane recess "on the frozen yard" (winter) + 22-week term + ≈27 weeks on the bluff + twelve-week recess lands B5's convocation nearer spring than autumn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/B4-RECONCILIATION-AUDIT.md:84:- **B4:** acquired d149/150; `chapter-37.md:213` "*Eighteen days old*" at the eleventh of Sowing (d167); ≈ seven weeks old at the B4 close, not sixteen. By B5 Ch14 ≈ a year. "Spring" is a season word (see A6).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/B4-RECONCILIATION-AUDIT.md:99:- **B4:** Ember is on Greyvane's record in one thin exhibit only (`chapter-43.md:219`); the wing has never measured it (ch53 draft:281 "No instrument in the wing had measured it"); the M8 plan measures it on the thermal vessel at **four instants** (B4 `packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:27`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/B4-RECONCILIATION-AUDIT.md:140:**Confirmed consistent (no action):** Bracken "he" throughout B5 (#7); Vastin no stated age (#8, #17); Gwen/Abbot only referenced as B4's pending placeholders (#11); Daeva thirteen (#16); "since his Kindling" rule honoured in packets (#18); the Shadow notice text (`chapter-34.md:140–145`) matches B4 §10 exactly and B5 does not requote it; Ilsev's Halcenvane filing is her **second** referral, eleven months after Greyvane (`chapter-49.md:31, :131`) and B5 calls it the second everywhere; Havel's four entries and `chapter-49.md:137` line are not misquoted in B5; Lira Iron Rank One at the Ostrand station on demonstration and record; the anomaly's three words; Fiske's "one signature that isn't mine" (`chapter-29.md:155`) is compatible with Gault filing for Lira with Ilsev witnessing; OWNER #10 (file note as inference) is carried by B5 §1 and C7's cross-reference.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-15.md:59:Only after that did Cael read the titles, and by then they were only what the money bought. Six of them, in Ostrand alone. The Greyvane hearing, printed whole from the shorthand and still costing six marks in its fourth edition. A three-mark pamphlet, *The Instrument in Full*, whose first page was right to the letter and whose second went wrong two lines down, as if the copyist had been let have one look. The print the girl had bought twice, at a copper and a half: a boy with a jaw like a spade standing in a way nobody who had ever fought would stand. And the two weeklies themselves, about nothing in the world but the proceedings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-15.md:77:"The seat refused him this morning," said the counsel. "At Ostrand. The Archmarshal's petition came to the seat by courier yesterday; he asked for standing to put an evaluator's finding before it, signed and sealed as his own. The refusal went out by seat courier this morning. Wherever the Archmarshal keeps his desk, it's a long road from the river, and he won't have it before tomorrow at the soonest." She put her bag down on the visitor's chair, but did not sit. "We have it tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-15.md:83:Cael stood in the middle of the carpet and did the sum, because he could not stop himself. The refusal had been written at Ostrand in the morning. At noon it had been a joke at an inn. By dusk it had come up the bluff in a chaise. By supper four people at a long table would have it word for word. And somewhere on a road, at this hour, a courier was riding with those same two sentences in a pouch toward the one man in the world they were written to, who did not know yet that he had been answered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r2/chapter-15.md:91:Then, in her own voice: "That's form thirty. Refusals, third series. They give you the drafting book in your second year at the registry, and you learn the refusals by heart before they let you near a counter." She took one hand off the desk. "I sent that line out more times than I could count. And when a junior came to me who couldn't say no to anybody, it was the first one I taught him. I told him it was the decent way. It doesn't claim to have weighed what it hasn't. It doesn't argue. It leaves the man on the other side of the counter nothing to be angry at." She looked at the lamp. "I called it a mercy. I taught it as a mercy to every junior I ever had. I'll be some while tonight working out what I was teaching them."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-02.md:3:The instrument reached Karis's room at the fourth bell of the afternoon, in Bracken's own hand, and she read it standing in the doorway the way Withrow had read it standing at the counter — the first page, then the whole, then the first page again — and said, when she had finished, the only sentence she would spend on feeling before the work began: "They've been reading us." Then she sent her assistant for the registry code, all three volumes, and the Greyvane transcript, and the four prior assay-challenge rulings Bracken kept in the records hall's locked press, and told the assistant to bring tea and not to expect to be sent home before the second bell, which was a lie by three bells and the assistant would tell it as a war story for years.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-02.md:37:She read the operative clause twice, aloud, and then laid the Greyvane hearing's ruling beside it — the adjudication that had made Cael, at fifteen, a person the law was forced to describe accurately — and traced with one finger the exact perimeter the instrument had drawn around it. "They don't touch the execution. Not the enrollment's filing, not the assay's conduct, not the semester renewals. Every fraud theory the broadsides have printed for a year — the concealed classification, the staged provision — they've declined all of it." She looked up. "Greyvane closed that door and they've read the closing. This is a document written by people who lose no arguments they can avoid having."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-02.md:45:That took longer, because the counsel wanted it checked against every prior challenge to an assay-provision enrollment in the registry's history — there were four; Bracken had them in the locked press, bound, because Bracken kept things — and the table read all four before midnight. A coastal academy's enrollment of a late-Kindled fisherman's son, challenged on procedure, sustained on the text, sixty years gone. A guild-house apprentice, challenged on sponsorship, sustained. Two more, older, thinner, both sustained — the provision's whole litigated history a short shelf of institutions losing to a clause they had failed to read. "Four challenges," the counsel said, closing the last. "Four losses. Every one of them attacked the text, and the text held. Your Greyvane hearing was the fifth, and it held the same way." She turned back to the instrument. "And this — this has been drafted by someone who read the same four and declined to be the sixth."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-02.md:47:The finding, when it landed, landed as procedure. "The clause's validity is conceded," the counsel said, at the end of an hour, in the voice of an advocate who had expected a fight and found the ground already ceded. "Explicitly. Paragraph six. *The petitioner does not contest the text of the provision under which the enrollment was executed, nor its lawful survival of the standardization directive.* They've handed you your whole Greyvane case as a stipulation." She set the page down. "I have been doing this for twenty-six years and I have never had an opponent concede my strongest ground in their opening instrument. It is either very generous or very frightening."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-02.md:145:"Every time the *people* failed," Lira said. She said it carefully, the drill-book still open on her knee, a woman testing a floor's footing before committing weight. "That's your sentence. You've said it since Greyvane. The text holds when the people fail." She looked at the embers. "What if the people didn't fail this time? What if they read the text better than we did and wrote a new one?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/packets/MOVEMENT-004.md:22:- **Karis's formal session** (minuted, two hours). Her Greyvane model is twenty-two nulls, then sustained observation plus genuine stakes, and *stakes means earnest engagement, not violence*. Seln's columns are all fuller. "The conditions are met… Which is why we have to talk about whether you may." Cael's first honest move: "You want it." / "Yes." / "Good. Say that part out loud again in about an hour when we're all being clever."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-14.md:133:*Eight confirmed fragments. Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — Feryn. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — source protected, deployment none, ever, anywhere; the seal's third year begins. Storm-adjacent — Daeva; settled, resettled twice this week around the new neighbor, holding. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; two days integrated; idle state learned; the doorways have stopped raising their hands, and I have entered in the deployment notes the first honest sentence about it: I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people, taken from a hand with no face, and I have not yet decided what it is for, and the not-deciding is entered as a decision.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:342:- **The schedule beside the final.** Copper final on the nineteenth of Reaping; observation reserved for the twentieth; the overlapping week. The rhyme with the Greyvane notice board. Log: "Coss I could read in a week…" and three men giving one instruction: hold the ordinary.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:344:- **No sparring for nine days.** Lira racks the batons: "I've beaten you nine times in two years and you've beaten me eleven… neither of us fights chess." Cael's sentence: "two Iron-equivalent practitioners who have never been allowed to fight anybody their own size." "Nine days. Nobody talks about hips."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:400:On this day of the year, the bluff had two lectures and a coursework deadline and nothing else at all. Nobody had marked this date in front of him since he left Fen Street, and somewhere along the road he had stopped being the kind of person who noticed. On the morning of his fifteenth he had written the date at the top of a page at Greyvane and gone on to the next line, and it had seemed to him then the right and grown-up thing to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:424:"I asked Hesk. In a letter. A year ago." With her good heel Lira dragged the next chair back from the table. "Karis wanted it for a column, at Greyvane, and came to me, because she thought of course I'd know. And I didn't. All that time, and I didn't know what day you were born, and you'd never once let it slip, and you never would, not unless somebody put it on a form in front of you with a pen. I was so angry about that I couldn't sleep. So I wrote to the one man who'd tell me. He answered by the next coach. Three lines: the date, and *thank you*, and *he won't have told you*. You people treat information like it's rationed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:464:"I'd like it understood," she said, "that this isn't a present. You asked me for it long ago, at Greyvane, and I never finished it. I've finished it. Today seemed as efficient a day as any to hand it over, and that's the whole of the reason."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:580:*Ember, Karis's. Consented to on paper. Already on Greyvane's record, which makes it the one I can show a panel without a second thought.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/compiled-prompt.md:582:*Shadow, Bronze tier, the first notice ever to name one. Eighteen days old. A hiding half and a moving half. Used: never. Price: running, from when I wake to when I sleep, whether I touch it or not. See the working ledger. And Karis's chart, column four, which reads* see holder *and will go on reading it for a long while.*
