books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-12.md:39:The escort order's probe: professionally conceived, professionally accepted in failure. That was the input he disliked most and he made himself sit with it longest. An amateur repeats a failed approach. A bureaucracy escalates a failed approach through its own grades, which takes months and generates paper. What had come to the gate had done neither. It had made one attempt, at a cost of four practitioners and a morning, had been refused by a chancellor and a registrar, had declined to demand the written refusal that any real office would have wanted more than the enrollee himself — and had ridden away at a working trot. An apparatus that prices its probes and does not repeat them is an apparatus with a schedule, and a schedule implies a next item, and the next item was not going to be paper.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-12.md:47:The custody that became paperwork by the time anyone asked. This was the grade that had ended two careers he knew of and had never once been recorded as anything but an administrative irregularity. A person is taken; a facility receives him; a file is opened at the facility with a lawful heading; within nine days there is an instrument on record that authorizes what has already happened, and the instrument is genuine, and the office that issued it exists by then, and every question thereafter is answered accurately.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-02.md:75:The request traveled paths Cael could not see. He logged what he could: nine days of silence, the fourteen-day correction window suspended pending advisory — which Karis noted was itself a concession, since you did not suspend a deadline for a question you expected answered against the applicant — and the watchers at the bluff road holding their rotation without so much as a new face.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-02.md:163:She took her own notebook and wrote — not long; a note, not a finding — and he read what he could of her face while she wrote it, which was less than usual. He had a file on that expression. He had been building it for two years, in archives and reading rooms: the look she wore at Greyvane's founding-era shelf, at the compilations she kept circling — the look of a reader who has just recognized a stratum. He could not see the page from where he sat. He could see the charter open to the old provisions, the fielding clause and the exhibition category both living in the parts printed before the current vocabulary existed, and he could see her look from the charter to her own notebook's front matter, where a certain family of citations lived, and back.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-10.md:3:The Silver program's first road meet fell at the term's midpoint, at the river academy's own hall two days north — the confluence town, the season's best floor, the place a regional Iron title had been decided a year before — and the delegation went to it as a delegation for the first time since Norhold, because Withrow had ruled that Halcenvane's Silver entrant traveled under the academy's colors with the academy's people, proceedings or no proceedings, and the ruling had the whole of her tenure in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/compiled-prompt.md:337:- **Karis's formal session** (minuted, two hours). Her Greyvane model is twenty-two nulls, then sustained observation plus genuine stakes, and *stakes means earnest engagement, not violence*. Seln's columns are all fuller. "The conditions are met… Which is why we have to talk about whether you may." Cael's first honest move: "You want it." / "Yes." / "Good. Say that part out loud again in about an hour when we're all being clever."
