editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/compiled-prompt.md:1215:**Calendar.** Greyvane final term: wk 3 the find (Thursday-ish afternoon); wk 4 nine letters; wk 7 first replies; wk 8 Bracken's first (four questions); wk 11 the fourth (forty-one); wk 14 Thursday night the finding, letter to Bracken next morning; wk 16 the other three file transfers; wk 17 the fifteenth letter; wk 19 counsel's seal to Naveth; wk 20 rematch (first bell); wk 21 Thursday Quenna; wk 22 Monday Naveth's notice, Wednesday Wray and packing, Thursday Prynn at the gate and departure. Four days' road. Arrival at Halcenvane = **five months and nine days after the ruling**. Then Halcenvane days 1–21: day 2 the rooms; day 3 the method failure and triage; day 6 the Crown yard; day 9 Brom assessed and placed, Ostrand; day 11 the log; day 13 the hatch; day 14 the faculty session (Rooke); about day 16 the carrel appeals; day 19 Withrow; day 21 the assessment notice. No season named at Halcenvane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/compiled-prompt.md:1225:- Term close (ch 4): *Greyvane. Twenty-two weeks. A word found and not killed. A bout won by one exchange, perhaps none. A sentence from Quenna and a book from Prynn, and both of them meant more than they said. / Done. Tomorrow, the river.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/compiled-prompt.md:1238:**Compact / watchers.** Greyvane: five, open. Road: seven faces, five changes, never fewer than two, three at junctions and inns. Halcenvane: two at the ferry landing, two at the bluff road's foot, changing every four hours (Lira's count). No contact.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:16:Greyvane, the first weeks of the final term after the hearing. Cael is fifteen. He has five fragments and the uncounted session-nine anomaly. The ruling is public and only jurisdictional. Karis has said the next problem is a *positive* basis, and she has only "the shape of where it should be." Five watchers rotate at the gate, openly. Edran has asked for a rematch "next semester, same terms." Prynn's shelf has its gap. Vell has not answered. The four of them already know they are outgrowing the place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:43:Chapter boundaries; how much of the Greyvane term is scene and how much is log; the Edran rematch's tactics within the facts above; the texture of Ostrand (barges, the customs house, the toll board's nine categories); Brom's habit of paying; Lira's grudge with a schedule ("That's a grudge with a schedule. Same thing, if you keep the schedule."); Karis's appetite for written terms; the humor of the gate clerk's impatience. You may invent conversation on the road and at Greyvane. Do not invent new named people.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-02.md:5:The question was not *what is this*. He could get *what is this* out of a prospectus, and Karis already had. The question was the one he had been sharpening since Greyvane, the one that had turned out to be the only question with teeth in it: *why did this academy make the investment?*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-02.md:7:Institutions did not do things for reasons they published. They did things for reasons that were visible in what they spent money on, where they put their best people, what they measured, and what they let fail. Greyvane had spent its money on beds and its conviction on one provost and one scout, and had let its library rot politely for sixty years because nobody in the building had ever had the standing to argue for shelving. Its priorities had been legible in three days. Cael had assumed, walking out of the gate on the fourth bell, that Halcenvane's would take four.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-02.md:13:His method had been built for a district and then extended to a small academy, and both of those had been fair tests of it. It ran in layers, and the layers had names by now: rhythm, weight, breath, and — added at Greyvane — *position*, which meant where a person or a thing was allowed to be, and by whose permission. The compound gaze was the four layers running at once, checking each other. He had gotten it down to something close to a reflex. He could enter a room and have the room's structure by the second look.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-02.md:19:He learned that number on the first morning, off a notice at the refectory door concerning bread allocation, and stood in front of it doing arithmetic he did not enjoy. At Greyvane he had known every student by face inside a month and by gait inside two. Four hundred and twelve was not a larger version of that problem. It was a different problem with the same shape, which was the sort of error he had trained himself out of making about opponents and had never once had to guard against in a building.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-02.md:27:*Method failure, north hall, third day. Not fatigue — I had slept and eaten. Capacity. The gaze holds somewhere between nine and twelve live subjects at working resolution and then degrades, and the degradation is not graceful: it doesn't get blurry, it gets confident. It starts filling the gaps with plausible material. That is the single most dangerous failure mode I have ever documented in myself and I have been running it undetected for three days because Greyvane never had more than nine people on a floor at once.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-02.md:41:The other thing triage bought him was a question he had never had to ask before: *who goes in the eight?* At Greyvane the answer had been everyone worth watching, because everyone worth watching had fitted. Here he had to choose, and choosing meant declaring, in his own notebook, a private ranking of who in this building was going to matter — which was, he realized on the fifth evening, exactly the kind of judgment the standings boards on the Crown yard's north wall made publicly, in chalk, season after season, and got wrong.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-02.md:53:*Library: forty-one thousand volumes by the accession board at the entrance, which I do not fully believe; Karis says the real figure is probably thirty-six and that accession numbers include everything ever received including duplicates and things later destroyed. Registry-law holdings in the south range, third and fourth floors, which is four floors more of that material than Greyvane owned in total. Prynn's whole archive would fit in one of the eleven bays and would be, Karis says, better indexed than any of them.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-02.md:61:Greyvane had sorted by need. That had been its whole character and its whole limitation. Halcenvane sorted by tier, and beneath the tier by something else — not money exactly, and not lineage exactly, but *proximity to the institution's ambition*. A Copper-tier student whose season looked like it might produce a standings placement got a better room than an Iron-tier student who trained quietly and would never be entered for anything. He tested this three ways over the following week and it held every time.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-02.md:199:He had expected hostility, because Greyvane had trained him to expect hostility, and Edran had come at him with a petition and a stance and a grievance he could read off a shoulder. This was not that. This was worse, in the sense that there was nothing in it to beat.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-02.md:205:The fame was the last piece, and it was the one he had least prepared for, because at Greyvane the problem had been that nobody could say what he was.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-02.md:219:*At Greyvane the problem was that nobody could answer the question of what I am, and so the question stayed open, and an open question can be closed later with evidence.*
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-02.md:225:*Worse than Greyvane. I hadn't expected that. Write it down before I talk myself out of it.*
