books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-12.md:7:The facts, as the delegation held them: three years of shared training floor. The lattice learned side by side — Ternhall's Ember doctrine ran on paired development; you built your ignition architecture against a partner's, and the partner's against yours, and the two documentations grew up interleaved. Notebooks traded for two of the three years. And when Karis had transferred out — Greyvane first, then the road, then Halcenvane — Ivenne had inherited the standing she vacated: Ternhall's top rank, Iron 2 now, the school's current best, carrying the institutional memory of everything her former partner had ever practiced, plus Ternhall's full scouting file, which the season's own economy guaranteed was current.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-06.md:35:The cost landed on the others first, which Cael did not see for nine days and then could not stop seeing.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-06.md:87:Daeva's two documents arrived in the same week, and the delegation understood what the week was the moment the first was read aloud.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-06.md:95:*To the proceedings convened upon the enrollment of the unclassified practitioner of Halcenvane, from Daeva, Auremont Academy, Storm Path, Gold-tier Rank Three, in her own name and under her own rank:*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-06.md:109:Karis had the statement's legal status mapped by the evening, in the annex, one lamp, the code open at the provision that permitted it: "Any practitioner of Silver or above may enter a statement in an institutional matter touching evaluation standards. It's a fossil — pre-standardization; the era when senior practitioners' opinions were part of how evaluation *worked*. It has been used, in the registry's recorded history, sixty-one times. By Silvers. By Golds, never — Golds don't need to enter statements; Golds are what the statements are about." She turned the page. "And *classify the instruments* is not a request the code contains a form for. She's filed a demand no office can process, under a provision no office remembers, at a rank no one has ever used it from. It's the most Daeva document I've ever read and I've read her challenge filing."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-06.md:117:Lira's reading of the statement came at the rail the next evening, and it came sideways, the way her important readings did. "Zerin told me *be there*. Next cycle, Silver bracket, in four words, at a board, and then walked off, and I've been building toward it since." She watched the yard. "Daeva's just done the same thing for you. In fourteen words, on registry stock, from the top of the ladder. *Classify the instruments.* That's not a defense. That's *be there* — she's telling the whole machine where she'll be standing when it's done, and daring it to file her somewhere else." She pushed off the rail. "Auremont builds them strange. I'm starting to think it's the only thing they build right."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-24.md:47:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — Feryn. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — source protected, sealed still, the logic updated and the discipline held: a defector's tradecraft in my hands would confirm the surveillance-era acquisition, so the seal stays, and Seln knows now that it does, and knows why, which changes nothing about the seal and everything about the debt. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; the piece of the machine itself. Blade-adjacent — Ephram; the only "non-hostile" in the log; taken in earnest from a friend who asked to be shown straight.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-24.md:67:*Reydan will get his answer. Daeva will get her rematch. The road east is long, and it runs the right direction. Good.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-24.md:83:To Hesk, four pages of the tournament's own straightness — the ruling, the council, the counter, the boxes, the bout, the ford — because his grandfather read strain gauges and not polish and would want the whole run of it including the parts that did not flatter anybody. And at the end of it, the paragraph that would go in the pouch even if the four pages did not:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-001/recheck-r1.md:49:**Calendar and counts.** The sequence holds: find in week 3; nine inquiries in week 4; first Bracken letter in week 8; fourth letter in week 11; finding in week 14; transfers in week 16; fifteenth letter in week 17; counsel's seal in week 19; rematch in week 20; Quenna in week 21; notice, Wray, Prynn, and departure in week 22; four road days; arrival five months and nine days after the ruling; Halcenvane days 1–21. Seven road faces and five changes remain distinct. Halcenvane's bracket arithmetic is exact: 209 Copper + 138 Iron + 51 Bronze + 14 Silver = 412, with the Gold fellow and Cael outside the bracket count.
books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-12.md:183:He had built the instrument in the second week of term and had run it since without missing a day, because it was cheap and because it read a system he could not otherwise see. Two men at the ferry landing, changing every four hours. Two at the foot of the bluff road, the same. Openly. Since the road south from Greyvane, since the hearing, since the day the file went from a watched thing to a resourced one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.md:18:> "They're all on paper somewhere already. Vell has them in her ledger. Greyvane's panel noted them. The transcript has them. And none of the sixth. … If the gallery sees a thing on that floor, it has to have seen it on paper first."
