editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.md:74:**Calendar.** Matches the pending ledger block day for day: d168 courier/reading (Third-day, unit at the sixth bell); d170 Fifth-day board; d172 Seventh-day (Seln's requisition, Lira's room "If I win tomorrow"); d173 the ninth (file closed at the second bell; Lira–Fiske at the fourth bell); d174 the tenth (Brom–Merrick; "Nine days"); d175 the eleventh (slip; Brom "Nine days from now … three hours"; "Three nights ago" the cross — d172, correct; "two days ago" Merrick on the stair — d172, correct); d176 the twelfth (arrival at the fourth bell; close "seven days … eight"). Gwen's "on Third-day" for the next unit = d175, consistent with the weekly Third-day unit. Seln's bundle "would wait four days" from d172 = d176. Lira's "nine days" is counted from the tenth, as the ruling allows; the hip will be ten days old at the final, as the coordinator noted for M8. No Sowing date, no month order, no Sowing→Reaping count (#35). "A month back" for session fifteen (d141→d173 = 32 days) is fair.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-06.md:33:The story they had come for had circulated in the region for a year, since the crown, in the version regions circulate: local girl, thrown out at fourteen, certified Iron by the registry's own instrument, coming home in another academy's colors. Cael had heard three tellings of it in the town inside two days — once at the inn, once at the weighbridge, once from a steward explaining the gallery crush to a colleague — and each telling bent a little further toward the shape stories wanted: the wronged girl, the reckoning, the academy shamed on its own ground. Nobody in any telling mentioned that the certification was arithmetic. Nobody mentioned Vell's ledger, or the seasons of circuit bouts at four coppers a rating, or a re-certification track at Greyvane, or the six institutional facts that had had to line up. The story had a girl and a grievance and a homecoming, and it wanted a scene.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-06.md:73:Hesk's letter caught the delegation at the quarry town, having chased it up the hill roads with the persistence his letters always showed, and Cael read it at the document table where Bracken's pouch was sorted, because that was where letters lived now, per the information policy.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-06.md:97:"I know. It's restful, being around someone whose patience is worse than a rock's." She looked out at the yard, where the delegation's wagons stood frost-furred under the lamp. "Here's the finding. I walked into that hall carrying three years of a speech I was never going to give — you know the speech. *Look what you threw away.* Had it memorized by fifteen. Revised it every certification, every title. This morning I stood at a coat-rack twenty feet from their colors, and I went looking for the speech, and it wasn't there." She turned the mug. "I kept thinking I'd want a moment. Turns out the moment was three years of work, and I already had it. The ledger, the circuit, Greyvane, the crown, the station with the door. Every one of those was the moment. Fenmark just wasn't in the room for any of them, and a speech needs an audience, and the audience stopped mattering somewhere on a wagon between here and there and never sent word."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:91:Not a carrel; not an assignment in the upper reading room. A *room* — a former records annex off the library's north range, with a door that locked and her name on a card in its frame and a budget line the academy's own council had voted, the first it had ever voted for an enrollee's private research — and she stood in the doorway of it on the first afternoon of term with the founding-era compilations in her arms and the expression Cael had catalogued at Greyvane's shelf and Norhold's charter and had not, until now, seen pointed at a *door*.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:95:"They gave me a room, a budget, archive privileges above faculty grade, and a second-year assistant who reads registry law the way I read it at sixteen — which is to say obsessively and wrong — and whom I am correcting at a rate of one error per morning so she'll learn to find the others herself." Karis set the compilations down on the room's one table and squared them, and the squaring had something in it he had never seen in her hands before, which he entered that evening as *ownership*. "Ternhall gave me a doctrine. Greyvane gave me a shelf with a gap in it. This place gave me a room with a lock and asked what I'd do with it." She looked at the door. "I have been transferring out of institutions since I was sixteen, Cael. I'd like it noted that this is the first one I've ever wanted to stay in, and that I noticed the wanting, and that I'm suspicious of it, because I'm me."
