editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-001/recheck-r1.md:49:**Calendar and counts.** The sequence holds: find in week 3; nine inquiries in week 4; first Bracken letter in week 8; fourth letter in week 11; finding in week 14; transfers in week 16; fifteenth letter in week 17; counsel's seal in week 19; rematch in week 20; Quenna in week 21; notice, Wray, Prynn, and departure in week 22; four road days; arrival five months and nine days after the ruling; Halcenvane days 1–21. Seven road faces and five changes remain distinct. Halcenvane's bracket arithmetic is exact: 209 Copper + 138 Iron + 51 Bronze + 14 Silver = 412, with the Gold fellow and Cael outside the bracket count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:39:Under the strapping the left side kept up a low steady complaint, like a kettle left on the back of a stove. The rest of him was not settled at all. All day he had felt the things he carried moving about in him, slowly, the way men move furniture in a house where a great heavy chest has just been carried in. Every one of them leaned a little toward the new weight, and none of them had found its place. Twice on the walk down he had reached for the Wind and got something else. He thought it would be a long while before he could take a step without thinking about it. He had written that in the old volume already, and the healers' account of it, and his own, which was more honest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:41:He took out Hesk's book and opened it on the rail. There was just enough light. He wrote standing up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:51:The long table had all of them that night. Gault, and Rooke, and Bracken at the end with the pouch, and Withrow at the head with her cup. The two reserves were wide-eyed at the bottom of it. Seln sat at the far corner, on the margin, where he had sat all sitting. Cael told them the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:71:There was a little quiet. Then Seln spoke from his corner, in his counter voice, without looking up from the pouch he had open in his lap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:73:"Wardens threaten. Politicians hint. That was neither." The pouch's strap went through its buckle under his fingers. "That was an honest man spending career capital to tell you the truth a few months early. Treat the warning as real, because it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:87:The wider table broke up a little after the tenth bell. Rooke and Gault went up the stair arguing about the best week of the year to start a cohort on stone. Bracken took the pouch. Withrow went last. She stood at the door for a moment looking back at the table, as if she were counting something, and then she said goodnight to all of them in one word and was gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:101:She wrote it while they watched, slowly, in the hand she kept for papers that strangers would read long afterwards, and she wrote it twice, a draft and then a fair copy, with nothing crossed out on the second. Then she turned the fair copy round and pushed it across the table to him, and he copied it into Hesk's book under the minute from two nights before, in his best hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.md:74:**Calendar.** Matches the pending ledger block day for day: d168 courier/reading (Third-day, unit at the sixth bell); d170 Fifth-day board; d172 Seventh-day (Seln's requisition, Lira's room "If I win tomorrow"); d173 the ninth (file closed at the second bell; Lira–Fiske at the fourth bell); d174 the tenth (Brom–Merrick; "Nine days"); d175 the eleventh (slip; Brom "Nine days from now … three hours"; "Three nights ago" the cross — d172, correct; "two days ago" Merrick on the stair — d172, correct); d176 the twelfth (arrival at the fourth bell; close "seven days … eight"). Gwen's "on Third-day" for the next unit = d175, consistent with the weekly Third-day unit. Seln's bundle "would wait four days" from d172 = d176. Lira's "nine days" is counted from the tenth, as the ruling allows; the hip will be ten days old at the final, as the coordinator noted for M8. No Sowing date, no month order, no Sowing→Reaping count (#35). "A month back" for session fifteen (d141→d173 = 32 days) is fair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:39:Withrow sat on his left and Rooke on his right, with Bracken behind them against the wall, holding the pouch. Across the table Auremont's delegation head sat with his hands folded, a tall greying man, heavy in the shoulders, with the expression of somebody who has come to do a thing properly and does not expect it to work. There was an empty chair beside him. Then there was a gap the width of a chair, which nobody had arranged and everybody could see, and then Daeva.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:215:He did not have an answer for that, so he did not give one. They went in along the wall together with the rain coming down properly now, and halfway back Lira started laughing at nothing, at the wet and the dark and the squall, and after a while he was laughing too. But it went into the old volume that night, under the four words about the steward, in the smallest hand he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/B4-RECONCILIATION-APPLIED.md:57:| B3 | "since last spring" → "a year, near enough" / "since the records hall"; C9 rewritten (acquired d149/150; eighteen days old at the eleventh of Sowing; the source's "Sixteen weeks" superseded) | BOOK_MAP §12 C9; M7 packet inventory line |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-001/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:19:| 5 | The First True Sentence | 5,275 | 5 | The toll board's nine lines; Brom pays; the barge horns; Karis's twenty thousand and the two Weighbridge Street offices; Halcenvane from mid-bridge; five months and nine days; fifteen. The bluff road's passing bays; watchers at the ferry and the road's foot; the gate clerk's four tracks. Intake: four desks for twelve; Brom nine minutes; Karis's eleven clauses; Lira's flag "goes on" to the queue and she makes the clerk write the dispatch date. Bracken: the stacks worked backward, Cael's catch, "never let a document… claim more than it proves", seventeen, the transcript at six silver marks, the record read three times, "It's the first one that's true.", two signatures, *94 min.*, Gault and the baseline, why twenty-two letters. The wall: carrel eleven; the grudge with a schedule; "Enrolled." … "I'd have paid more." |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-001/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:30:**Calendar.** Greyvane final term: wk 3 the find (Thursday-ish afternoon); wk 4 nine letters; wk 7 first replies; wk 8 Bracken's first (four questions); wk 11 the fourth (forty-one); wk 14 Thursday night the finding, letter to Bracken next morning; wk 16 the other three file transfers; wk 17 the fifteenth letter; wk 19 counsel's seal to Naveth; wk 20 rematch (first bell); wk 21 Thursday Quenna; wk 22 Monday Naveth's notice, Wednesday Wray and packing, Thursday Prynn at the gate and departure. Four days' road. Arrival at Halcenvane = **five months and nine days after the ruling**. Then Halcenvane days 1–21: day 2 the rooms; day 3 the method failure and triage; day 6 the Crown yard; day 9 Brom assessed and placed, Ostrand; day 11 the log; day 13 the hatch; day 14 the faculty session (Rooke); about day 16 the carrel appeals; day 19 Withrow; day 21 the assessment notice. No season named at Halcenvane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:3:He closed the year that evening in Hesk's book, at the table under the window, with Hesk's steel pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:37:Bracken shut the pouch for the north at the eighth bell to the minute, and had said so at supper, so the letters had a shape to fit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:49:He took both down the stair at a quarter to the bell. Bracken weighed Hesk's in his palm, and looked at Cael over it, and put it in the pouch without any remark at all, which from Bracken was a remark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:87:Seln came in last, with the pouch, as he had come in last every evening of the sitting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:89:He did not go to the end of the board, where the margin was. He did not go to the corner by the door, or stand at the counter with the keeper, or take the stool by the fire where he had sat in other waystations with the pouch on his knee. He came down the room and sat on the bench, in the middle of it, between Lira's boots and Karis's lamp, at the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:99:*A line on a form says what a man is listed as,* he wrote in the old volume that night, very small, because the old volume had only a few leaves left. *It has no way to notice when he's become something else underneath it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:103:"Eleven," said Seln, from along the bench, without looking up from the pouch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:139:The old volume ran out a leaf at a time as the road did. He gave it a few lines a night, small, and most nights they said the same things. *The Wind at three on yard clay. Came when called, twice of three. Yellow creeping in at the edges of the left side. The flag stands.* Each night the things he carried had shifted a little further toward the new weight, and each night they had not finished. The healers had said a month. His own reckoning said more. The notice's warning sat at the back of his eyes where he had first read it, and did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:159:It was dated four days before. Hesk had not had his six pages. He could not have; they were still somewhere on the hill road, in Bracken's pouch. He had written off the broadsides, which reached the coast faster than any pouch. Then he had worked out which road the wagons would take, and which crossroads they would come to on which day, and sent the letter to meet them there. He had been right about all of it. Hesk's answers had always set out before the questions reached him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:165:He read it again. Then he slid it in behind the old volume's front board, with the other letters that lived there, and ate the stew cold, and did not mind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:191:On the ferry he wrote in the old volume for the last time, at the foot of its last leaf, until the line ran into the binding and there was no room for a word more. He shut it and put it inside his coat with Hesk's letter. Everything he wrote from now on would go into Hesk's book, and he thought it would be a long while before Hesk's book was full. There had been no letter from Fiske all year. Lira had said she was not counting the days, and had not, and would be in the Silver bracket next cycle whether Fiske watched it or not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:209:Seln was on the tailboard with the pouch across his knees. He looked up the road toward the gate and said nothing. Cael had learned that this was how he agreed.
