editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/review-cold.md:13:Cael matters most to me because his observational gift is inseparable from his vulnerability: the better he reads others, the greater the danger that he will go “thin.” Lira is nearly coequal in emotional pull. Her refusal of Cael's pages, use of foundry knowledge on Fiske's slick, and decision to climb the tier solely to watch him give her independent agency rather than making her supportiveness decorative. Karis, Brom, Bracken, Rooke, Fiske, and even Jask all earn distinct places with relatively small actions.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/review-cold.md:30:| Humor / warmth | 9/10 | Chapter 39, Bracken promising to write fifteen dull days “up as a month”; chapter 43, the repeated refusal to talk about Lira's hip and Karis issuing a “Receipt” for the batons. | The humor comes from established habits and affection. It relieves pressure without erasing fear or injury. | High |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/review-cold.md:72:1. **Location:** Chapter 38, opening through the first records-hall meeting, with one possible echo in chapter 41's hall-three failure. **Observed issue:** The cold entrant encounters “hold ordinary,” bumps, edges, going thin, provision status, and several Compact roles before receiving a compact physical account of what Cael is preventing and what exposure would cost. **Effect on the reader:** Immediate tension is present, but some younger or newly arrived readers can recognize danger only abstractly and must retroactively reinterpret the opening. **Proposed scope:** Add or revise no more than two short anchoring sentences—one early sentence tying “thin” to observable disappearance/exposure, and one clean reminder of what an adverse provision evaluation changes. Avoid a history recap or a glossary paragraph. **Strength to preserve:** The courier reveal, Cael's disciplined refusal to follow, and the manuscript's trust in concrete behavior over explanatory summary.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/completion/whole-arc-read.md:10:The weaknesses a straight read shows are not structural. First, the author has a repertoire, and it has shifted since Book 4 rather than thinned: the squared page (198 hits of "square/squared" in 51 chapters: "squared to the edge", "square to the table", "her small square hand"), "flat" (186), the superlative-ever formula ("the most careful thing anybody had ever done to him on a floor", 41 instances), "as he read/did/considered everything" (27), "Nobody said anything" (31) and "said nothing / did not say anything" (92), "for a moment" (120), the micro-timing coinage ("a fraction" 53, "half a beat" 36, "quarter-beat" 35), "to nobody in particular" (25), the ears going red (13, eleven of them Ephram's), and the number eleven again (68, a third of them reached for). Second, a shape that repeats until a reader can predict it: the exhibition bout with its five parts (walk the floor; find the one thing the opponent never priced; the handshake aphorism; the figure "at the proper interval"; the Log entry with the bill), run fifteen times, and at Norhold the back room with its rocking table and the pump, visited twelve times. Third, a soft stretch at the confluence (ch21–23: five exhibitions in two chapters, three of them teaching lessons the quarry town has already taught) and a flat one on the empty board (ch47). Fourth, four seams a line pass closes: a wrist "healed for a month" five months after the final; "the tournament was still nine days off" at a flag nine days from the city and sixteen from the opening; a colour-guard that reads as eight men where the ledger has four; and Seln opening "his own case" on the eve of finals when the book says the locked case was never opened. Eleven line fixes below, three cross-movement priorities for the two lanes. Nothing needs a rebuild.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/completion/whole-arc-read.md:26:Checked every dated beat against the ledger's day-numbers and against each other. The recess: twelve weeks and four days, sixty-one sessions, nine letters, one dated change at the landing in the ninth week (ch1). The qualifying year: d0 convocation (First-day), d4 Fifth-day the filing and the counter, d5 Rooke signs Ephram's sheet, d8 the memorandum "four days after the courier", d9 the stair at a run, d10 Bracken's check, d11 Withrow signs, d12 the morning courier, d21 the fourteenth day crossed off, d16 Hesk's reply "the fourth day of the silence", d18 "the sixth evening" on the wall, d25 the card ("Thirteen days, Karis says", ch5:131, which is d12 to d25), d28 the map room "the first evening of the fifth week", d29 the mock ring, d30 "five days out" and "a little over three weeks" from the signing (ch4:153, d11 to d35), d33 the ferry, d34 the wool town, d35–36 the meet, d37 the ruling before noon, d38–39 half work, d45 Bracken on the stair, d46–52 a week west with frost on the fourth morning, d53 the mill town, d56 the yard-master, d57 the evaluation and "Variance is cheap", d59 the first pole at a coaching inn, d66 the circular "two days after the wagons got home", d67 the counter and the pouch "in two days", d69 the ferry, d77 the milestone and the silence, d78 the quarry town "at dusk on the tenth day", d81 the first meet day "eleven weeks" after d4 (ch15 and the ledger), the coat-rack "the end of the silence's fifth day", d83 the foreman and the costing "more than a fortnight ago" (d66), d84 the quarterly, d86 the waystation ("two days coming down", "another week" to the bluff, the circular at midday, the chancellor told "approximately four hours ago"), d93 the bluff on a Third-day evening, d97 the walking exchange "on the fourth morning", d101 "the second Fourth-day" on the speed line, d106 the wagons north with the carriage, d107 the confluence at dusk, d108–109 the meet, d110 the convenor, d112 the lamps on a First-day "two days later", d119 the cold term, d149 the reading room "a Third-day afternoon in the fifth week", the sixth week's letter, d186 Seln's report "the last Fifth-day", d187 the seedings, d188 the wall, "the next night" the melt, d190 departure on a Second-day. The road: day 3 the coast column, day 4 "downhill", day 6 the corner game, day 9 the flag "a week out", day 12 the five-arch bridge, day 14 the road sorting itself, day 17 the rise, day 18 the gate. Norhold: the second morning the likeness, the third evening Ephram's "pre-sold", the fourth Auremont, the fifth morning the orientation and the draw at dusk, Umber's eve, T1 "the first day of the new month", T2 the first rounds, T3 the two filings, T4 the trial rules and seat twelve, T5 Lira's quarterfinal and the deferral, T6 Ephram's exit, T7 the open warm-up, T8 Zerin and Brom's rematch, T9 Marek, Ilsev's form, Ivenne, the plaque "on the ninth day", T10 Karis's semifinal "the tenth day" and the crews, T11 the captain, T12 the cake ordered "the morning after your first bout", T13 the hired hall and Vastin by post road, T14 "the fourteenth day" and seat twelve filled "thirteen days" held, the night of seventeen, T15 the rest day, the fourth of Gault's four, the draw and the filing, T16 the trial, T17 Rhagen's news and "Twenty-first day, then, for third", "four mornings from now", T18 rain, the warehouse "on the second morning of the empty board", T19 the form "before dawn on the third day", Bracken's three troubles, the strip at dusk, the long table at the eighth bell, T20 the acceptance, the hearing at the fourth bell, the squall, Vastin's notice "the day after the filing" with departure "the morning after next", T21 the hill, the standings, the party and "three days from now", the midnight crews, T22 the shut hall, the ring "two days", T23 the fifth bell and the eve, T24 finals day, "two evenings ago" at the mast, the keeper's cap after "three weeks of mornings", T24–25 the panel, T25 the entry at dawn, the copy line, the return, the hour at the fourth bell, the pole, the rule, T26 the sixth guess "on the second morning", the closing at the second bell, the ledger "that evening", the pouch at the eighth bell, T27 Auremont's four words (as the M9 ledger corrects), road days 1, 3, 5, 9, 11 (Hesk's letter "dated four days before"), 12, 14, 17. Every Halcenvane weekday named falls where the ledger's arithmetic puts it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/completion/whole-arc-read.md:28:One place drifts. **ch27:293**: "The tournament was still nine days off" at the crossroads flag on the ninth road day: the city is nine days off (day 18), the opening sixteen; the sentence's own second half ("the tournament's weeks had begun at that pole") wants "city". Fix 3.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/completion/whole-arc-read.md:46:Into Book 6: seventeen (staged on the page at the rest day, ch43–45, no date printed); seven confirmed, the seventh Storm-adjacent by the notice verbatim, its stability flag standing at the close ("The flag stands.", ch60:139); the anomaly uncounted; the bank spent permanently and listed (ch60:11–19); Shadow never deployed and Seln never told ("The debt it was sealed for is still owed and still unspoken"); the costed rule initialled by four (ch59:103–107); the match record verbatim and "the most-reviewed result" (the copy line, Auremont in it); every public theory wrong and the words never used outside the circle; third of fourteen; Brom continental Copper champion with the summons collected on road day nine, "ninety days", "The recess's problem." (ch60:55); Lira "Silver bracket. Next cycle." (ch60:75); Karis's quarterfinal over Ternhall's best; Ephram the round of sixteen and the caller; Seln at the middle of the bench from the first waystation with "records and floor scheduling" against his name on his own manifest (ch60:87–97), one true report with "Routine." left off (ch58:103–109), the case unopened; Vastin's warning off the record, his testimony verbatim and set aside, "He declined to discuss the Level 4 designation" rendered as "I won't answer that one" (ch58:213); Ilsev's return verbatim and her first private sheet; Havel's fifth; the question entered for formal proceedings, no vote, the flag "no living hand had authorized" and no age; Daeva's "Rematch." and the banner to Auremont; the scouting economy closed benign (ch17) and the dossier in circulation; Fenmark closed as a wound at the coat-rack (ch15) and shut for good by Lira's "scrapyard" at the closing (ch59:147); Reydan's one line; Vell's letter; Hesk's letters; the watchers back on the twelfth day (ch60:167); the old volume's last line on the last ferry and the road's entries in it, the year's ledger in Hesk's book (C6). Hand-off items for the Book 6 reconciliation, not for this book: no Norhold session was drafted (C8), so two renewals stand on paper at the close and A11's "four stamps" goes to the owner as the plan says; "two watchers and a horse" at a market-town bridge is the shape Book 6 opens on; Withrow's eighteen years and Vastin's nineteen are now in the edition.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/completion/whole-arc-read.md:81:**Development cadence.** About two realisation beats a chapter, as planned, with the bump in M6–M7 ("Silver is not a bigger Iron", "the doctrine is filling", "the first one I didn't set", the ladder), and the progression vocabulary front-loaded as §7.2 asks: tier, rank, rating, par, band, seeding, exhibition, validation ("adjacent"), the three words, the strike, the composite, the documented rate, all taught by ch13 and surfacing afterward at the pin (page thirty-one), the threshold, Brom's review, the trial rule, the bank, the entry, the ledger. The supporting cast all get their arcs without taking the budget: Lira from "waiting for the bill" to "It's a better want. It costs more." to "Put her down" to "Silver bracket. Next cycle."; Brom priced right by himself first (the buried Copper, the funnel, "Sound", the hill fighter's five, Marek's "I read it as your mistake", the wall, the step, "The recess's problem"); Karis from the clause to the trade in records to "Unknown." to the burned plan to the finding about Cael's loneliness (ch39:117, the book's most generous page) to the model to "Now it's the house's"; Ephram from the second weight to freight to trial caller to "pre-sold" to "South." to the handshake to the plain version, his ears a running line; Seln from the last line of the roster to the middle of the bench by one table at a blue door and one word on a tailboard; Rooke from the near-question to the pocket to the pads to "household" to the mercy line; Withrow's order of operations, her sentence at a waystation, her toast, "we taught her they were parts", her absence from the board and her reading of a letter's outside; Gault from the handle to fourth on the figures to the ribbon; Bracken's mirror, pouch, quarry stone and the herald's fine; Umber from the seals to the screen to "approve it before somebody makes me invent something" to his own boot on the floor to "under"; Vastin's five stages exactly as §3 D asks; Ilsev's pen stopping and her girlhood hand; Havel packing the notebook like part of the work; and Daeva, the best new character the edition has made, whose window (ch49) is the book's finest cutaway and whose "Am I early, or are you late?", "Specimen.", "Today somebody asked me something." and the two ways to use a slackened lane are all hers.
