editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:11:Behind the ledger, in a pocket of the back board, was the file's correspondence. Most of it was ordinary paper. At the very back, under a brass clip, lay a single folded sheet with the registry's stamp on it. Pinned to it was a narrow docket in Havel's hand: *Correspondence in. Delegation's courier, up. Eighth bell.* It had come up the bluff the evening before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-006/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:133:- **Rooke:** has asked nothing. He offered to scratch Karis and accepted her refusal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-006/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:363:   - M7's packet wants the Shield captain, Vastin's seat ("held for him for nine days"), the duelist and *then* the rest day (T11, the birthday). As drafted, M7 must either move its exhibitions past the rest day, or put both exhibitions on T11–T12 with the rest day and trial re-dated, or re-count the "nine days".
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-006/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:378:12. **Umber's cutaway** keeps to craft and the mark, with no theory and no Cael. He "struck the Blade judge a week ago" (it was the eve of the opening, nine days before).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/pre-repair/chapter-43.md:149:"Every one of those twenty," said Lira, "we won knowing everything. That's how we've always done it, you and me. Everything on the table, every time, and then we go and use it on each other. There's been nothing better in either of our lives these two years. I mean that." She tightened the knot with her thumb. "And on the nineteenth it's worth nothing. Less than nothing. You know my leg, and since yesterday you know it worse. I know where your iron gives out. If we go on, we'll spend nine days guessing what the other one's guessing about what we're guessing, and we'll walk out onto those boards playing chess." She looked up at him. "Neither of us fights chess."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/pre-repair/chapter-43.md:185:"Nine days," said Lira. "Nobody talks about hips."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/pre-repair/chapter-43.md:293:"Down *today*." Brom's voice stayed low. Shouting would have been easier to bear. "Nine days from now you're going to be on a floor for three hours. Gault, and the panel, and an Archmarshal, and whoever else they've brought with them. And somewhere in that gallery there'll be a person whose whole trade is noticing when a thing isn't where it should be. And you've just shown me that the one thing you can't steer goes thin when all of you is busy." He did not move out of the way. "What do you call a three-hour evaluation, then?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/pre-repair/chapter-43.md:311:*Hold. First. Before growth, before control, before the five. Nine days of it on every floor I stand on, and then three hours of it in front of the panel. Everything else stands on this, and if this goes, everything on top of it falls.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:5:The pouch came up from the river gate at the first bell on the rest day as on every other day, because the post kept no holidays and nor did the office that answered it. He unlocked it at the end of the long table in the common room, with the house still asleep above him, and sorted it into the order in which it would be wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:109:He crossed the shop to the long table. He put the parcel down in front of Cael, and laid a card and an envelope on top of it, and said, "The pouch. This morning's," in exactly the voice he used across the counter at home on the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:183:"The office," said Seln, "was once required to travel nine days by coach in the company of a clerk who whistled. The clerk whistled one tune. It had four bars. The office counted the repetitions on the seventh day as a matter of record, and stopped counting at nine hundred, and has never since been able to hear the tune without wishing to file a complaint." He picked up his cup. "That is all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:51:What would it cost him? He made himself ask it, because a man who does not price his own advice ends by giving the expensive kind for nothing. And the honest answer was: nothing. The advisory recommended no action and took no side. It quoted only what anyone could buy at a stationer's, and its one refusal was to find words in a charter that the charter had never held. He had built a career on the principle that the text governed, and had held to it in rooms with far worse air in them than this one. He would not let it bend for one enrollee, in either direction. It cost him nothing, yet. He made a note of the *yet* in his head, where he kept such notes, and did not examine it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:61:The ordinary silence after an advisory had a shape he knew as well as he knew the porter and his pigeons: an acknowledgment from the receiving office, a file marked closed somewhere below with a clerk's initials, a line in the weekly digest. He had read that silence ten thousand times. This was not it. The advisory was received. He knew it was received, because the dispatch slip in his coat said so. After that, nothing. No acknowledgment came back up the chain. The file did not reappear in the digest. The two letters on the fourth line of the routing slip were not on any other slip that crossed his table in the next nine days, and he looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:73:Bracken laid it on the records-hall counter at the third bell with no covering letter, because there had been none. It was the first meet's official card, printed by the host's office on thick cream stock in two columns, and it listed every delegation entered at the wool town in the order of their registration, with each house's competitors in a block beneath its name. Halcenvane's block was the ninth, and it ran five lines. The first four looked like any house's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:115:"It's not kindness. If you go lame at the first meet I lose my sessions." He walked on, and then said over his shoulder, without turning, "The second-year entry. Every Iron fighter in that hall will have it. Watch the heel."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:121:He wrote it at the rail of the Crown yard's top tier that afternoon, with the observation notebook on his knee and the porter below him chalking the first meet's date onto the long board in letters a hand high.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/completion/LANE-A-REPORT.md:49:- **ch18, the audit** (formerly ch18:207–245, 836 → 459 words). The re-listing of what the market holds is now one sentence. Two rules remain: the pouch for letters, and closed floors (Ephram), with Karis's closed-rooms rider. Rooke's "cheaper than the lesson" line and Seln's copied sheet ("small square hand") are kept. The southern coach's "two columns" paragraph is cut, so Karis's earlier one-line note on him stands unpaid, as a quiet observation. Ephram's red ears are dropped, and "From Rooke it was very nearly a speech" is cut. The Log line is trimmed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/completion/LANE-A-REPORT.md:73:- **ch27, "The city was still nine days off".** It reads cleanly against Karis's "a full week from the city. More." and the keeper's "a week out".
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-006/pre-repair/chapter-42.md:263:*Four. One very long minute at the end, which their first meeting had never needed. L. stands still. F. has to come. L. goes onto the oil. Two to one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-001/review-editorial.md:13:Interest caught immediately at `chapter-01.md:3`, with “The pages stopped.” The sensory absence, Prynn's lifted attention, and Karis's refusal to call four lines a finding make archival research feel like danger before the legal explanation begins. The second strong hook is the week-twenty rematch: the question of what Edran bought with a hand of reach becomes a clean tactical mystery, and every exchange changes the answer. The third is the move to Halcenvane, where the school itself becomes an instrument Cael can read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-001/review-editorial.md:52:- At arrival, Cael is explicitly fifteen and five months nine days past the ruling (`chapter-05.md:41`). Halcenvane's charter age is 141 years (`chapter-01.md:223`; `chapter-02.md:61`), and the Copper Crown carries 131 years of names (`chapter-06.md:193`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:293:Bracken's pouch came up on the hill coach that afternoon, as Bracken had said it would, and found the quarry town, as Bracken had said it generally did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:335:The letter went behind the observation notebook's front board, where Hesk's letters lived.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md:89:He treated Cael's refusals the way a good steward treats an expense. He did not try to stop them, but let each one happen and made sure it cost something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md:141:He held it. He went on refusing, on worse ground than before, with the racks at his shoulder and the ropes closing, and he paid for every refusal on the contested boards in a way he had not paid in the first two exchanges. The yard-master's steps came closer together. Twice Cael had to turn hard off his left foot to keep out of the old man's reach, and the second time something in the back of his left calf drew tight, a long thin pull from the knee to the heel, like a cord being wound. He felt it and noted it and kept moving. He did not burst. He did not touch. He gave the panel a third exchange in which a boy kept himself alive on two-thirds of a floor and did nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md:153:He refused at a steady pace for the first part of the glass, steadier than before, the same short sideways step every time, so that the old man's short forward steps fell into time with it, step and refusal, step and refusal, like two men sawing a log. The yard-master did not resist it. A siege had its own rhythm, and he had no reason to break it. Cael watched the light on the floor and watched the old man's feet and counted, and on the fourth beat of the count he made his refusal a fraction short.
