editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-006/review-editorial.md:149:- ch37 Umber: "a week ago" for the struck judge (it was the eve of the opening, nine days; the author flags it) and "the four who were left and the one who had replaced him" assumes the struck judge belonged to this final's panel, which M5 did not say (calibration week tests judges in fives). "Struck one from the lists before the opening" and "the five below him knew it" would be safe.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-006/review-editorial.md:202:- Rule on §3a items 2–4 before M7 drafts: the rest day's T-number, the §6 re-dating from the trial onward, and Seln's "nine days".
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-002/review-cold.md:38:**The scores: yes, and the order is right.** The first meet's figures are given with band glosses before the mechanism is explained (26 = "a strong Iron's figure, with a little Silver at its edge"; 15 = "a Copper figure, low in the band"), and the mechanism (five slates, strike top and bottom, add; par is a Silver fifth-rank on an ordinary day at ten per mark) arrives in ch. 11 when Cael stands behind the trestle. Feel first, machinery second, motivated by Cael's own choice not to read the manual before watching the instrument. The eleven-minute interval is a wonderful concrete. Seln's arithmetic in ch. 13 is fully checkable from what the reader has been given: 23⅓, 22⅓, 24⅓, 21⅔, 21⅓, 23 all round to the posted figures, the spread is three points, and the comparison set (Brom 15 and 23 in one morning; the Blade 18 and 27) is one the reader has mostly seen. A listener can do this sum in their head, which is the whole point.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-002/review-cold.md:107:- **The adjudicator's two sentences** and Karis's reading of "not available from this office" as a direction, not a refusal (ch. 10).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-002/review-cold.md:111:- **The held third exchange** (ch. 12): a fight where the hero's hardest choice is to not win yet, paid for in a calf. Keep every refusal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-002/review-cold.md:135:- **Strength to preserve:** the siege itself ("He treated Cael's refusals the way a good steward treats an expense"), the apprentice's "*Oh*," and "You fight like an auditor."
