editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:205:"The quarry town," he said. "Ten days by the hill roads, and the post up there is a matter of faith." He laid the page on its pile. "So I'll send a pouch after you. The records office keeps one for the hill coach, which goes up on the Second-day and the Sixth-day, and it will find you at the quarry town if the hill coach finds the quarry town, which it generally does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:207:He took a canvas pouch from under the counter and laid it on the wood. It was grey and worn soft, with the academy's seal stamped on the flap and a brass ring for the lock. "Certified copies of anything the meet's clerks want from us. The chancellor's letters. And yours, if anybody writes to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:211:"Letters." Bracken looked at him over the counter with an expression of great patience. "Your grandfather writes, I believe, at a considerable rate. A letter sent to the town post at the quarry town sits in a box on a counter with forty others, where anybody who likes can read the outside of it. The pouch is locked here and unlocked by Seln at the far end, and what's inside it stays sealed until it reaches the hand it's addressed to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:213:He put the pouch back under the counter. "I don't suppose anybody on the circuit cares who writes to you. I simply prefer that they shouldn't have the chance to find out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:217:"It's a pouch," said Bracken, and went back to his sanding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:21:| 4 | A Mirror | 5,198 | 6 | **Night one at the long table:** Cael reads every version ("Untrue. Those are the only notes I'll take."); page one is the clause, standing alone. **Day 9:** Karis takes the residence stair at a run with her hair out of its pins; **the third schedule**, between the fee table and the rules for travelling surgeons; **both exhibition lines (exact)**; "a fossil"; "I've just solved ours"; Brom: "I've never seen you run." **Night three:** Bracken checks every citation in an hour and three-quarters; the four pages; page four is Bracken's, and he asked to write it; "That last line's a trap." / "It's a mirror." / **"A trap catches you moving. A mirror just stands there."**; the careful officer will forward it upward; "I'd have read the third schedule first." **Day 11, Withrow's office:** she signs with Cael present, gives **her order of operations once** (the house, the rules, the people, "last of all, you"), and adds that a thing built straight stands whoever stumbles in it. Cael finds that coming last is the safest place to stand; the founding charter on the wall. **Day 12, first Ephram session:** Cael calls weights aloud and reads **Ephram's shoulders**; Rooke: "I've been waiting a year for somebody else to"; *Good trade.* **The silence:** what people don't do; the Fourth-day session (*Better. Both.*); **Hesk's reply** ("pack wool… Every man should have one thing he's foolish about"; Cael notices that two men told him to pack for the cold, and does not join them); three letters now behind the front board. **Day 18, the wall:** Lira's "Who signed it?"; "You're counting their choices as your debts"; **Karis's card** and Brom's pencil, *Tell him the floor's still the floor*; the Log: handled by three people with a card, *on the wealth side of the ledger* |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:22:| 5 | Advisory | 5,128 | 4 | **Vastin (≈1,860):** the routing slip, initialled against *Referred for advisory* by a hand he cannot place; his room high in the Compact's house, the porter and the pigeons, the left hand; he reads the defence before the charge (the empty space under page one; the third schedule checked; the fourth paragraph's bones, as he once wrote to the registrar); the memorandum's solder; "of all the officers in this building, he was the one best placed"; two reasons to route a question to the man who had looked closest; the long draft struck through, then **the advisory (exact)**, signed with his name entire; "nothing, yet"; the wrong kind of silence; on the tenth day, the printed card filed for information. **Day 25, the card:** no letter; **the card line (exact)**; "*C. Cael*" explained as the printer's cut; "the first time any official paper has ever called him what we call him"; **"That is how careful institutions surrender. In the schedule."**; Lira hugs Karis; the house moves again (targets posted, cover sheet dated, forms filled); Ephram on the pine floors at the wool town ("Watch the heel… so it's said"). Log: *the shape the deciding left behind… I can't see the hand*; nine days. **Day 28, the map room:** Rooke's own yellow **rating card** (lost two touches to one, rated 26 to the winner's 24); the Standard taught on it: **execution, control, effect; five judges; strike high and low; average three; add three; par thirty**; the bands; weighting for standings; an exhibition gives only the figure; the road and Norhold; "By paper"; Umber; **"three honest instruments"**; Karis on who chose the three words, "**So was the sorting.**"; "Both can be true." **After:** Cael rules his own grid on the back of the card, and finds the band is his to pick; he prices Lira at 26, Brom at 24 and Ephram at 24, and does not price Karis. Log: **the honest want** (*I want that number*) and the other column (*I've already decided how to manage it*) |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:89:**Calendar.** Day 0 is convocation, a First-day; Halcenvane weekdays run First-day … Seventh-day, and Fifth-days fall on days 4, 11, 18 and 25. No season is named. No month is named, and no month order is given. The only date-names in the movement are the B4 recollections (the eleventh of Sowing; the note of the twentieth of Reaping). The first meet falls on about day 35, two days' drive south (the wool town).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:139:- Hesk's reply, behind the observation notebook's front board with the pine-case note and "Sixteen suits you" (three sheets now).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:296:**Tics** (after a hand pass). Explanatory "the way a X…" plus "as if" / "as though" now run at about 3 or fewer per chapter. "Exactly" is down to 1–3 per chapter, kept where it is literal (the card's "Twenty-three. Exactly."; "reads exactly as it reads tonight"). "For a long moment" appears once each in ch1, ch4 and ch5. "Plainly" appears twice in ch2. Incidental numbers were checked against canon counts: "nine" only for Hesk's letters, the ninth week and nine days; "eleven" only for eleven names; "four years" only for the landing beat; "three years" only for Book 2 events.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:376:- "Nine days" → "**Thirteen days**" (response down on d12, card up on d25). The line "Long enough for an answer. Too long for an easy one." is untouched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:413:- Greps after the last edit find none of "four years", "sixpence", "hymn", "tenth line" or "Nine days" in the chapters. The only "nine days" left is Vastin's own nine days of looking, in ch5. No season words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:139:The third meet. By the board, it was a quarry town ten days' drive to the north-west, a name he had never heard and a date in the deep part of the season. There had been nothing on the board about halls. There had been nothing about anything, except a date and a name. Twelve empty weeks behind a counter had given the man the season, read down to the temperature of its rooms, and he had spent one sentence of it, in front of a queue, in the voice he used for the hours.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:225:He kept it short. Hesk did not like long letters; he said a long letter was a man who had not yet decided what he meant. So Cael wrote what had happened, in the order it had happened, and stopped. The roster had been filed at noon by the registrar, with every name on the enrollment record, his own among them in the registry's spelling. The courier had gone over on the ferry with a cart of turnips. Gault had been issued a case with a handle and was unreasonably pleased about it. The first meet was five weeks out in a wool town two days to the south.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:87:Seln was at the door. Nobody had heard him come up. He had the pouch under his arm and he had evidently been there for some of it, and he did not apologise for that, or come in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:89:"He has kept every rule of a forty-year career," he said, in his counter voice. "If he means to break one for you, the breaking has been paid for somewhere you will never be shown the bill." He shifted the pouch. "Listen accordingly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:109:The space stayed empty. He blotted the sheet as it was, and folded it, and sealed it, and laid it on the corner of the table for Bracken's pouch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:247:For a moment the grey face did something Cael would spend half that night failing to name in Hesk's book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:3:He wrote the day in the old volume first, because the old volume was still the one he lived in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:15:*Seventeen. The first page of Hesk's book, on the rest day in the middle of the sitting. Kept at the blue door by everybody who matters, and written here by me, as I was taught to write down the numbers nobody else was keeping. Somebody kept this one. That's the first thing this book knows.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:85:She looked at the place on the rail where Withrow was standing, with Bracken a step behind her and the travel pouch under Bracken's arm. Then she looked at Cael. The torch over the board flared in the evening wind and settled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:99:He wrote the second entry in Hesk's book that night, under the inventory, with the window open and the court still murmuring below.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:16:| 51 | Two Clocks | 5,469 | 6 | **Lira (≈1,070):** the hip sets in two stages; the foundry; three bursts, the fourth bought on credit and told to Brom long ago; Fenmark's ninety breaths and the clerk's pen; breakfast, the batons untied ("Receipt"), "Nine days." **Cael:** the tiers fill from the back; his usual stone; ledger day 21, holding from waking; Bracken lays the two sealed records under the glass's stand; Karis: "a record made after the result has been coached"; Rooke in the north entrance; Ephram's nod; Fiske in the north front row; the delegation; Vastin seated, notebook shut on one knee; Havel empty-handed; the read let up on purpose, the hold kept. **Havel (≈970):** the card; the counsel's "It's a fight"; nothing in his hands; the pear; the notebook he does not wonder about; the broom; choosing to be here. **Lira (≈430):** the north door; Brom's hands; "Good." **Cael:** the two clocks; the terms; **exchange 1** (three asks at the seam, the answer already there, a different frame; the forearm lands on her set guard, *even*; she grins); **exchange 2** (Rooke's repair: stop swallowing whole; two bursts; ribs and floor, to Iron Skin; "Two gone.") |
