editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-57.md:223:He wrote all six into Hesk's book that night, one under another, and drew a line beneath them, and wrote under the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/review-cold.md:27:**What I expect next:** the first meet, at the wool town, with pine floors that give on the left side; a stranger's exhibition card with Cael's name on a line nobody has run in forty years; five badged judges writing three numbers; Cael rating in the low twenties on purpose and hating it; Brom priced by strangers against the buried Rank Four girl; Lira against the top of the Iron seeding and, eventually, Fenmark; and, above the bluff, whoever owns the two initials on the routing slip deciding when to stop being silent. I also expect the page Karis marked in the examiner's manual and the founding article she found to come back as the same discovery from two directions.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/review-cold.md:60:| Read-aloud quality | 6 | 7 | ch1 l.235–237; ch3 l.17; ch3 l.253–259; ch6 l.221–224; ch5 l.131 | Cadence is excellent and dialogue mostly self-attributes by voice. Specific stumbles: a duplicated beat ("turned round to the four of them. / He looked round at the four of them"); Lira's reply run into Rooke's paragraph; "She says:" arriving after a Karis paragraph so the first hearing assigns it to Karis; a missing section rule before "Cael stood alone"; and "nine days" that does not fit the day names. All punctuation/paragraph fixes. | High |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/review-cold.md:70:- **Timeline — ch5 l.131, "Nine days, Karis says, between the response going down and the card coming up."** The response left on the morning courier of a Sixth-day (ch4); the card arrived on a Fifth-day (ch5). On the seven-named-day week the text uses elsewhere (ch3: Fifth-day + four days = Second-day), Sixth-day to Fifth-day is six or thirteen days, never nine. Vastin's own count (advisory written, nine days of looking, card on the tenth) requires more than ten days plus transit each way, so thirteen is the number that fits both chapters. One word to change.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/review-cold.md:103:- **Observed issue:** (a) ch1 l.235/237: duplicated beat — "turned round to the four of them." / "He looked round at the four of them." (b) ch3 l.17: Lira's reply ("Lira's face did not change. 'Good,' she said…") is run into the end of Rooke's long paragraph, so a listener hears it as Rooke continuing. (c) ch3 l.253–259: "He was back within the half-hour… with one sentence of Withrow's" is followed by a flashback paragraph about Karis reading the other items, and *then* by "She says: *Don't argue with the man…*" — on first hearing the "She says" attaches to Karis, and only "Bracken took his pen" corrects it a sentence late. (d) ch6 l.221–224: a double blank between "he had wanted it asked" and "Cael stood alone in the long room" where either a section rule or a continuous paragraph is needed; the re-establishment reads as a seam. (e) ch5 l.131: "Nine days" does not fit Sixth-day → Fifth-day on the week the book uses, or Vastin's "tenth day"; "thirteen" fits both.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/review-cold.md:105:- **Proposed scope:** Punctuation, paragraph breaks and one word. Delete one of the two Ephram sentences; break Lira's reply into its own paragraph; move "While he was gone Karis read the other four items aloud…" *before* "He was back within the half-hour," and open the Withrow sentence with "Bracken gave it to them exactly as he had been given it. 'She says: …'"; replace the double blank in ch6 with a section rule (or drop "Cael stood alone in the long room" and run on); change "Nine days" to the number that matches the calendar. No prose beyond that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/review-cold.md:106:- **Strength to preserve:** The cadence of the Withrow sentence itself ("leave a chair at the table so that he can come round and sit on our side of it"), Rooke's seeding speech as a single unbroken block, and the two-column Log entry that "nine days" sits under — "Long enough for an answer. Too long for an easy one." is exactly right and should not be touched; only the number above it should.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:51:Cael wrote the toast down within the hour, word for word, at the corner of the long table in the old volume's last leaves. Then he sat over it for a while and gave it a few lines of its own underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:269:He stayed. The light was nearly gone. He sat down on the edge of the oak round, with the four masts standing round him in the dusk, and took Hesk's book out of his coat and wrote in it on his knee, because this one would not keep till he got home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:49:The crowd did not like it. By the third refusal they had begun to mutter. By the fourth somebody at the back called out, "Fight him, then!", and somebody else laughed, and the noise came back off the wall twice over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:51:The ganger did not mutter. After the fourth refusal he stood in the middle of the floor with his hands on his hips, breathing a little harder than he had been, and looked at Cael for a long while. Then he grinned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:245:The standings were read from the guild hall steps at dusk on the Second-day, with the delegations standing in the square below in their colours and the lamps being lit along the porch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:283:*First meet. The companions are winning in brackets with their names on them. I'm winning in a category invented before the categories. Everyone's being measured. I'm being — accommodated. Filed under: things the season will have to answer.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/review-cold.md:15:The person who matters is Cael, but Lira is the heart of the first half, and the movement is honest enough to let her be: her grin at the closed seam, "Third one someday", the thumbnail marks in the chair wax. Brom is the surprise; he is given the best single piece of advice in the book ("Like somebody with something in his pocket") and the best refusal to perform (the hard chair, "That was horrible").
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/review-cold.md:86:Observation to keep: the sixth question, the brace, "Records of what? Ask it," and the chin dipping over a receipt. The prepared refusal is never spent and the page knows that is the point.
