editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:97:"That's the correction," he said. "Don't answer the front foot. Answer the back one. You'll get it right one time in three this week and two in three next week and you'll stop thinking about it by the first meet." He tipped the lath at him. "Now watch this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:119:At the water break the squad drifted to it, and Rooke lifted the stone off the stacks and let them look. Profiles. Forty-one of them, one to a sheet, a fighter to a page: every name entered at the first meet in the Iron and Copper brackets, with a Path, a rank, a house, a record where the house had one, and down the margin in Rooke's hand a verdict of three words or fewer. *Quick hands, slow feet.* *Honest; telegraphs left.* *Lose to her once and she studies you.* *Hits above his rank.* One sheet had only a question mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-19.md:141:He knew at once how it had happened. It was the trade. Nine days ago he and Brom had swapped for a single Sixth-day, his hour on the timber for Brom's later hour on the stone, one week only, on a half-sheet he had carried to the counter himself. Somebody making up the new week's issue had copied from the wrong line of the ledger, from the traded week instead of the standing one, and carried a single week's exception forward as if it were the rule. It was the most ordinary mistake an office could make. A wing that issued paper for four hundred people must make it twice a week, and mend it whenever somebody came to the counter to complain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/review-editorial.md:55:2. **Lira's hip ten days old.** ch51 l. 9 "Ten days. The oil on Fiske's boards had been ten days ago." Semifinal the ninth (M6 ruling) → the nineteenth: verified. "Nine days" of no sparring from the tenth (M6): verified. Four days off after the final (ch53 l. 11): verified against the packet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/REPAIR-BRIEF.md:57:1. **ch5 ¶131.** "Nine days" becomes "Thirteen days". The response went down on d12, and the card came up on d25.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-003/AUTHORSHIP.md:44:- the counter and Bracken's pouch;
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-002/pre-repair/chapter-12.md:89:He treated Cael's refusals the way a good steward treats an expense. He did not try to stop them, but let each one happen and made sure it cost something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-002/pre-repair/chapter-12.md:141:He held it. He went on refusing, on worse ground than before, with the racks at his shoulder and the ropes closing, and he paid for every refusal on the contested boards in a way he had not paid in the first two exchanges. The yard-master's steps came closer together. Twice Cael had to turn hard off his left foot to keep out of the old man's reach, and the second time something in the back of his left calf drew tight, a long thin pull from the knee to the heel, like a cord being wound. He felt it and noted it and kept moving. He did not burst. He did not touch. He gave the panel a third exchange in which a boy kept himself alive on two-thirds of a floor and did nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-002/pre-repair/chapter-12.md:153:He refused at a steady pace for the first part of the glass, steadier than before, the same short sideways step every time, so that the old man's short forward steps fell into time with it, step and refusal, step and refusal, like two men sawing a log. The yard-master did not resist it. A siege had its own rhythm, and he had no reason to break it. Cael watched the light on the floor and watched the old man's feet and counted, and on the fourth beat of the count he made his refusal a fraction short.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-01.md:35:He knew better than to push. He had learned the shape of Karis's *not yet* across a whole winter. It was not a refusal. It was a date she had not set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:133:He did not hurry, and he did not look up at the crowd, not once. He unlocked the case and opened the book and read the last entry and looked at the frame and checked one against the other, with the rain darkening the shoulders of his coat, and shut the book and locked the case and went and stood against the wall with his hands behind his back. When the hour was over he took his keys and left. Cael thought it was a fine piece of ordinary work, and he wrote it down that night in the old volume's last leaves, in four words, because the old volume was running out.
