editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:19:"Against the figure." Karis turned back to the record itself and ran her finger down the exhibition leaf. "The record posted at the first bell. Enrollee, against the quarry ganger: twenty-three. Enrollee, against the Bronze: twenty-two." She looked up. "Twenty-two. And the Bronze is a Bronze Rank Three. His own floor is somewhere up around twenty-five. He read the sheet aloud on the guild hall steps, in front of the morning crowd, and did the arithmetic for them. He'd been beaten in three exchanges by a fighter the panel rated below his own tier."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:35:"No." Karis kept her hand on the sheet. "She's telling him that what he's actually angry about is that Cael didn't try hard. And she's telling him she agrees that's what happened. And then she's telling him it isn't her business." She read the last clause again, under her breath. "*Not available from this office.* That's not a refusal. That's a direction. She's pointing out of the window."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-05.md:41:He had been keeping the count of days since the gate at Greyvane, every evening, in the margin of the observation notebook. It was five months and nine days, tonight, since a magistrate had stood up in a crowded hall and read him a ruling. He was fifteen years old. He had added the months up twice on the road against Hesk's last letter, because he no longer trusted any number that only he was keeping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-002/pre-repair/chapter-07.md:47:The guild hall stood at the top of the main square, broad and old, with its windows set deep in stone. Behind it, where the ground fell away toward the river, lay the meet ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-18.md:219:"Letters," said Karis. "Bracken's pouch, both ways, and never the town post. The pouch is sealed at both ends. The town post is a box on a counter." She turned another page, and looked at Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-003/review-cold.md:19:**Which event I expect next.** Norhold. Specifically: Lira drawing Zerin in a semifinal and finding out whether "faster than me *right now*" holds; Cael meeting whoever has read the Auremont woman's page on his resets; Brom against Marek, the Copper who takes people apart at the joins. And the fourth meet, the threshold, and the shoulder having four days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-003/review-cold.md:99:- **Timeline slip at the quay (ch19 L385, L389).** Lira: "And this morning there they were, two lanes over… So all day I've been asking where it went." The coat-rack was the Fifth-day morning (ch15 L249ff); the quay scene is the Seventh-day near midnight, after the exhibitions and the pouch. Two days, not one morning. Also "I've been saving the coat-rack" (L353) reads as if saved for a day. The rest of the week's arithmetic checks out (departure Seventh-day, ten road days, arrival Second-day dusk, hall Third-day morning, draw Fourth-day, brackets Fifth- and Sixth-day, exhibitions Seventh-day; the "fourth day of the silence" and "five days" counts are right).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-19.md:293:Bracken's pouch came up on the hill coach that afternoon, as Bracken had said it would, and found the quarry town, as Bracken had said it generally did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-19.md:335:The letter went behind the observation notebook's front board, where Hesk's letters lived.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-02.md:139:The third meet. By the board, it was a quarry town ten days' drive to the north-west, a name he had never heard and a date in the deep part of the season. There had been nothing on the board about halls. There had been nothing about anything, except a date and a name. Twelve empty weeks behind a counter had given the man the season, read down to the temperature of its rooms, and he had spent one sentence of it, in front of a queue, in the voice he used for the hours.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-02.md:225:He kept it short. Hesk did not like long letters; he said a long letter was a man who had not yet decided what he meant. So Cael wrote what had happened, in the order it had happened, and stopped. The roster had been filed at noon by the registrar, with every name on the enrollment record, his own among them in the registry's spelling. The courier had gone over on the ferry with a cart of turnips. Gault had been issued a case with a handle and was unreasonably pleased about it. The first meet was five weeks out in a wool town two days to the south.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:167:"Whoever goes out for this house goes because the tournament's own charter sends them, read off our own enrollment record, and for no other reason. The registrar will administer that charter as written. I have read that sentence over several times this recess, and I find I don't want to change a word of it." Her eyes stayed on the slow clock over the door while she said it. Cael wrote that down at once, because Withrow's refusals to look were aimed, and after a year he knew roughly where. "The standards, the meet schedule and the first travel roster will be on the covered walk's board when you leave this hall. Coach Rooke has the training calendar. During the inspection I asked you to be, with people watching, what the house had written down that it was. You did it. Do it again. The only difference is that this time the people watching will be everybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:185:Cael read it from the back of the crowd, over heads. The selection standards took the left-hand sheet, in the hand of Bracken's inky-knuckled clerk. The meet schedule took the middle: four towns, four dates, the first of them five weeks out and two days' drive to the south. The travel roster for that first meet hung on the right, eleven lines long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:47:The guild hall stood at the top of the main square, broad and old, with its windows set deep in stone. Behind it, where the ground fell away toward the river, lay the meet ground.
