editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-06.md:274:South of the river, where the road climbed out of the valley toward the wool town and the first meet, the road itself showed as a paler seam laid across the black of the fields.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:235:The three exercises were inside, in the driest hand he owned, every word of them true and none of them complete, each with a tick beside its title and two figures in its margin and an initial on the tab, as the wing marked everything. Something he had never handed in lay against the back board. A slip of paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:67:"So he's scheduled," said Karis. "Under a provision forty years older than any of us. Which means somebody at the first meet has to read it, and work out how to run it, and find out what a demonstration performance is in this century." She closed the transcription. "That's their problem. I've just solved ours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:197:Rooke did not post the first meet's bout targets. Every other week on the calendar board had its targets pinned beside it in Rooke's hand, and the first meet's week had a clean space the size of a sheet where they would go. Bracken's clerk opened a travel file for the delegation and wrote out its cover in full, every name and wagon and stable fee, and left the date line blank. The wagoner came up from the town to look at the axles and went down again without being told which morning to come back. And at the counter on the Fifth-day, when Cael brought the week's coursework, the teaching assistant took it, read the head of it, and laid it in the tray face down, square to the others, and said "Mm," and that was all, as it should have been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:213:The letter went behind the front board of the observation notebook with the other two that lived there: the folded note that had come in the pine case with the pen, on the eleventh of Sowing, and the sheet that had said *Sixteen suits you.* Three now, in the same hand. He sat with the notebook shut on all three before he put it away, and did not write in the Log what it cost him to have them there together, because some lines in a ledger were better left as a space.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-56.md:87:"Nine days he's been on this hill, and not a soul has played to him. Withrow stood still on purpose. Bracken's file stood up without anybody holding it. Rooke signed his sheets and went back to his cohort. Yesterday Gault ran three hours of true instruments and wrote down a flat line and stood up for it. The whole house has spent nine days being exactly what it is, as hard as it can." He spread his hands on the table. "So picture me walking in there and playing to him. Then I'm the only performer on the whole bluff, and I've spoilt the single thing this house has managed to let him see."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-56.md:95:Karis took the grey notebook out of her satchel, opened it at a fresh page, and wrote, in her small upright hand, *Refusal in the open. Reason: good. Also convenient.* She turned it round so that he could read it, and he nodded, and she closed the book again with the air of somebody filing a document in a box that nobody would be allowed to open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-56.md:205:Four true answers lay to hand. Three would have opened every door he had kept bolted all his life. The fourth was complete and honest and opened none of them, and it was waiting for him, written two years ago inside a notebook's front board for some reason that had nothing to do with today.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:59:Some of it was. Last night, at the window, before anything had happened, he had written the leaf in Hesk's book: that he did not think he could go on not-doing it in front of someone who could kill him by accident, and *Write it down before, not after.* That was the wish, in his own hand, a day ahead of the thing, which was one of the two things the third line had always asked for. And every price he had ever paid was in the old volume: days of quiet after each reach, while the rest of him moved over to make room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-03.md:95:"That's the correction," he said. "Don't answer the front foot. Answer the back one. You'll get it right one time in three this week and two in three next week and you'll stop thinking about it by the first meet." He tipped the lath at him. "Now watch this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-03.md:117:At the water break the squad drifted to it, and Rooke lifted the stone off the stacks and let them look. Profiles. Forty-one of them, one to a sheet, a fighter to a page: every name entered at the first meet in the Iron and Copper brackets, with a Path, a rank, a house, a record where the house had one, and down the margin in Rooke's hand a verdict of three words or fewer. *Quick hands, slow feet.* *Honest; telegraphs left.* *Lose to her once and she studies you.* *Hits above his rank.* One sheet had only a question mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-14.md:205:"The quarry town," he said. "Ten days by the hill roads, and the post up there is a matter of faith." He laid the page on its pile. "So I'll send a pouch after you. The records office keeps one for the hill coach, which goes up on the Second-day and the Sixth-day, and it will find you at the quarry town if the hill coach finds the quarry town, which it generally does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-14.md:207:He took a canvas pouch from under the counter and laid it on the wood. It was grey and worn soft, with the academy's seal stamped on the flap and a brass ring for the lock. "Certified copies of anything the meet's clerks want from us. The chancellor's letters. And yours, if anybody writes to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-14.md:211:"Letters." Bracken looked at him over the counter with an expression of great patience. "Your grandfather writes, I believe, at a considerable rate. A letter sent to the town post at the quarry town sits in a box on a counter with forty others, where anybody who likes can read the outside of it. The pouch is locked here and unlocked by Seln at the far end, and what's inside it stays sealed until it reaches the hand it's addressed to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-14.md:213:He put the pouch back under the counter. "I don't suppose anybody on the circuit cares who writes to you. I simply prefer that they shouldn't have the chance to find out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-14.md:217:"It's a pouch," said Bracken, and went back to his sanding.
