editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/BOOK_MAP.md:66:13. **First meet, the wool town (Ch3).** Recognitions (the transcript; the validator's "adjacent to it"). Lira's Iron debut against the Stone Path. Brom's two Copper bouts. Karis's fieldwork. Ephram watched. Cael against the quarryman ("Story undersold you") and the Bronze. Second of six. Rooke's review.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/BOOK_MAP.md:233:- **Qualifying year (convocation to the year's turn).** Day 0 is convocation. The roster is filed on day 4; the memorandum arrives inside the week; Withrow signs on the fourth evening of drafting; the card is printed after about nine days of silence. **The first meet** (the wool town, two days south) falls about five weeks after convocation. **The second** (the mill town, a week west, first hard frost) comes about three weeks later. **The third** (the quarry town, ten days north-west, cold halls) is "eleven weeks" after Seln's counter sentence. **Qualified** comes on the road home. **The fourth meet** (the confluence, two days north of Ostrand) is at the year's turn, in the deep cold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/BOOK_MAP.md:236:- **Tournament (T = day from the opening).** T1 procession and locking. T2–T4 opening rounds; Silver filings post on T3; the Compact row is charted on T4. About T5 Lira's quarterfinal; about T8 the Zerin semifinal (eleven days before the trial). Brom's semifinal and final run in the second week (the Copper bracket finishes early; see §12 C14). Ivenne about T9, Karis's semifinal about T11. The ring goes up in the second week; the Shield captain bout about T7; **Vastin sits about T10** (the chair held nine days); the duelist about T10. **Rest day T11 = the birthday** (recommend the eleventh of Sowing; §12 C7) [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05: OWNER-pending — the #35 update offers moving the birthday to "the third of Reaping", in which case B5's recollection follows; default stays the eleventh of Sowing; never place the date beside a season word]. Team trial T12. Filing T15. Acceptance and convening T16. Ring conversation T18. Third place T18. **Finals day T20.** Panel overnight T20–21. Meeting T22. Closing ceremony T23. Road home seventeen days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/BOOK_MAP.md:237:- **[M6-redated 2026-10-05 — coordinator ruling; governs over the line above from T10 on.]** M6 (ch33) defers the exhibition ring by a west-tower notice on T5; the Copper final clears the main floor on T9 morning; the crews raise the ring on the night of T10, complete by dawn T11. Rooke's "four days … seven" (M5 ch32) is dead on the page and is not restated. Protected items 22–23 fix the order captain → duelist → rest day → trial ("Tomorrow I find out…"). New spine: **T11 Shield captain** (first Silver exhibition, main floor's ring). **T14 duelist** ("three days later"); **Vastin sits T14**, between the bouts' business; the chair was held T1–T13, so Seln's line reads "held for him for **thirteen** days" (not §10-protected). **T15 rest day = the birthday** (date still OWNER-pending #35; never beside a season word). **T16 team trial.** T17–T18 the empty board; **T19 filing**, the board at dusk; **T20 convening**; **T21 third place**, the ring rebuilt at midnight; T22 the ring "two days out"; T23 eve; **T24 finals day**; panel overnight T24–25; T25 the morning after and the meeting; **T26 closing.** Road home seventeen days. The office's "three weeks" (M5 ch29, ch32) stands as the round figure for the span; no later text states the tournament's length in days. Drop §6's "eleven days before the trial" for Zerin's semifinal (it was T8 against a T16 trial).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/BOOK_MAP.md:247:| M1 | Inventory in the old volume: six fragments, each with its standing cost and deployment rule; the anomaly; the stamp [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05: mirror B4 ch62's inventory a year on — Wind six free on the sprung oak, the landing beat unshortened; Pressure held back at the plate, on the Ardenmere record not the wing's, "four blows a sitting" to the right shoulder; the Iron read eleven of twelve at the fourth weight; Compression "nowhere on any record", "nobody official has ever seen it"; Ember four instants on the copper pot; Shadow deployment none, rent from waking, drifts in the gaps, the second year of "nowhere yet"; the anomaly's three words "Still open. Still real. Patience."] | — |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/BOOK_MAP.md:304:- **The yard-master (M2; mill-guild hall; crane-beam light; tool racks on the west flank).** Siege by paid-for inches; the floor shrinks to two-thirds. The answer is the light he has stopped seeing. The fifth exchange is bought on purpose, as the doctrine's tax. Cost: the left calf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/BOOK_MAP.md:361:20. (Ch3 close) *First meet. The companions are winning in brackets with their names on them. I'm winning in a category invented before the categories. Everyone's being measured. I'm being — accommodated. Filed under: things the season will have to answer.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/BOOK_MAP.md:434:- **C6 · Hesk's volume.** Ch24 calls it "a birthday gift in midwinter … not been opened past its fourth leaf all season," which contradicts Ch14 (the rest-day gift, inventoried in the new volume, with Ch15's log in it). B6 Ch1 has the road home "closed in the old volume." **Plan:** the year's ledger goes in Hesk's volume (Ch24); the road's daily entries go in the old volume's last leaves (satisfying B6 Ch1); drop "midwinter" and "fourth leaf."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/BOOK_MAP.md:437:- **C9 · Shadow-adjacent's age.** Ch14 "sixteen months" and Ch19 "sixteen months ago" against B4 Ch24 "Sixteen weeks" at Book 4 close. **Plan:** "a year, near enough" / "since the records hall" / no count [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05: replaces "since last spring" (season word); in the edition the fragment was acquired on the stair on B4 d149/150, was "eighteen days old" at the eleventh of Sowing (ch37) and about seven weeks old at the B4 close — the source's "Sixteen weeks" is superseded]. (B6 Ch15's "two and a half years" is Book 6's problem; flag downstream.)
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-04.md:67:"So he's scheduled," said Karis. "Under a provision forty years older than any of us. Which means somebody at the first meet has to read it, and work out how to run it, and find out what a demonstration performance is in this century." She closed the transcription. "That's their problem. I've just solved ours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-04.md:203:Rooke did not post the first meet's bout targets. Every other week on the calendar board had its targets pinned beside it in Rooke's hand, and the first meet's week had a clean space the size of a sheet where they would go. Bracken's clerk opened a travel file for the delegation and wrote out its cover in full, every name and wagon and stable fee, and left the date line blank. The wagoner came up from the town to look at the axles and went down again without being told which morning to come back. And at the counter on the Fifth-day, when Cael brought the week's coursework, the teaching assistant took it, read the head of it, and laid it in the tray face down, square to the others, and said "Mm," and that was all, as it should have been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-04.md:219:The letter went behind the front board of the observation notebook with the other two that lived there: the folded note that had come in the pine case with the pen, on the eleventh of Sowing, and the sheet that had said *Sixteen suits you.* Three now, in the same hand. He sat with the notebook shut on all three for a moment before he put it away, and did not write in the Log what it cost him to have them there together, because some lines in a ledger were better left as a space.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-05.md:51:What would it cost him? He made himself ask it, because a man who does not price his own advice ends by giving the expensive kind for nothing. And the honest answer was: nothing. The advisory recommended no action and took no side. It quoted only what anyone could buy at a stationer's, and its one refusal was to find words in a charter that the charter had never held. He had built a career on the principle that the text governed, and had held to it in rooms with far worse air in them than this one. He would not let it bend for one enrollee, in either direction. It cost him nothing, yet. He made a note of the *yet* in his head, where he kept such notes, and did not examine it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-05.md:61:The ordinary silence after an advisory had a shape he knew as well as he knew the porter and his pigeons: an acknowledgment from the receiving office, a file marked closed somewhere below with a clerk's initials, a line in the weekly digest. He had read that silence ten thousand times. This was not it. The advisory was received. He knew it was received, because the dispatch slip in his coat said so. After that, nothing. No acknowledgment came back up the chain. The file did not reappear in the digest. The two letters on the fourth line of the routing slip were not on any other slip that crossed his table in the next nine days, and he looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-05.md:73:Bracken laid it on the records-hall counter at the third bell, square to the edge, with no covering letter, because there had been none. It was the first meet's official card, printed by the host's office on thick cream stock in two columns, and it listed every delegation entered at the wool town in the order of their registration, with each house's competitors in a block beneath its name. Halcenvane's block was the ninth, and it ran five lines. The first four looked like any house's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-05.md:115:"It's not kindness. If you go lame at the first meet I lose my sessions." He walked on, and then said over his shoulder, without turning, "The second-year entry. Every Iron fighter in that hall will have it. Watch the heel."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-05.md:121:He wrote it at the rail of the Crown yard's top tier that afternoon, with the observation notebook on his knee and the porter below him chalking the first meet's date onto the long board in letters a hand high.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-001/pre-repair/chapter-05.md:131:*Nine days, Karis says, between the response going down and the card coming up. Long enough for an answer. Too long for an easy one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.md:91:- **Not repaired, deferred to the owner (unchanged by r1, per the brief):** "The tournament was still nine days off" (ch27 l.293); "fifty-odd feet" for 52×48 (ch30 l.191); Vastin's "a week before the opening" on a Fourth-day; three bout-losers "looking at his hands". None is a clarity fault a reader would stop on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-004/recheck-r1.md:74:**Calendar.** Arrival at the confluence "on the Third-day at dusk" (ch20 l.209) = d107; day one d108 Fourth-day, day two d109 Fifth-day; the record and the convenor's reading "the next morning"/"at the meet's close" = d110; "Two days later, on the First-day" home (ch24 l.73) = d112 (112 mod 7 = 0 → First-day). Only Halcenvane weekdays appear (Second-, Third-, Fourth-, Fifth-, First-day). No English weekday, no month name, no month order, no season word: the scan's three hits are false positives ("no spring to buy the quarter-beat back" is the floor; "May it keep on trying" is the verb; "No." is the word). "The deep cold", "the cold term", "the year's turn", "the recess", "the melt" are the ledger's own terms. Daeva's window is not in this movement.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-43.md:149:"Every one of those twenty," said Lira, "we won knowing everything. That's how we've always done it, you and me. Everything on the table, every time, and then we go and use it on each other. There's been nothing better in either of our lives these two years. I mean that." She tightened the knot with her thumb. "And on the nineteenth it's worth nothing. Less than nothing. You know my leg, and since yesterday you know it worse. I know where your iron gives out. If we go on, we'll spend nine days guessing what the other one's guessing about what we're guessing, and we'll walk out onto those boards playing chess." She looked up at him. "Neither of us fights chess."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-43.md:185:"Nine days," said Lira. "Nobody talks about hips."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-43.md:297:"Down *today*." Brom's voice stayed low. Shouting would have been easier to bear. "Nine days from now you're going to be on a floor for three hours. Gault, and the panel, and an Archmarshal, and whoever else they've brought with them. And somewhere in that gallery there'll be a person whose whole trade is noticing when a thing isn't where it should be. And you've just shown me that the one thing you can't steer goes thin when all of you is busy." He did not move out of the way. "What do you call a three-hour evaluation, then?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-43.md:315:*Hold. First. Before growth, before control, before the five. Nine days of it on every floor I stand on, and then three hours of it in front of the panel. Everything else stands on this, and if this goes, everything on top of it falls.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-51.md:47:Karis had the marbled book open beside her bowl. When Lira sat down, Karis reached under her chair without a word and brought up the two practice batons, still tied together in the middle with Lira's own hip bandage, in the ugly knot Lira had pulled tight nine days ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-51.md:57:"Nine days," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-51.md:59:"Nine days," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-51.md:63:For nine days they had not sparred, or talked about the bout, or watched each other cross a room. It had not been politeness. Without the silence, each of them would have spent nine days building a fight out of the other one's wound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-51.md:67:And he knew hers. If either of them had spent the last nine days thinking about the other's number, they would have walked out today to fight a ledger instead of a person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-51.md:143:For nine days he had kept the read at its thinnest layer, because the reach at depth and the hold were paid out of one purse, and the purse was shallow. This afternoon, though, no one on the bluff would spare him a look. Every eye in the yard, the Archmarshal's among them, would be on the oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-51.md:269:She knew his number, and he knew hers. And for nine days each of them had refused to build one single thought on the other's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-48.md:35:*Things that are going to happen whether I schedule them or not.* He had written that three nights ago, in Hesk's book, under the trial. He had thought then that he was being dry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-48.md:45:Bracken brought the west tower's half of it to the long table at midday, with the pouch under his arm and the expression of a man who has been given a very good piece of gossip by a colleague and intends to hand it on with every courtesy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-48.md:95:Rooke was at the back of the crowd with his hands deep in his coat pockets, and said nothing. Seln had gone up three steps of the west tower's porch, out of the press, with the travel pouch held flat against his chest, and was reading the board from there with exactly the expression he used for a revised timetable.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-48.md:113:Withrow sat at the head of it with a pot of tea at her elbow. Rooke had the chair at her right, Bracken the chair at her left with the pouch on his knees. Gault was at the far end peeling an apple with great attention. Ephram sat between Brom and Karis. Seln had the last chair, the one nearest the door, where he always sat when he sat with them at all.
