editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-26.md:5:Cael found it there on the morning of the posting, in the clerk's square hand among two dozen others: the Iron Skin's name, an arrow, and four lines higher on the Copper sheet a Blade third-year's. He stood in front of it. Brom had beaten Merrick by refusing to turn, and the whole cohort had been feeding him short ever since to find out whether the refusal would hold, and it had held, mostly, for three weeks. Tarn was the best short feeder in the cohort. He was the one who had found the left heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-53.md:5:The house was quiet in the way a house is quiet when everybody in it has gone somewhere else at once. The squad was at the hired hall. Rooke was with them. Gault had gone to the healers' corridor at the Concourse to look at the station that would stand at the ring's north break. Bracken was at the west tower with the pouch. On the stair the keeper's cat lay in a square of sun, and down in the courtyard somebody's washing moved on a line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-53.md:189:He wrote the minute into Hesk's book while they watched, under the ring, in his best hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-53.md:231:He wrote it in the old volume, in its very last leaves, in the smallest hand he owned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-53.md:253:He opened Hesk's book under the lamp, past the ring and the minute, to the next clean leaf, and wrote slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:39:Nothing happened for long enough for him to begin turning the key over in his fingers, wondering whether this was a refusal. He did not hang it back. The porter had not said how long to wait, and a rule that did not say how long usually meant longer than you thought.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/review-editorial.md:138:Six confirmed plus the anomaly ("The still place from session nine, untouched in its three words"). No new capability. **Reading the medium** is a retasking of the Iron-adjacent read and the page says so ("the read that had read weight all his life … He did not reach for her this time. He reached the other way"): twelve late in ch47; the squall's front early twice with the powder wharf's masts humming (ch50); ten in ten "a half-beat before it reached him, off the air, with her body put away" (ch52), timed on Hesk's clock in Karis's column and bounded by her in the right words ("At Iron. At Lira. … I don't know what it is at anything else, and nor do you."). The price appears only as narrowing, twice, with Karis's mark beside it "for things she meant to come back to". The Gold-scale price is not known (§3B item 9 ✓). **The bank** is named as the packet's B4-reconciliation requires: Wind past six "and the bill comes in the morning"; the push "from the Bronze at Ardenmere, at a fighting load, past the four blows a sitting" (Feryn, by the audio rule); "Reydan's give, on a scored floor in front of a crowd, which it hasn't been since the bout at Ardenmere, and that bout's the only paper it's on" (not "first time in its existence"); "Karis's spark past the two contacts we allow in public"; the read "as far down as it'll go". The minute is written before, in Hesk's book under the ring entry, and the B4 three-rule minute is restated in Karis's line ("Nothing made to happen. Only inside a fight somebody else begins. The cost on paper first."). **Shadow sealed**, priced aloud by Lira as presence-suppression and movement-masking without those words, and kept for the debt; "a year ago, near enough" (C9). §7.1's line is verbatim.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/review-editorial.md:215:| ch48 s3 | "which was where she had been at the cheap slate in the circuit house at Ardenmere, and at every board since" · "Ephram had been standing exactly so at every board since the cold term, and had never said a word about it to anybody" · "Seln had gone up three steps of the west tower's porch, out of the press, with the travel pouch held flat against his chest, and was reading the board from there" · "She had looked at this board somewhere earlier, alone, and then decided whose it was. It was theirs. She had given it to them." · "the four bracket finals stood waiting, the titles the continent had fought over longest, set lower and printed smaller than a bout whose result would go into nobody's standings at all" | "Lira at his left shoulder where she had stood at every board since Ardenmere" · "Ephram at the outside … having appointed himself to that job at some point in the winter without mentioning it to anyone" · "Seln was at the colonnade's edge, forty feet off, holding the delegation pouch, reading the board with the rest of the city" · "Withrow was not there … the chancellor had decided this particular reading belonged to the five of them" · "The finals below it, the four bracket titles of the oldest competition on the continent, in smaller type than a bout worth zero points" (the whole scene runs the source's beat order: walk down by custom, the crowd's noise, the line, the formation, Seln, Rooke, Withrow, the bracketed word, the protected line) |
