editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-004/pre-repair/chapter-26.md:147:He signed the report, sealed it with the office's plain seal, and put it in the pouch for the north. The pouch would go down the bluff on the morning coach, past the two coats at the foot of the road. They would not look at it. Nobody ever looked at the pouch; that was what a pouch was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-47.md:135:He did not hurry, and he did not look up at the crowd, not once. He unlocked the case and opened the book and read the last entry and looked at the frame and checked one against the other, with the rain darkening the shoulders of his coat, and shut the book and locked the case and went and stood against the wall with his hands behind his back. When the hour was over he took his keys and left. Cael thought it was the best piece of ordinary work he had seen anybody do all sitting, and he wrote it down that night in the old volume's last leaves, in four words, because the old volume was running out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-005/review-editorial.md:109:9. **Minor and optional.** "The tournament was still nine days off" (ch27 L293) reads as "the city is nine days off"; the opening is sixteen days off by the author's own calendar — "the city was still nine days off" is exact. Vastin's "a week before the opening" on a Fourth-day is eight days on the author's table; nobody will notice. The ring "fifty-odd feet, a little longer one way than the other" (ch30 L191) — 48 feet is not "fifty-odd"; "fifty feet or so one way and a little less the other" matches the planned 52 × 48 without stating it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-52.md:49:Cael wrote the toast down within the hour, word for word, at the corner of the long table in the old volume's last leaves. Then he sat over it for a while and gave it a few lines of its own underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-52.md:269:He stayed. The light was nearly gone. He sat down on the edge of the oak round, with the four masts standing round him in the dusk, and took Hesk's book out of his coat and wrote in it on his knee, because this one would not keep till he got home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-007/review-editorial.md:109:**Ruling: approve.** Checks: he is "the Archmarshal" throughout (B4's title); no age; "forty years" twice as his own rounding (#17); he travels himself by the post road with one bag; his day book carries *Norhold, on observation. Not requested by any office.*; the two notes are in his register and state no conclusion ("He did not know what the boy was. … It was not a finding. It was only the place where the findings stopped."); no threat, no side-switch, no Seln, no mechanism, no maker. The faction above him appears only as a shape (a chair held at his grade, a steward who knew it, three nouns under a seal), which is what §11 allows. The steward who greets him "This way, Archmarshal" is a face but not the faction's; Vastin's refusal to think past "had been told it by somebody" is in character and keeps the boundary. "He knew Ilsev's back as he knew his own handwriting" is supported by B4 ch45 (four nights of inns on the same road; "he did not take notes on colleagues"). His choosing to sit in the held chair, and noticing that he chose it, is the best new beat in the cutaway and is exactly the "five-stage escalation" §11 permits. Seln's added sentence ("The stewards' book at the east stair carried his grade against that seat on the first morning of the sitting. His own office's notice of travel reached the west tower yesterday at noon.") does not over-shape the faction: it speaks at a table the same fact M5 planted on Havel's sheet and Vastin's day book (the seat held at his grade from T1), and it is Seln's trade (M5: "One only had to find the right clerk"). Approve, and ledger it.
