editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:57:"Nine days," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:59:"Nine days," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:63:For nine days they had not sparred, or talked about the bout, or watched each other cross a room. It had not been politeness. Without the silence, each of them would have spent nine days building a fight out of the other one's wound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:67:And he knew hers. If either of them had spent the last nine days thinking about the other's number, they would have walked out today to fight a ledger instead of a person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:143:For nine days he had kept the read at its thinnest layer, to spare the hold. This afternoon, though, no one on the bluff would spare him a look. Every eye in the yard, the Archmarshal's among them, would be on the oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:269:She knew his number, and he knew hers. And for nine days each of them had refused to build one single thought on the other's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-24.md:5:He did not write the argument out again. It was in Karis's minute, signed by four people, and the minute was pinned inside the back board where anyone reading the Log would find it first. He gave the rules one line each, the way he would have listed a fighter's three habits at the top of a page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-41.md:139:"No," she agreed, and nearly smiled. "It's an answer, though. I'm not giving you the number either, because you'll sit on the tier counting, and I'll feel you counting, and it'll be like having a clock in my hip." She shifted the cloth again. "The hip's fine. It's tight. It's been tight since the foundry. Tomorrow it's going to hurt, and the day after tomorrow it's going to hurt more, and then it's going to have nine days, and that's how it's going to be. Nobody's to talk about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-41.md:185:"I'm not thinking about the nineteenth," he said. "Not once. Not until I've beaten him. Every time I think about her, I think about how she moves, and then I'm in a bout with her in my head, and I'm losing to Merrick in real life while I do it." He set his heel again. "So I've decided. Merrick first. Merrick only. If I win, I'll think about her for nine days and not sleep. If I lose, it doesn't matter."
