editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-16.md:31:He came along it at a clerk's pace, neither quick nor slow. He passed the turned post, where he had stood on every one of Cael's mornings in this hall for nine days, and he did not slow at it. He did not glance at it. Most of all, and this was the thing that went through Cael like cold water, he did not look down. Below him, alone in an empty hall at an hour when nothing was scheduled, sat the most stared-at student on the bluff. Every porter and lecturer and first-year who had ever crossed that gallery would have glanced down at him from pure habit, the way anybody glances at the one lit window in a dark street.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-16.md:199:*For the record: he is better at this than I am. I've been at it nine days with a pencil and a lamp. He has been at it every hour he has spent on this hill, and for years before that, I'd guess, and in all that time I have found him put a foot wrong once, on a morning I built for the purpose.*
