editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:87:"Nine days he's been on this hill, and not a soul has played to him. Withrow stood still on purpose. Bracken's file stood up without anybody holding it. Rooke signed his sheets and went back to his cohort. Yesterday Gault ran three hours of true instruments and wrote down a flat line and stood up for it. The whole house has spent nine days being exactly what it is, as hard as it can." He spread his hands on the table. "So picture me walking in there and playing to him. Then I'm the only performer on the whole bluff, and I've spoilt the single thing this house has managed to let him see."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:95:Karis took the grey notebook out of her satchel, opened it at a fresh page, and wrote, in her small upright hand, *Refusal in the open. Reason: good. Also convenient.* She turned it round so that he could read it, and he nodded, and she closed the book again with the air of somebody filing a document in a box that nobody would be allowed to open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:205:Four true answers lay to hand. Three would have opened every door he had kept bolted all his life. The fourth was complete and honest and opened none of them, and it was waiting for him, written two years ago inside a notebook's front board for some reason that had nothing to do with today.
