editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/BOOK_MAP.md:197:Track injuries forward: Cael's shoulder after the baseline (3 days) and again after the evaluation; the hip after the evaluation (3 days minimum); the Compression bone hum after the pin (day 4). Lira's hip from the semifinal (nine days into the final) and after the final (4 days off). Brom's knee from the final and the strapped knuckles. Rule from the seat: a learning fight leaves a correction that can be attempted again, and a loss buys knowledge, not a disguised victory.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/review-editorial.md:105:2. **Location:** Chapters 22–23, from Karis's four-column comparison through Cael's signed three-rule procedure; secondarily chapter 28's warm/cold audit explanation. **Observed issue:** The measured sentence spread is narrow (SD 11.78 versus about 26), the median is 9 versus 11, and analytical paragraphs average 40.43 words; consecutive gift/floor/weapon/fence/bill metaphors can briefly obscure the governing rule for the younger lens. **Effect on the reader:** The words are easy, but the hierarchy of the reasoning can become harder to retain aloud; adult readers are likely to admire the parts while a thirteen-year-old may need to reconstruct their relationship. **Proposed scope:** A hand edit of only the densest analytical paragraphs: join clusters of clipped sentences that form one thought, break paragraphs at true changes of claim, and remove at most one redundant paraphrase where Cael's numbered rules already carry it. Do not alter protected wording, the four distinct moral voices, or any developed fight. **Strength to preserve:** The debate's refusal to call the acquisition clean, Cael's explicit desire, Brom's concrete weapon argument, and Seln's memorable audit-by-warmth craft.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/review-editorial.md:27:**An adult genre reader.** The refusal to score is delivered by the system's own honest men, which is the right shape for this series: the machine's best instruments say "cannot" before its worst rooms say "threat". The ending's *entered for formal proceedings* carries the dread without a villain. The developed exchanges are earned — nothing in E2–E4 repeats, and the Gold's learning (she stops being at the ends of her lanes, ch55:119) is the kind of adaptation the owner asked for. Reservations: the climax's silence told secondhand; the sheer density of "afterwards" framing in ch56; and one piece of the circle's logic (rule three) that the prose asserts and then undercuts in the same paragraph (see §4.3).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/review-editorial.md:71:B4 ch23's signed minute (edition `chapter-23.md:119`): *Three: the full cost to be written beforehand, including C.'s stated wish for the fragment.* B5 ch53:181 restates it: *The cost on paper first.* In the ring (ch56:57–61) Cael goes down the three lines, finds no page that holds the price "at this weight, in the middle of a bout, in front of a city", and then: "He did it anyway. He did it with his eyes open, knowing which line it was, **and that it had held.**" Read aloud, "that it had held" claims rule three held immediately after the paragraph has shown where it did not. Karis's line the next night is the honest one — "the third line held too, as far as it went" (ch59:93) — and the costed amendment (protected 32) is the right remedy for exactly that gap. Two things make the author's reading defensible and should be on the page: (a) B4's rule three is about the **wish** as much as the price, and the wish *was* written beforehand, in protected 28 on the eve (ch53:243–249: "I don't think I can. Write it down before, not after."); (b) every price ever seen was in the old volume. So rule three held in B4's own terms on the wish and on every known cost, and failed only on the price at Gold weight. **Ruling:** accept the author's reading; repair ch56:59–61 so the paragraph recalls last night's leaf as the beforehand page and names the weight as the one thing no page could hold ("knowing which line it was, and how far it held"). The costed rule then reads as the repair of a named gap rather than a cover for a broken one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/review-editorial.md:166:**Does the ending land as a book ending?** Yes. The last movement closes every thread §1 says to close (the match; the rating; third of fourteen; the banner; Fenmark as a wound by Lira's scrapyard line) and opens exactly the ones §1 says to open (the rematch; "the people who want this to stop"; Brom's review; the convening's question, entered and unscheduled). The two poles of the internal arc are both on the page in Cael's hand (protected 21 and 29). The shape of the last chapter — the ledger, the letters, the wall, the bench, the room with no windows, the road thinning the tournament out of them "the way a long walk takes a fever out of a man", the watchers back, the old volume ending at the binding, the protected close on the climb — is a book's ending and not a movement's. The sixth man at the bench is the warmest beat in the book and is given without a word of explanation, which is the rule. The handoff to Book 6 Ch1 is clean: the year's ledger in Hesk's volume, the road closed in the old volume's last leaves (ch60:189–191), the flag unresolved, the watchers resumed, Vastin still the instrument, no season word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/REPAIR-BRIEF.md:21:   find-and-replace applies the owner's choice. Name him **Abbot** once, where Lira first meets him on the board
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/REPAIR-BRIEF.md:50:- the refusal to manufacture an encounter;
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/review-cold.md:11:Cael matters most to me. His unusual gift is inseparable from the habit that can deform him, and the book makes self-scrutiny useful without pretending it makes him innocent. Lira is nearly as compelling: her refusal to explain herself is not emotional blankness, because the prose keeps showing what it costs her to be watched, classified, injured, and underestimated. Karis and Brom are not satellites; each changes what the others can know and do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/review-cold.md:64:1. **Location:** Chapter 22 from Karis's finding through Chapter 24's opening Log entry. **Observed issue:** The conditions, consent problem, refusal to manufacture an encounter, acknowledged desire, and advance accounting are each stated multiple times in closely adjacent forms. **Effect on the reader:** The moral problem remains strong, but its urgency flattens before the first physical turn; a movement-only younger reader may feel the story has paused to reread its own minutes. **Proposed scope:** Make a focused compression across the Chapter 23 conclusion and Chapter 24 opening, removing only explanations the signed minute has already made unambiguous; preserve the later insight that Cael can build either side of an internal argument to win. **Strength to preserve:** The four distinct moral voices, Karis's procedural honesty, Cael's “hats” list, and the refusal to make acquisition clean by rhetoric.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-003/review-editorial.md:60:The movement delivers its planned arc: the watcher is found through bookkeeping, tested, kept in place by a contested group decision, charted from the outside, and then set beside a bracket that mismeasures Lira, Brom, Fiske, and Cael in different ways. Cause and effect are strong. Failed methods produce improved methods; the attention cost produces the late read; the late read produces the bruise; the four-day rest produces two explicit recovery instances; Brom's known seam produces Merrick's plan, and Brom's refusal to redirect produces Rooke's next correction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-62.md:17:He went up to his room and put the sheet inside the notebook's front board, behind the cover, where the other one already lived: the folded note that had come in the pine case with the pen on the eleventh of Sowing. Two sheets now, in the same hand. What it cost him to put the second beside the first, he kept out of the Log. Some lines in a ledger are better left as a space.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-44.md:5:The pouch came up from the river gate at the first bell on the rest day as on every other day, because the post kept no holidays and nor did the office that answered it. He unlocked it at the end of the long table in the common room, with the house still asleep above him, and sorted it as he sorted everything, into the order in which it would be wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-44.md:109:He crossed the shop to the long table. He put the parcel down in front of Cael, and laid a card and an envelope on top of it, and said, "The pouch. This morning's," in exactly the voice he used across the counter at home on the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/AUTHORSHIP.md:11:| Coordinator rulings | Sent with the task and applied over the packet: the #41 calendar (finals T24 with the morning card of the Iron, Silver and Gold finals and the team-trial final, Auremont v the river academy; panel overnight T24–25; morning after and meeting T25; closing T26 with third confirmed; road home seventeen days, Brom's summons on the ninth day; the tournament's length in days never stated). The M9 ring as the packet note gives it. The bank spent by minute only, inside a fight Daeva began; Shadow sealed and unused; Seln never told why. Daeva's Storm vocabulary kept hers and apart from Karis's terms; no theory of Cael. Match format per packet and §8, ratings per #38. Firsts: first notice to say *Gold*, first stability warning. Year's ledger counted a year on from B4 ch62 with no further increments. Brom's LEFT shoulder; Cael's left side hit shoulder to hip. Cutaways held toward the book's ~13%. No new names; no season, month or English weekday names; no metres; no birthday date; no secrets printed; no git; nothing edited outside the seven chapters and this folder. Mid-run note received: M8 closed with twelve line fixes (ch52 "each mast grounded twice"; ch51 colour-guard of two; ch53's "They had kept this night twice before…"). The banner's colour-guard here is two, one at either side; nothing drafted quotes the changed lines. |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-007/review-editorial.md:182:- *Strength to preserve:* Vastin "noticed himself doing it" — the twice-read sheets and his refusal to grow a theory; the folder laid "where nobody was" and the shoe at the ankle bone exactly as written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-49.md:13:Behind the ledger, in a pocket of the back board, was the file's correspondence. Most of it was ordinary paper: acknowledgements, a transfer notice, a slip from a district clerk apologising for a smudge. At the very back, under a brass clip, lay a single folded sheet with the registry's stamp on it. Pinned to it was a narrow docket in Havel's hand: *Correspondence in. Delegation's courier, up. Eighth bell.* It had come up the bluff the evening before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/pre-repair/chapter-26.md:5:Cael found it there on the morning of the posting, in the clerk's square hand among two dozen others: the Iron Skin's name, an arrow, and four lines higher on the Copper sheet a Blade third-year's. He stood in front of it for a while. Brom had beaten Merrick by refusing to turn, and the whole cohort had been feeding him short ever since to find out whether the refusal would hold, and it had held, mostly, for three weeks. Tarn was the best short feeder in the cohort. He was the one who had found the left heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-004/pre-repair/chapter-24.md:7:*Karis's minute is pinned inside the back board. Her six checks are all in it, with the doubts she raised against herself. Anyone reading this should read that first.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-59.md:39:Under the strapping the left side kept up a low steady complaint, like a dog that has decided to go on grumbling all night. The rest of him was not settled at all. All day he had felt the things he carried moving about in him, slowly, the way men move furniture in a house where a great heavy chest has just been carried in. Every one of them leaned a little toward the new weight, and none of them had found its place. Twice on the walk down he had reached for the Wind and got something else. He thought it would be a long while before he could take a step without thinking about it. He had written that in the old volume already, and the healers' account of it, and his own, which was more honest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-59.md:41:He took out Hesk's book and opened it on the rail. There was just enough light. He wrote standing up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-59.md:51:The long table had all of them that night. Gault, and Rooke, and Bracken at the end with the pouch, and Withrow at the head with her cup. The two reserves were wide-eyed at the bottom of it. Seln sat at the far corner, on the margin, where he had sat all sitting. Cael told them the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-59.md:71:There was a little quiet. Then Seln spoke from his corner, in his counter voice, without looking up from the pouch he had open in his lap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-59.md:73:"Wardens threaten. Politicians hint. That was neither." The pouch's strap went through its buckle under his fingers. "That was an honest man spending career capital to tell you the truth a few months early. Treat the warning as real, because it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-59.md:87:The wider table broke up a little after the tenth bell. Rooke and Gault went up the stair arguing about the best week of the year to start a cohort on stone. Bracken took the pouch. Withrow went last. She stood at the door for a moment looking back at the table, as if she were counting something, and then she said goodnight to all of them in one word and was gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-59.md:101:She wrote it while they watched, slowly, in the hand she kept for papers that strangers would read long afterwards, and she wrote it twice, a draft and then a fair copy, with nothing crossed out on the second. Then she turned the fair copy round and pushed it across the table to him, and he copied it into Hesk's book under the minute from two nights before, in his best hand.
