editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-60.md:87:Seln came in last, with the pouch, as he had come in last every evening of the sitting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-60.md:89:He did not go to the end of the board, where the margin was. He did not go to the corner by the door, or stand at the counter with the keeper, or take the stool by the fire where he had sat in other waystations with the pouch on his knee. He came down the room and sat on the bench, in the middle of it, between Lira's boots and Karis's lamp, at the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-60.md:99:*A line on a form says what a man is listed as,* he wrote in the old volume that night, very small, because the old volume had only a few leaves left. *It has no way to notice when he's become something else underneath it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-60.md:103:"Eleven," said Seln, from along the bench, without looking up from the pouch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-60.md:139:The old volume ran out a leaf at a time as the road did. He gave it a few lines a night, small, and most nights they said the same things. *The Wind at three on yard clay. Came when called, twice of three. The left side gone yellow at the edges. The flag stands.* Each night the things he carried had shifted a little further toward the new weight, and each night they had not finished. The healers had said a month. His own reckoning said more. The notice's warning sat at the back of his eyes where he had first read it, and did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-60.md:159:It was dated four days before. Hesk had not had his six pages. He could not have; they were still somewhere on the hill road, in Bracken's pouch. He had written off the broadsides, which reached the coast faster than any pouch. Then he had worked out which road the wagons would take, and which crossroads they would come to on which day, and sent the letter to meet them there. He had been right about all of it. Hesk's answers had always set out before the questions reached him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-60.md:165:He read it again. Then he slid it in behind the old volume's front board, with the other letters that lived there, and ate the stew cold, and did not mind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-60.md:191:On the ferry he wrote in the old volume for the last time, at the foot of its last leaf, until the line ran into the binding and there was no room for a word more. He shut it and put it inside his coat with Hesk's letter. Everything he wrote from now on would go into Hesk's book, and he thought it would be a long while before Hesk's book was full.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-60.md:209:Seln was on the tailboard with the pouch across his knees. He looked up the road toward the gate and said nothing. Cael had learned that this was how he agreed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-58.md:73:He looked at the space for some time. Then he blotted the sheet as it was, and folded it, and sealed it, and laid it on the corner of the table for Bracken's pouch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-58.md:115:Seln was at the door. Nobody had heard him come up. He had the pouch under his arm and he had evidently been there for some of it, and he did not apologise for that, or come in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-58.md:117:"He has kept every rule of a forty-year career," he said, in his counter voice. "If he means to break one for you, the breaking has been paid for somewhere you will never be shown the bill." He shifted the pouch. "Listen accordingly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-58.md:252:For a moment the grey face did something Cael would spend half that night failing to name in Hesk's book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-007/review-cold.md:21:**Which person matters.** Cael, obviously, but the person I am now most curious about is Ilsev, which I did not expect. Vastin is the movement's engine; Ilsev is its conscience. Her "and you may not ask" section and her refusal to take the next thought ("She knew she was not taking it, and she was quite comfortable with that") are the most adult pages in the movement. Havel is the heart. Karis earns her ending. Lira and Brom are the warmth and they never once feel like relief valves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-007/review-cold.md:92:**Reserved disclosures.** Vastin's actual question (the baseline behind the three weeks) is disclosed to the reader in ch45 and withheld from every character; nothing in ch46–50 leaks it. Ilsev's "and you may not ask" and her refusal to take the next thought keep the designation's meaning reserved. Havel's "Nothing has ever come back with a name on it" is reserved from Ilsev (he "could not have said how he knew it"). Karis's "I'm not saying anything about them at all" keeps the directive's two articles from becoming a claim. The movement is disciplined about what it does not say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-007/review-cold.md:114:- **Strength to preserve:** "a coincidence with good posture"; Karis's refusal ("I'm not saying anything about them at all… like a hand coming down on a lid"); the one line on an otherwise empty page; Cael's part 3 ("I'm not going to start doing it his way in the very week the list held").
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-56.md:59:It was on paper. Every reach he had ever made was in the old volume, with what it had cost him afterward: days of quiet while the rest of him moved over to make room, the bill paid in a shut room with nobody watching. He had written every one of them down. He had never written down what it would cost at this weight, in the middle of a bout, in front of a city. Nobody could have. There was no page anywhere that held it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-009/pre-repair/chapter-57.md:221:He wrote all six into Hesk's book that night, one under another, and drew a line beneath them, and wrote under the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-16.md:29:He came along it at a clerk's pace, neither quick nor slow. He passed the turned post, where he had stood on every one of Cael's mornings in this hall for nine days, and he did not slow at it. He did not glance at it. Most of all, and this was the thing that went through Cael like cold water, he did not look down. Below him, alone in an empty hall at an hour when nothing was scheduled, sat the most stared-at student on the bluff. Every porter and lecturer and first-year who had ever crossed that gallery would have glanced down at him from pure habit, the way anybody glances at the one lit window in a dark street.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-16.md:195:*For the record: he is better at this than I am. I've been at it nine days with a pencil and a lamp. He has been at it every hour he has spent on this hill, and for years before that, I'd guess, and in all that time I have found him put a foot wrong exactly once, on a morning I built for the purpose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-003/pre-repair/chapter-19.md:141:He knew at once how it had happened. It was the trade. Nine days ago he and Brom had swapped for a single Sixth-day, his hour on the timber for Brom's later hour on the stone, one week only, on a half-sheet he had carried to the counter himself. Somebody making up the new week's issue had copied from the wrong line of the ledger, from the traded week instead of the standing one, and carried a single week's exception forward as if it were the rule. It was the most ordinary mistake an office could make. A wing that issued paper for four hundred people must make it twice a week, and mend it whenever somebody came to the counter to complain.
