26-editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md-37-Halcenvane stood along its top. The first thing he noticed was that nothing in it had been inherited. Every school he had ever seen had been made out of something older: a road-house, a granary, a lord's barn with a floor laid in it. This one had been built to be what it was, all at once, by somebody who had drawn it first. The roofs all ran at the same pitch. The stone was the same pale stone from end to end. Along the bluff's north lip ran three long halls with their windows set high under the eaves, all on the side the sun came up. One heavy-roofed building had walls thick enough to carry a great weight of something, and he thought it must be books. A separate block sat behind a low wall of its own. And in the very centre, where everything else on the bluff seemed to lean toward it, there was a great hollow ringed on three sides with tiered stone seats.
