editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-1-# Chapter 60 — Seven Things
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-2-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:3:He closed the year that evening in Hesk's book, at the table under the window, with Hesk's steel pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-5-It was not the end of a year by anybody's calendar but his own. But it was near enough a year since he had sat at another window, on the last night of the old term, and written down what was in the till. A thing that has been opened ought to be closed, and the ledger had been open all that time. He turned past the inventory at seventeen, and the trial, and the ring, and the minute, and the pole, and the rule, and the warning, to the next clean leaf. He ruled the margin with the edge of the closing ceremony's printed card, because he had no rule with him. Then he sat a moment before he wrote, with the pen lifted. He did that perhaps once a year and had never written it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-7-*The year, closed.*
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-95-Nobody said a word about it, then or at any board between that one and the bluff, and nobody ever would; but every night, at every board, every one of them counted him. They did it the way you count heads at the door at the end of a long day, to be sure that everybody you care about has come in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-96-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-97-The manifest for the road had been written out the evening before the closing, and Seln had written it himself, as he had written the roster for the road down. Cael saw it on the third night, when Seln left it open on the board to check a cartage figure. Eleven names. Against his own, in his own small upright hand, Seln had put *records and floor scheduling*. It was the line the board in the covered walk had carried on the first morning of the qualifying year, to the letter. Nobody had asked him to change it. It was still true. It had been true on the first morning too, and complete in form, and it had meant almost nothing. Now it meant a seat on a bench, and not one letter of it had moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-98-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:99:*A line on a form says what a man is listed as,* he wrote in the old volume that night, very small, because the old volume had only a few leaves left. *It has no way to notice when he's become something else underneath it.*
