editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-99-"No," said Karis. "A rule that only holds when the fighter across from you is kind isn't a rule. It's luck." She took up the pen. "So we put a line in. Not a new rule. The old one, with one more word in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-100-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:101:She wrote it while they watched, slowly, in the hand she kept for papers that strangers would read long afterwards, and she wrote it twice, a draft and then a fair copy, with nothing crossed out on the second. Then she turned the fair copy round and pushed it across the table to him, and he copied it into Hesk's book under the minute from two nights before, in his best hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-102-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md-103-*The conditions may complete themselves anywhere; the deciding may not. The line holds: directed, open-eyed — and from tonight, costed. Never again in the middle of a fight unless what follows has been priced first, at the weight it will come at. Yesterday the bill fell due with the one person on the floor who could have made it worse choosing not to. We don't get to plan on that twice.*
