editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:55:"Three of them came up to the lodge on the First-day," she said. "Did you hear? They argued with the lodge-man for a quarter of an hour about the board, and one of them tried to give him a coin, and he pretended not to see it, because he said afterward it was a very small coin." She turned the apple round to find the last good side. "I've got eighteen days to the road meet. Rooke says I'm not to think about it. I think about it every time I put my foot down." Then, in exactly the same voice, she said, "*How often does the unclassified enrollee come up to watch the Silver drills?*"
49:He did not need it. He knew what he would find. But he went through it anyway, the way a man going out on a long road checks a buckle he knows is fastened. There they were: four acknowledgments in forty years, pasted in where they had come, each from a seat that had not sent for his finding and had received it anyway. A district's, from a dozen years back, thanking him without rancour for a finding that had cost the district its own petition. A hill station's, whose frame had come out of its housing on his evidence. A guild hall's. A sponsorship quarrel so old the guild's name had faded. Every one of them had answered inside a fortnight.
112:Then, since a morning has to be spent on something, he went on with the tray. Near the top of *in* lay a guild hall's request for more time to answer a finding on its own assessors, which he had already decided to refuse. He drew a sheet toward him and began, and his pen wrote, as it had written perhaps a hundred times in forty years, *The hall's request for further time is not contemplated by*
120:He struck the half-line through, corner to corner, so that it could still be read. On a clean sheet he wrote the refusal again: the section the hall had failed to meet, the officer it might apply to instead, the date by which it should. It took him a quarter of an hour. He signed it with his name entire and put it in *done*.
