editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:5:He took the rail at the north end, as he had on the second morning of the year. The high windows were behind him. At this hour the sun came through them low and flat, and laid long bars of light across the painted floor below, over the white crosses and the arrows, so that the whole hall looked ruled like a page in a ledger, with a line for every stride a fighter might take. Lira said she could read it. She said that on a bright morning she could see her own feet a quarter-beat before they landed, because the light told her where the shadow of her heel would fall. She had said it lightly, the way she said most things that mattered to her, and then she had gone out of her way never to train in the afternoon again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:37:In the lamplight Lira could not read her heel. She had said so. But Cael, watching her come at the lane on the four-count, saw her find something else. The dust in the cracks between the painted boards had woken as the lane went down, the way it always did, and was running south along the lane in a thin pale stream. In daylight from the high windows you could hardly see it. Under two lamps set low on the floor, it threw a shadow. The whole lane showed on the boards as a faint moving band of dark, like the wake of a fish under still water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:47:"The dust," she said. "Under the lamps the dust makes a shadow. I can see the lane by its shadow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:57:Lira had gone down on one knee by the end of the lane, and was looking along the boards at the last of the dust, with her face close to the floor and the lamp throwing her shadow long behind her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:77:He wrote it in Hesk's book at the sill before he slept. *The only honest measurement anybody has taken of me since Norhold was taken tonight, by Karis, on a closed floor, under two lamps. It will never be entered anywhere. Lira found the lane by its shadow. That will be entered nowhere either, and it's the best thing anybody learned on this hill this week.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:127:Everything else that morning he saw only between things. The side-door officer, his cold gone, not needing to ask the last name on his list. Somebody had already copied *Caelen Hesk-ward* into his door log from the last sitting, so that all he had to write was *the enrollee of record, unclassified*, which took him nine seconds now that his hand knew the way. *Seventeen* under Cael's left foot again, the third time, and this time chosen; he could not choose the room, but he could choose where he stood in it. And a slip of paper folded twice that Karis had handed Brom on the ferry, which was in Brom's right inside pocket now, with orders not to be opened until the coach went home. The left pocket was for Velmere, and it had a sealed letter in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:171:It was the names that had made the evaluators look up. Ilsev heard her own read out among them and wrote something small in her file without lifting her head. The counsel read twenty-two of them, one at a time, each with a grade and a house and a day, at the pace at which a person reads names in a room where names matter. The grant at Greyvane and its two renewals on paper, the season's officers under a regional registrar's seal, the tournament's, and three days of the Compact's own senior evaluator on the bluff. At the Archmarshal's name Cael thought the stillness under the hood altered, the way still water alters when something passes under it. It took four minutes. In the quiet he heard a cart's iron tyre grind over the cobbles in the street. More looking, the counsel said when she had finished, than most practitioners get in a working life; and all of it done by people who could see, sealed and minuted, across three years. She turned the rule. Tick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:255:The river went by. Lira kept one hand on the closed drill-book and never lifted its cover. Brom put the slip back in his right inside pocket and left his hand over it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:151:Cael saw it from the court on his way in, one low light behind the middle roof's windows, and went up the stair as far as the rail by the door. Brom was at the old striking frame at the north end, alone. He was not running the long sequence he ran before a road. He was hitting the padded post, over and over, forearm and turn and drive, at fighting pace, with the post's tired springs groaning, and he was not stopping between them the way he always stopped to reset. The cream envelope was in the coat he had hung on the frame's corner; Cael could see the square of it against the cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:51:"Mine," she said. "The keeper of the archive at Ternhall wrote to me this morning, privately. Four lines. A district clerk came to her counter six days ago with a sealed request for my transfer file: my old standing, my privileges, the training log from the paired floor." She kept two fingers on the letter, as if it might blow away. "On the Third-day a fee office wanted to know who I was. Now somebody wants to know who I used to be." She was quiet a moment. "That's twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:71:Brom had come up behind them, slower. At the turn of the stair he stopped, and Cael, a step below, saw his right hand go to the left side of his coat, where the inside pocket was, and rest there flat over the cloth. There was a square cream envelope in that pocket with its dark red seal still whole. It had come up with the post four days ago, and he had turned it over once at the table and put it away unopened, and nobody had asked. Now an office in his own province kept an open book with a question in it about his family, and his grandmother could turn its pages whenever she liked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:239:He signed it with the one letter Hesk signed his with, and sealed it, and in the morning he gave it to Bracken for the pouch, and Bracken took it without a word and put it in the pouch himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:29:There was one letter on the counter that had not gone into any column. It was sealed in grey.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:103:The second post came up while she was tying the piles. The porter brought one letter to the table himself, because he had learned the seal: a square cream envelope, a dark red seal pressed with the bar and the ring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:107:The last one had come on the thirty-first day, and Brom had kept it two nights on his table before he opened it. This one had come in fourteen. He looked at it. He picked it up and turned it over once in his big hands, and looked at the seal, whole, the bar and the ring. Then he put it, unopened, into the left inside pocket of his coat, where the summons was, and the docket, and the confirmation, and the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:159:Under Brom: *Velmere, a fortnight after the last. Into the pocket sealed. Hand on the pocket.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:151:Everything they had spent six weeks fighting about was gone. Whether the clause was law; whether the house's record was honest; whether anybody had done anything wrong. Jent had handed all of it across the floor in his first ten minutes, and it could never be taken back now, because it was in the minute in his own voice. What was left was one question with no paper in it at all: may the registry stand him in a frame? And everyone in the room knew already what the frame would say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:213:Cael made himself say it the coach way, plainly, so that he would have it straight. What they had won, they had for good: no seat would ever again argue whether the clause was law or whether the house's paper was honest; that was settled under seal, in the minute, forever. What they had not won was the only thing that could still reach him. The sittings that were left would argue a single question, whether to stand him in a frame. And nobody in the room, not Jent, not Ilsev, not the four evaluators, not the hood, could pretend not to know what the frame would say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:247:Brom said a runner had gone into him on the second step, a boy with a satchel, who said sorry over his shoulder without stopping and was round the corner by the corn chandler's before Brom had his balance back; there had been three more behind him. Lira said four towns by tonight. Cael thought of the printer in the wharf street on the old press that did not smear, needing two headlines and having room for one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:63:"The weeklies," said Brom. He took the pencil from behind Cael's ear and turned the last sheet over to its clean side, and worked it out the other way round from how anybody else would have, from the answer down to the reasons, the way he checked a load he had already lifted. "That's what it comes to. The bridge-end stall sells three quires a day of each now, by what the girl says, and a quire's twenty-four sheets. The back of the pamphlet says both weeklies sell in three other towns as well as Ostrand, and all three are bigger, so call it four quires each to be safe. Four towns. Two weeklies. A copper a sheet." He wrote the figures in his large slow hand, each line lashed to the one under it before he trusted it with the next, and drew a line, and the total under the line was what he had said before he began. "There."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:81:"Same thing, to the carter." She hefted the volume onto her own shoulder, which was not built for it, and stood with it there, frowning at the window. "When I was fourteen and walking from guild door to guild door, I'd have given a great deal for a copper. Just one. For bread." She went to the stair. "Somebody in four towns is paying a copper a week to read whether you're a person. I'd like to meet them. I'd like to ask what they'd do with the answer."

